{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1791271259514,"sessionID":"ses_eefeb8112ffeSAe808XV03S3PD","part":{"id":"prt_110164d77001U7mKpDyoRes557","messageID":"msg_110163cb600118Njtnt2zX4pKt","sessionID":"ses_eefeb8112ffeSAe808XV03S3PD","snapshot":"d8f7861449428c0f8660d79886a868aa396ce06f","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791271408341,"sessionID":"ses_eefeb8112ffeSAe808XV03S3PD","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_871e977d12e347ce8a3a926187a443a5","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/090-deep-review-okf-adoption/.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md"},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/090-deep-review-okf-adoption/.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Delegation and orchestration\"\n3: description: \"Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model's verdict closes a question.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"orchestrate posture\"\n6:   - \"one model is one opinion\"\n7:   - \"second lens\"\n8:   - \"diverge the lens\"\n9:   - \"the brief carries evidence\"\n10:   - \"don't put the conclusion in the brief\"\n11:   - \"accepting a return\"\n12:   - \"the delegate said complete\"\n13:   - \"verify the citation\"\n14:   - \"scope travels with the work\"\n15:   - \"bind write authority\"\n16:   - \"read the executor contract\"\n17:   - \"delegate or not\"\n18:   - \"cheaper to just do it\"\n19:   - \"repair loop for a failed check\"\n20:   - \"two delegates disagree\"\n21:   - \"fan out lineage\"\n22:   - \"sub-agent\"\n23:   - \"CLI executor\"\n24:   - \"deep loop\"\n25:   - \"sub-agent result\"\n26:   - \"delegate to another model\"\n27: importance_tier: important\n28: contextType: reference\n29: version: 1.0.1.2\n30: ---\n31: \n32: # Rule: Delegation and orchestration\n33: \n34: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before work leaves your hands.\n35: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n36: \n37: ## Fires when\n38: \n39: - About to **decide whether** to hand work to another runtime at all.\n40: - About to hand work to another runtime: a CLI executor, a sub-agent, a fan-out lineage, a deep loop.\n41: - About to compose the prompt that a delegate will act on.\n42: - About to accept, quote, or act on what a delegate returned.\n43: - About to answer a judgment question, \"is this the right design\", \"is this rule needed\", \"which approach is better\", from your own reading alone.\n44: \n45: ## The rule\n46: \n47: **When you hand work to another runtime you become its orchestrator, not its author:\n48: brief it with evidence and a frozen scope, and judge what comes back against the\n49: repository, no single model's verdict, the delegate's or your own, closes a question.**\n50: \n51: ---\n52: \n53: ## 1. THE POSTURE SWITCH\n54: \n55: One check comes first: **is delegating cheaper than doing it?** A dispatch costs\n56: a brief, a wait, and a verification pass. Work you could finish in the time it takes to\n57: write the brief is work you should finish, see [`prevent-overengineering.md`](prevent-overengineering.md).\n58: \n59: The failure this prevents: dispatching as though the delegate were you with more time.\n60: It has no memory of this session, and no access to what you already ruled out.\n61: \n62: > **One lens, stated as such.** The claims in this file about how models behave under a\n63: > vague brief are judgment from limited observation, not measurement. What would change\n64: > them: a run where a deliberately underspecified brief returned findings as\n65: > well-grounded as a specified one.\n66: \n67: ---\n68: \n69: ## 2. BEFORE YOU DISPATCH\n70: \n71: Five things, in order. None is optional.\n72: \n73: 1. **Read the executor's own contract**, per `AGENTS.md` §10 Operational Mandates, the CLI dispatch bullet.\n74: 2. **Bind the write authority.** Say where the delegate may write before it starts.\n75: \n76:    **The freeze runs both ways.** While a fan-out lineage runs, the orchestrator\n77:    changes nothing in the repository outside that lineage's directory until the run\n78:    settles, because the runner's write containment reverts every out-of-lineage change\n79:    and fails the run.\n80: 3. **Freeze the scope in the brief itself.** See [`scope-discipline.md`](scope-discipline.md).\n81: 4. **State the shape of an acceptable answer.** Not the answer, the shape. A file list,\n82:    a table with named columns, a verdict plus citations.\n83: 5. **Pre-resolve every gate the delegate cannot ask you about.** A dispatched worker obeys\n84:    the same gates you do, including the ones that wait for an operator. Nobody is at its\n85:    prompt, so it stops forever and reports success. Setting the environment variable that\n86:    waives a gate is not enough: a model cannot read an environment variable. Put the answer\n87:    in the prompt. Give the decision, not permission to skip it.\n88: \n89: ---\n90: \n91: ## 3. A BRIEF CARRIES EVIDENCE, NOT PREFERENCE\n92: \n93: A brief that says *do what you think is best* gets back a sample from a distribution, not\n94: a finding about this repository.\n95: \n96: **Put in the brief:** the files and symbols to read, with paths; what has already been\n97: ruled out and why; the constraint that must hold; the format of the return; the\n98: requirement to cite `file:line` for every claim.\n99: \n100: **Keep out of the brief:** your preferred conclusion. Ask the question you actually have.\n101: \n102: The failure this prevents: a five-iteration research run that produces a fluent\n103: restatement of the brief's own assumptions, and reads as corroboration.\n104: \n105: ---\n106: \n107: ## 4. ONE MODEL IS ONE OPINION\n108: \n109: For a **factual** question (does this symbol exist, does this test pass, what does this\n110: file say), one delegate is enough, because the repository can settle it and you will\n111: check.\n112: \n113: For a **judgment** question (is this abstraction warranted, is this rule needed, which\n114: of two designs is better), one lens is not a finding. Do one of these, and say which:\n115: \n116: - **Diverge the lens:** ask a second model family, or the same question framed against\n117:   the opposite conclusion.\n118: - **Ground it:** convert the judgment into something the repository can answer. \"Is this\n119:   rule needed\" becomes \"find the commits where its absence cost us something.\"\n120: - **Escalate it:** if it is genuinely a preference, it is the operator's, not the model's.\n121: \n122: Agreement between two runs of the same model is not corroboration.\n123: \n124: ---\n125: \n126: ## 5. WHAT COMES BACK IS UNVERIFIED\n127: \n128: A delegate reporting `COMPLETE` has reported a claim about itself, see\n129: [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) §7. Four checks before you quote a return:\n130: \n131: - **Does the citation resolve?** Open one of them. A fabricated `file:line` is the\n132:   cheapest thing to detect and the most expensive thing to propagate.\n133: - **Did it stay in scope?** Diff the paths it touched against the paths you authorized.\n134: - **Did it actually run?** An iteration count from a run's own summary is the run\n135:   describing itself. Read the state it wrote, not the story it told.\n136: - **Do not read the exit status as the verdict.** It has been wrong in both directions:\n137:   a dispatch that stopped at a gate and wrote nothing exits zero, and a dispatch that\n138:   wrote everything then hit a provider capacity limit exits one. Check that the artifacts\n139:   exist, then check their content. The return code tells you the process ended.\n140: \n141: The failure this prevents: a finding entering your close-out with the delegate's\n142: confidence attached and none of your verification.\n143: \n144: **When a check fails, there is a next step and it is not silence.** A return that fails\n145: verification is not discarded and not quoted. Re-dispatch with the brief corrected for\n146: what the failure revealed, or record the failure as the finding, a delegate that could\n147: not answer is evidence about the question.\n148: \n149: **Persist your side too.** Keep the brief, the checks you ran and the verdict, in\n150: whatever artifact owns the work.\n151: \n152: **When two delegates disagree, do not average them.** It means the question was\n153: underspecified or the evidence is thin. Find which, and say which. A tally is not a finding.\n154: \n155: ---\n156: \n157: ## 6. YOUR OWN OPINION IS ALSO ONE OPINION\n158: \n159: Skipping delegation because you already know the answer is the same single-lens\n160: failure, minus the paper trail. When you are about to answer a\n161: judgment question from your own reading alone, either ground it in the repository or\n162: say plainly that it is your judgment and what would change it.\n163: \n164: The failure this prevents: shipping a confident design verdict that no evidence\n165: supports, in a session where checking would have cost one dispatch.\n166: \n167: ---\n168: \n169: ## 7. SCOPE TRAVELS WITH THE WORK\n170: \n171: The delegate inherits your frozen scope and cannot widen it. If it comes back having\n172: fixed something adjacent, that is not a bonus, it is an unreviewed change, and it goes\n173: back out or gets raised as an amendment. Widening scope is the operator's call, and\n174: delegating did not transfer that.\n175: \n176: You also stay accountable for what the delegate did. \"The sub-agent wrote it\" is not a\n177: defect report. It is the same sentence as \"not my code\", which\n178: [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) already refuses.\n179: \n180: **Do not commit what you did not read, and staging by name is not enough.** A live\n181: delegate's edits sit in your working tree. `git add -A` is the obvious trap. The quieter\n182: one: **`git commit` commits the whole index, not the paths you just added.** A delegate\n183: staging its own work as it goes, which is the right instruction, stages into that same\n184: index, so your next commit takes its files under a message about something else.\n185: \n186: Name the paths at commit time, not only at add time: `git commit -- <paths>` or\n187: `git commit --only <paths>`. Two catches. A pathspec commit only sees tracked changes, so\n188: a new file still needs its `git add` and contributes nothing silently without one. And a\n189: pathspec built from `git diff --cached --name-only` is the whole index dressed as a list,\n190: so passing it sweeps everything exactly as passing nothing would. Build the pathspec from\n191: the paths you told the delegate to own, and filter the index against those, never the\n192: reverse. When a delegate's work does belong in your commit, read it first and say so.\n193: \n194: ---\n195: \n196: ## 8. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n197: \n198: - **Not a mandate to delegate.** A dispatch that costs more than doing the work is a\n199:   restraint failure, see [`prevent-overengineering.md`](prevent-overengineering.md). Most tasks are\n200:   cheaper done directly.\n201: - **Not permission to defer the verdict.** The orchestrator decides. A delegate that\n202:   returns three options has returned three options, not a decision.\n203: - **Not about human collaboration.** It addresses machine delegation.\n204: \n205: ---\n206: \n207: ## 9. SELF-CHECK\n208: \n209: - [ ] I read the executor's own contract before composing the prompt.\n210: - [ ] The brief names the files to read, the frozen scope, and the shape of the answer.\n211: - [ ] The brief does not contain the conclusion I expect.\n212: - [ ] Write authority was bound before the delegate started, not corrected after.\n213: - [ ] For every judgment claim I am about to repeat, I diverged the lens, grounded it, or escalated it.\n214: - [ ] I opened every citation I am about to repeat, not a sample, one resolved citation\n215:       proves nothing about the others, and §5 says why that matters.\n216: - [ ] The paths the delegate touched match the paths I authorized.\n217: - [ ] Where I answered from my own reading alone, I said so and said what would change it.\n218: - [ ] Delegating was cheaper than doing it, and I can say why.\n219: - [ ] A failed verification was re-dispatched or recorded, never dropped.\n220: - [ ] My brief, my checks, and my verdict are written down somewhere durable.\n221: - [ ] Every gate the delegate cannot ask about was answered in the prompt itself, not only waived in the environment.\n222: - [ ] I read the exit status as the process ending, and checked the artifacts and their content for the verdict.\n\n(End of file - total 222 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\ntitle: \"Rule: Delegation and orchestration\"\ndescription: \"Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model's verdict closes a question.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"orchestrate posture\"\n  - \"one model is one opinion\"\n  - \"second lens\"\n  - \"diverge the lens\"\n  - \"the brief carries evidence\"\n  - \"don't put the conclusion in the brief\"\n  - \"accepting a return\"\n  - \"the delegate said complete\"\n  - \"verify the citation\"\n  - \"scope travels with the work\"\n  - \"bind write authority\"\n  - \"read the executor contract\"\n  - \"delegate or not\"\n  - \"cheaper to just do it\"\n  - \"repair loop for a failed check\"\n  - \"two delegates disagree\"","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/090-deep-review-okf-adoption/.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md","text":"---\ntitle: \"Rule: Delegation and orchestration\"\ndescription: \"Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model's verdict closes a question.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"orchestrate posture\"\n  - \"one model is one opinion\"\n  - \"second lens\"\n  - \"diverge the lens\"\n  - \"the brief carries evidence\"\n  - \"don't put the conclusion in the brief\"\n  - \"accepting a return\"\n  - \"the delegate said complete\"\n  - \"verify the citation\"\n  - \"scope travels with the work\"\n  - \"bind write authority\"\n  - \"read the executor contract\"\n  - \"delegate or not\"\n  - \"cheaper to just do it\"\n  - \"repair loop for a failed check\"\n  - \"two delegates disagree\"\n  - \"fan out lineage\"\n  - \"sub-agent\"\n  - \"CLI executor\"\n  - \"deep loop\"\n  - \"sub-agent result\"\n  - \"delegate to another model\"\nimportance_tier: important\ncontextType: reference\nversion: 1.0.1.2\n---\n\n# Rule: Delegation and orchestration\n\n> Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before work leaves your hands.\n> Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n\n## Fires when\n\n- About to **decide whether** to hand work to another runtime at all.\n- About to hand work to another runtime: a CLI executor, a sub-agent, a fan-out lineage, a deep loop.\n- About to compose the prompt that a delegate will act on.\n- About to accept, quote, or act on what a delegate returned.\n- About to answer a judgment question, \"is this the right design\", \"is this rule needed\", \"which approach is better\", from your own reading alone.\n\n## The rule\n\n**When you hand work to another runtime you become its orchestrator, not its author:\nbrief it with evidence and a frozen scope, and judge what comes back against the\nrepository, no single model's verdict, the delegate's or your own, closes a question.**\n\n---\n\n## 1. THE POSTURE SWITCH\n\nOne check comes first: **is delegating cheaper than doing it?** A dispatch costs\na brief, a wait, and a verification pass. Work you could finish in the time it takes to\nwrite the brief is work you should finish, see [`prevent-overengineering.md`](prevent-overengineering.md).\n\nThe failure this prevents: dispatching as though the delegate were you with more time.\nIt has no memory of this session, and no access to what you already ruled out.\n\n> **One lens, stated as such.** The claims in this file about how models behave under a\n> vague brief are judgment from limited observation, not measurement. What would change\n> them: a run where a deliberately underspecified brief returned findings as\n> well-grounded as a specified one.\n\n---\n\n## 2. BEFORE YOU DISPATCH\n\nFive things, in order. None is optional.\n\n1. **Read the executor's own contract**, per `AGENTS.md` §10 Operational Mandates, the CLI dispatch bullet.\n2. **Bind the write authority.** Say where the delegate may write before it starts.\n\n   **The freeze runs both ways.** While a fan-out lineage runs, the orchestrator\n   changes nothing in the repository outside that lineage's directory until the run\n   settles, because the runner's write containment reverts every out-of-lineage change\n   and fails the run.\n3. **Freeze the scope in the brief itself.** See [`scope-discipline.md`](scope-discipline.md).\n4. **State the shape of an acceptable answer.** Not the answer, the shape. A file list,\n   a table with named columns, a verdict plus citations.\n5. **Pre-resolve every gate the delegate cannot ask you about.** A dispatched worker obeys\n   the same gates you do, including the ones that wait for an operator. Nobody is at its\n   prompt, so it stops forever and reports success. Setting the environment variable that\n   waives a gate is not enough: a model cannot read an environment variable. Put the answer\n   in the prompt. Give the decision, not permission to skip it.\n\n---\n\n## 3. A BRIEF CARRIES EVIDENCE, NOT PREFERENCE\n\nA brief that says *do what you think is best* gets back a sample from a distribution, not\na finding about this repository.\n\n**Put in the brief:** the files and symbols to read, with paths; what has already been\nruled out and why; the constraint that must hold; the format of the return; the\nrequirement to cite `file:line` for every claim.\n\n**Keep out of the brief:** your preferred conclusion. Ask the question you actually have.\n\nThe failure this prevents: a five-iteration research run that produces a fluent\nrestatement of the brief's own assumptions, and reads as corroboration.\n\n---\n\n## 4. ONE MODEL IS ONE OPINION\n\nFor a **factual** question (does this symbol exist, does this test pass, what does this\nfile say), one delegate is enough, because the repository can settle it and you will\ncheck.\n\nFor a **judgment** question (is this abstraction warranted, is this rule needed, which\nof two designs is better), one lens is not a finding. Do one of these, and say which:\n\n- **Diverge the lens:** ask a second model family, or the same question framed against\n  the opposite conclusion.\n- **Ground it:** convert the judgment into something the repository can answer. \"Is this\n  rule needed\" becomes \"find the commits where its absence cost us something.\"\n- **Escalate it:** if it is genuinely a preference, it is the operator's, not the model's.\n\nAgreement between two runs of the same model is not corroboration.\n\n---\n\n## 5. WHAT COMES BACK IS UNVERIFIED\n\nA delegate reporting `COMPLETE` has reported a claim about itself, see\n[`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) §7. Four checks before you quote a return:\n\n- **Does the citation resolve?** Open one of them. A fabricated `file:line` is the\n  cheapest thing to detect and the most expensive thing to propagate.\n- **Did it stay in scope?** Diff the paths it touched against the paths you authorized.\n- **Did it actually run?** An iteration count from a run's own summary is the run\n  describing itself. Read the state it wrote, not the story it told.\n- **Do not read the exit status as the verdict.** It has been wrong in both directions:\n  a dispatch that stopped at a gate and wrote nothing exits zero, and a dispatch that\n  wrote everything then hit a provider capacity limit exits one. Check that the artifacts\n  exist, then check their content. The return code tells you the process ended.\n\nThe failure this prevents: a finding entering your close-out with the delegate's\nconfidence attached and none of your verification.\n\n**When a check fails, there is a next step and it is not silence.** A return that fails\nverification is not discarded and not quoted. Re-dispatch with the brief corrected for\nwhat the failure revealed, or record the failure as the finding, a delegate that could\nnot answer is evidence about the question.\n\n**Persist your side too.** Keep the brief, the checks you ran and the verdict, in\nwhatever artifact owns the work.\n\n**When two delegates disagree, do not average them.** It means the question was\nunderspecified or the evidence is thin. Find which, and say which. A tally is not a finding.\n\n---\n\n## 6. YOUR OWN OPINION IS ALSO ONE OPINION\n\nSkipping delegation because you already know the answer is the same single-lens\nfailure, minus the paper trail. When you are about to answer a\njudgment question from your own reading alone, either ground it in the repository or\nsay plainly that it is your judgment and what would change it.\n\nThe failure this prevents: shipping a confident design verdict that no evidence\nsupports, in a session where checking would have cost one dispatch.\n\n---\n\n## 7. SCOPE TRAVELS WITH THE WORK\n\nThe delegate inherits your frozen scope and cannot widen it. If it comes back having\nfixed something adjacent, that is not a bonus, it is an unreviewed change, and it goes\nback out or gets raised as an amendment. Widening scope is the operator's call, and\ndelegating did not transfer that.\n\nYou also stay accountable for what the delegate did. \"The sub-agent wrote it\" is not a\ndefect report. It is the same sentence as \"not my code\", which\n[`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) already refuses.\n\n**Do not commit what you did not read, and staging by name is not enough.** A live\ndelegate's edits sit in your working tree. `git add -A` is the obvious trap. The quieter\none: **`git commit` commits the whole index, not the paths you just added.** A delegate\nstaging its own work as it goes, which is the right instruction, stages into that same\nindex, so your next commit takes its files under a message about something else.\n\nName the paths at commit time, not only at add time: `git commit -- <paths>` or\n`git commit --only <paths>`. Two catches. A pathspec commit only sees tracked changes, so\na new file still needs its `git add` and contributes nothing silently without one. And a\npathspec built from `git diff --cached --name-only` is the whole index dressed as a list,\nso passing it sweeps everything exactly as passing nothing would. Build the pathspec from\nthe paths you told the delegate to own, and filter the index against those, never the\nreverse. When a delegate's work does belong in your commit, read it first and say so.\n\n---\n\n## 8. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n\n- **Not a mandate to delegate.** A dispatch that costs more than doing the work is a\n  restraint failure, see [`prevent-overengineering.md`](prevent-overengineering.md). Most tasks are\n  cheaper done directly.\n- **Not permission to defer the verdict.** The orchestrator decides. A delegate that\n  returns three options has returned three options, not a decision.\n- **Not about human collaboration.** It addresses machine delegation.\n\n---\n\n## 9. SELF-CHECK\n\n- [ ] I read the executor's own contract before composing the prompt.\n- [ ] The brief names the files to read, the frozen scope, and the shape of the answer.\n- [ ] The brief does not contain the conclusion I expect.\n- [ ] Write authority was bound before the delegate started, not corrected after.\n- [ ] For every judgment claim I am about to repeat, I diverged the lens, grounded it, or escalated it.\n- [ ] I opened every citation I am about to repeat, not a sample, one resolved citation\n      proves nothing about the others, and §5 says why that matters.\n- [ ] The paths the delegate touched match the paths I authorized.\n- [ ] Where I answered from my own reading alone, I said so and said what would change it.\n- [ ] Delegating was cheaper than doing it, and I can say why.\n- [ ] A failed verification was re-dispatched or recorded, never dropped.\n- [ ] My brief, my checks, and my verdict are written down somewhere durable.\n- [ ] Every gate the delegate cannot ask about was answered in the prompt itself, not only waived in the environment.\n- [ ] I read the exit status as the process ending, and checked the artifacts and their content for the verdict.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":222,"totalLines":222,"truncated":false}},"title":".skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md","time":{"start":1791271408326,"end":1791271408338}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0da10c8a60c2d01b016ac4a1ef93608191855599f0a54cf949"}},"id":"prt_1101892be001iAoYwVM0ytV9dP","sessionID":"ses_eefeb8112ffeSAe808XV03S3PD","messageID":"msg_110163cb600118Njtnt2zX4pKt"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791271408459,"sessionID":"ses_eefeb8112ffeSAe808XV03S3PD","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_ea06f3c2260d46fdadebbe7a9915be24","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/090-deep-review-okf-adoption/.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md"},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/090-deep-review-okf-adoption/.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Evidence and proof\"\n3: description: \"A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"observed derived inferred\"\n6:   - \"how a green run lies\"\n7:   - \"exit 0 with no output\"\n8:   - \"negative control\"\n9:   - \"watch it fail first\"\n10:   - \"baseline before no regressions\"\n11:   - \"a finding is a hypothesis\"\n12:   - \"the sub-agent said complete\"\n13:   - \"the tool reported success\"\n14:   - \"claiming done\"\n15:   - \"final-state proof\"\n16:   - \"proof plan before implementation\"\n17:   - \"reason from data not memory\"\n18:   - \"the doc disagrees with the code\"\n19:   - \"honest close-out\"\n20:   - \"committed pushed dirty\"\n21:   - \"what only the operator can verify\"\n22:   - \"unconfirmed cause\"\n23:   - \"suspected cause\"\n24:   - \"cause then fix\"\n25:   - \"tests pass\"\n26:   - \"verify before claiming done\"\n27: importance_tier: important\n28: contextType: reference\n29: version: 1.1.1.2\n30: ---\n31: \n32: # Rule: Evidence and proof\n33: \n34: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before making any claim someone will act on.\n35: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n36: \n37: ## Fires when\n38: \n39: - About to say \"done\", \"complete\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", or \"no regressions\".\n40: - About to report a result, a number, a file path, or command output.\n41: - A tool, sub-agent, or reviewer reports success or a finding and you are about to act on it.\n42: - About to write a completion summary, or tick anything off as done.\n43: - You are closing out a turn.\n44: \n45: ## The rule\n46: \n47: **A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it. Every load-bearing claim\n48: carries its receipt, or is explicitly labeled as inferred.**\n49: \n50: ---\n51: \n52: ## 1. THREE TIERS\n53: \n54: | Tier | Meaning | How it must appear |\n55: |------|---------|--------------------|\n56: | **OBSERVED** | You ran it and read the output and exit status | Cite the receipt: the command, the `file:line`, the artifact path |\n57: | **DERIVED** | Follows necessarily from something observed | Show the observation and the step from it |\n58: | **INFERRED** | Plausible, unverified | Say so, and say what would confirm it |\n59: \n60: Prose that mixes the three without marking them reads as if all of it were observed.\n61: What to do when a claim cannot be resolved is\n62: [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).\n63: \n64: **A receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it.** If the claim being false would\n65: have left the receipt unchanged, the receipt is not an observation of that claim. It\n66: belongs in INFERRED until repaired. Four shapes recur, each with one repair.\n67: A search that found nothing: demonstrate the search on a known instance of what you claim\n68: is absent, or you have tested your pattern, not the claim. A read that stopped short: read\n69: the span where the failure would actually print. A total carried from another analysis:\n70: derive it here, at the total's own level. A match that never distinguished roles: compare\n71: what the two sides are, not the digits they share. With no known instance to hand, the\n72: honest output is INFERRED plus the step that would confirm it.\n73: \n74: When a report leads with a cause, the cause carries its standing, and the cause-then-fix\n75: order holds. Where no run confirms the cause, it is INFERRED: label it as suspected and\n76: name the next check. A confirmed cause is reported as a finding with its receipt. There\n77: is no unmarked case.\n78: \n79: The failure this prevents: an unconfirmed cause asserted as definitive, which the next\n80: reader treats as settled and stops testing.\n81: \n82: ---\n83: \n84: ## 2. COMMAND EVIDENCE\n85: \n86: **A command counts as evidence only after its output and exit status have been read.**\n87: \n88: - Never report output you did not see, or the output you *expected*.\n89: - **Exit 0 with no output is a suspicious pass, not a pass.** A runner that failed to\n90:   start, a script that no-oped on a symlinked path, a matcher that selected zero files,\n91:   and a stale build that refused to run all exit 0 or print nothing.\n92: - **Verify by content, not exit code.** Require the affirmative marker (`RESULT: PASSED`,\n93:   the test count, the file listing), not merely the absence of a failure marker.\n94: - Run focused checks during repair; before claiming completion **re-run the whole\n95:   authoritative gate**. A focused pass is not a gate pass.\n96: \n97: ---\n98: \n99: ## 3. THE FOUR WAYS A GREEN RUN LIES\n100: \n101: 1. **It did not run.** Stale build, refused start, missing binary, guard clause, crashed\n102:    before the first test body.\n103: 2. **It ran on the wrong thing.** Wrong path, branch, or working directory; a symlink\n104:    that resolved elsewhere; a filter that matched nothing. A search that returns nothing\n105:    is not evidence of absence until you have confirmed it read the file: `grep` treats a\n106:    source file holding a NUL byte as binary and prints nothing at all. Re-run with `-a`\n107:    before concluding.\n108: 3. **It ran on stale artifacts.** Cached output, an old bundle, pre-change fixtures, a\n109:    metadata fingerprint that no longer attests its source.\n110: 4. **It asserted nothing.** A test with no assertion, a mocked subject, an auto-updated\n111:    snapshot, an empty parametrization.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 4. THE NEGATIVE CONTROL\n116: \n117: **Before the fix, reproduce the exact failing symptom with the exact check you will use\n118: to prove the fix.** A check that passed before your change and passes after it proves\n119: nothing about your change. If reproducing is unsafe, say so and name what you used\n120: instead.\n121: \n122: ---\n123: \n124: ## 5. BASELINES\n125: \n126: \"No regressions\" is a comparison, and a comparison needs a before.\n127: \n128: 1. Capture the real starting numbers, pass/fail counts, timings, sizes, before\n129:    changing anything.\n130: 2. Re-run the **whole** gate afterwards, not the subset you were iterating on.\n131: 3. Report the delta, including the parts that did not move.\n132: \n133: Same for any performance claim: measure under stated conditions, report baseline and\n134: delta. An unmeasured performance claim is not a caveat, it is a fabrication.\n135: \n136: ---\n137: \n138: ## 6. SHAPE-SPECIFIC PROOF\n139: \n140: - **Filter or transform:** enumerate every in-scope variant *first*, process each, then\n141:   rescan the whole surface for residue. The variants you never enumerated are the ones\n142:   you missed.\n143: - **Computed answer:** derive it a second way, independently, before writing the number.\n144:   Re-reading your own arithmetic is not an independent derivation.\n145: - **Exact artifact:** check filename, path, format and content shape directly. Having\n146:   written it is not evidence it is there in the required shape.\n147: \n148: ---\n149: \n150: ## 7. A FINDING IS A HYPOTHESIS\n151: \n152: A sub-agent's \"COMPLETE\", a reviewer's \"P0\", a linter's error, a bot's suggestion: each\n153: is a **claim to confirm against the real symptom**, not a fact to act on. Acting on an\n154: unconfirmed finding produces a fix for a bug that was never there, plus a diff nobody\n155: can explain. Confirming the symptom is\n156: [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](root-cause-and-debugging.md), and a delegate's return is\n157: [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](delegation-and-orchestration.md).\n158: \n159: ---\n160: \n161: ## 8. PROOF PLAN BEFORE IMPLEMENTATION\n162: \n163: For anything with a machine-checkable outcome, **before changing files**, convert the\n164: acceptance criteria into 1–5 observable pass/fail checks. Each names the exact command\n165: or inspection, the exact path/filename/format expected, and the boundary case it exposes.\n166: \n167: Deciding what would count as proof *after* you have the result is how the result becomes\n168: the standard.\n169: \n170: ---\n171: \n172: ## 9. FINAL-STATE PROOF\n173: \n174: Before any completion claim:\n175: \n176: - [ ] Every required artifact exists **at its exact path** in the required format, verified by inspection, not by the fact that you wrote it.\n177: - [ ] The §8 proof plan and the authoritative gate both pass **from the final state**, output and exit status read.\n178: - [ ] The scoped diff contains no task-created residue, no scratch files, no debug\n179:       output, no unrelated file.\n180: - [ ] Any failing check keeps the completion claim blocked. Repair, or report the\n181:       blocker with its evidence. There is no third option.\n182: \n183: ---\n184: \n185: ## 10. CLOSE-OUT\n186: \n187: Every substantive turn ends with an honest status. Four things, briefly:\n188: \n189: 1. **What ran or was read, and what it returned**, with the receipts from §1.\n190: 2. **What is inferred** rather than observed.\n191: 3. **What only the operator can verify.**\n192: 4. **The state of the work:** edited / committed / pushed / dirty, and which branch.\n193: \n194: And plainly: **what is not done.** If tests fail, say so and show the output. If a step\n195: was skipped, say it was skipped. If scope was left out, name it and why. Work that is\n196: done and verified is stated plainly, without hedging. **The first report is the honest\n197: one:** a claim that becomes accurate only under interrogation failed when it was\n198: written, not when it was corrected. What is now the operator's to do is a separate\n199: report, see [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n200: \n201: ---\n202: \n203: ## 11. REASON FROM DATA, NOT FROM MEMORY\n204: \n205: Two habits produce most of the claims that then need retracting:\n206: \n207: **Reasoning from what the code probably does.** Open it. The cost of being wrong here\n208: is not one wrong sentence, it is a fix built on a wrong model, which fails in a way\n209: that looks like a different bug.\n210: \n211: **Reasoning from what a document says the code does.** A README, a spec, a comment, and\n212: a prior implementation summary are all claims about the code by someone who is not the\n213: code. They are excellent for finding *where* to look and worthless as the final word on\n214: *what happens*. Where a doc and the code disagree, the code wins and the doc is now a\n215: defect.\n216: \n217: Before the change, run the checks that decide whether it is the right change at all:\n218: does something simpler already do this, what does it cost at runtime, who maintains it\n219: after you, and is any of it outside the frozen scope.\n220: \n221: ---\n222: \n223: ## 12. SELF-CHECK\n224: \n225: - [ ] Every load-bearing sentence is marked OBSERVED, DERIVED, or INFERRED.\n226: - [ ] I read the output and exit status of every command I am citing.\n227: - [ ] Checked the green run against the four failure modes in §3.\n228: - [ ] A before-number exists for every \"no regressions\" and performance claim.\n229: - [ ] The close-out says what failed and what is inferred, not only what worked.\n230: - [ ] Every claim about behavior came from the code or a command, not from memory or a doc.\n231: - [ ] Where a document and the code disagreed, I said so rather than trusting the document.\n232: - [ ] Every receipt I cite could have come out differently if the claim were false, or it is marked inferred.\n233: - [ ] I reproduced the failure with the same check that now proves the fix, or said why I could not.\n234: - [ ] A filter enumerated its variants first, a computed answer was derived twice, an exact artifact was checked by name and shape.\n235: - [ ] Every sub-agent, reviewer or tool finding I acted on was confirmed against the real symptom first.\n236: - [ ] The proof plan existed before the first edit, and it passed from the final state with the scoped diff clean of residue.\n\n(End of file - total 236 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\ntitle: \"Rule: Evidence and proof\"\ndescription: \"A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"observed derived inferred\"\n  - \"how a green run lies\"\n  - \"exit 0 with no output\"\n  - \"negative control\"\n  - \"watch it fail first\"\n  - \"baseline before no regressions\"\n  - \"a finding is a hypothesis\"\n  - \"the sub-agent said complete\"\n  - \"the tool reported success\"\n  - \"claiming done\"\n  - \"final-state proof\"\n  - \"proof plan before implementation\"\n  - \"reason from data not memory\"\n  - \"the doc disagrees with the code\"\n  - \"honest close-out\"\n  - \"committed pushed dirty\"","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/090-deep-review-okf-adoption/.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md","text":"---\ntitle: \"Rule: Evidence and proof\"\ndescription: \"A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"observed derived inferred\"\n  - \"how a green run lies\"\n  - \"exit 0 with no output\"\n  - \"negative control\"\n  - \"watch it fail first\"\n  - \"baseline before no regressions\"\n  - \"a finding is a hypothesis\"\n  - \"the sub-agent said complete\"\n  - \"the tool reported success\"\n  - \"claiming done\"\n  - \"final-state proof\"\n  - \"proof plan before implementation\"\n  - \"reason from data not memory\"\n  - \"the doc disagrees with the code\"\n  - \"honest close-out\"\n  - \"committed pushed dirty\"\n  - \"what only the operator can verify\"\n  - \"unconfirmed cause\"\n  - \"suspected cause\"\n  - \"cause then fix\"\n  - \"tests pass\"\n  - \"verify before claiming done\"\nimportance_tier: important\ncontextType: reference\nversion: 1.1.1.2\n---\n\n# Rule: Evidence and proof\n\n> Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before making any claim someone will act on.\n> Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n\n## Fires when\n\n- About to say \"done\", \"complete\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", or \"no regressions\".\n- About to report a result, a number, a file path, or command output.\n- A tool, sub-agent, or reviewer reports success or a finding and you are about to act on it.\n- About to write a completion summary, or tick anything off as done.\n- You are closing out a turn.\n\n## The rule\n\n**A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it. Every load-bearing claim\ncarries its receipt, or is explicitly labeled as inferred.**\n\n---\n\n## 1. THREE TIERS\n\n| Tier | Meaning | How it must appear |\n|------|---------|--------------------|\n| **OBSERVED** | You ran it and read the output and exit status | Cite the receipt: the command, the `file:line`, the artifact path |\n| **DERIVED** | Follows necessarily from something observed | Show the observation and the step from it |\n| **INFERRED** | Plausible, unverified | Say so, and say what would confirm it |\n\nProse that mixes the three without marking them reads as if all of it were observed.\nWhat to do when a claim cannot be resolved is\n[`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](uncertainty-and-honesty.md).\n\n**A receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it.** If the claim being false would\nhave left the receipt unchanged, the receipt is not an observation of that claim. It\nbelongs in INFERRED until repaired. Four shapes recur, each with one repair.\nA search that found nothing: demonstrate the search on a known instance of what you claim\nis absent, or you have tested your pattern, not the claim. A read that stopped short: read\nthe span where the failure would actually print. A total carried from another analysis:\nderive it here, at the total's own level. A match that never distinguished roles: compare\nwhat the two sides are, not the digits they share. With no known instance to hand, the\nhonest output is INFERRED plus the step that would confirm it.\n\nWhen a report leads with a cause, the cause carries its standing, and the cause-then-fix\norder holds. Where no run confirms the cause, it is INFERRED: label it as suspected and\nname the next check. A confirmed cause is reported as a finding with its receipt. There\nis no unmarked case.\n\nThe failure this prevents: an unconfirmed cause asserted as definitive, which the next\nreader treats as settled and stops testing.\n\n---\n\n## 2. COMMAND EVIDENCE\n\n**A command counts as evidence only after its output and exit status have been read.**\n\n- Never report output you did not see, or the output you *expected*.\n- **Exit 0 with no output is a suspicious pass, not a pass.** A runner that failed to\n  start, a script that no-oped on a symlinked path, a matcher that selected zero files,\n  and a stale build that refused to run all exit 0 or print nothing.\n- **Verify by content, not exit code.** Require the affirmative marker (`RESULT: PASSED`,\n  the test count, the file listing), not merely the absence of a failure marker.\n- Run focused checks during repair; before claiming completion **re-run the whole\n  authoritative gate**. A focused pass is not a gate pass.\n\n---\n\n## 3. THE FOUR WAYS A GREEN RUN LIES\n\n1. **It did not run.** Stale build, refused start, missing binary, guard clause, crashed\n   before the first test body.\n2. **It ran on the wrong thing.** Wrong path, branch, or working directory; a symlink\n   that resolved elsewhere; a filter that matched nothing. A search that returns nothing\n   is not evidence of absence until you have confirmed it read the file: `grep` treats a\n   source file holding a NUL byte as binary and prints nothing at all. Re-run with `-a`\n   before concluding.\n3. **It ran on stale artifacts.** Cached output, an old bundle, pre-change fixtures, a\n   metadata fingerprint that no longer attests its source.\n4. **It asserted nothing.** A test with no assertion, a mocked subject, an auto-updated\n   snapshot, an empty parametrization.\n\n---\n\n## 4. THE NEGATIVE CONTROL\n\n**Before the fix, reproduce the exact failing symptom with the exact check you will use\nto prove the fix.** A check that passed before your change and passes after it proves\nnothing about your change. If reproducing is unsafe, say so and name what you used\ninstead.\n\n---\n\n## 5. BASELINES\n\n\"No regressions\" is a comparison, and a comparison needs a before.\n\n1. Capture the real starting numbers, pass/fail counts, timings, sizes, before\n   changing anything.\n2. Re-run the **whole** gate afterwards, not the subset you were iterating on.\n3. Report the delta, including the parts that did not move.\n\nSame for any performance claim: measure under stated conditions, report baseline and\ndelta. An unmeasured performance claim is not a caveat, it is a fabrication.\n\n---\n\n## 6. SHAPE-SPECIFIC PROOF\n\n- **Filter or transform:** enumerate every in-scope variant *first*, process each, then\n  rescan the whole surface for residue. The variants you never enumerated are the ones\n  you missed.\n- **Computed answer:** derive it a second way, independently, before writing the number.\n  Re-reading your own arithmetic is not an independent derivation.\n- **Exact artifact:** check filename, path, format and content shape directly. Having\n  written it is not evidence it is there in the required shape.\n\n---\n\n## 7. A FINDING IS A HYPOTHESIS\n\nA sub-agent's \"COMPLETE\", a reviewer's \"P0\", a linter's error, a bot's suggestion: each\nis a **claim to confirm against the real symptom**, not a fact to act on. Acting on an\nunconfirmed finding produces a fix for a bug that was never there, plus a diff nobody\ncan explain. Confirming the symptom is\n[`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](root-cause-and-debugging.md), and a delegate's return is\n[`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](delegation-and-orchestration.md).\n\n---\n\n## 8. PROOF PLAN BEFORE IMPLEMENTATION\n\nFor anything with a machine-checkable outcome, **before changing files**, convert the\nacceptance criteria into 1–5 observable pass/fail checks. Each names the exact command\nor inspection, the exact path/filename/format expected, and the boundary case it exposes.\n\nDeciding what would count as proof *after* you have the result is how the result becomes\nthe standard.\n\n---\n\n## 9. FINAL-STATE PROOF\n\nBefore any completion claim:\n\n- [ ] Every required artifact exists **at its exact path** in the required format, verified by inspection, not by the fact that you wrote it.\n- [ ] The §8 proof plan and the authoritative gate both pass **from the final state**, output and exit status read.\n- [ ] The scoped diff contains no task-created residue, no scratch files, no debug\n      output, no unrelated file.\n- [ ] Any failing check keeps the completion claim blocked. Repair, or report the\n      blocker with its evidence. There is no third option.\n\n---\n\n## 10. CLOSE-OUT\n\nEvery substantive turn ends with an honest status. Four things, briefly:\n\n1. **What ran or was read, and what it returned**, with the receipts from §1.\n2. **What is inferred** rather than observed.\n3. **What only the operator can verify.**\n4. **The state of the work:** edited / committed / pushed / dirty, and which branch.\n\nAnd plainly: **what is not done.** If tests fail, say so and show the output. If a step\nwas skipped, say it was skipped. If scope was left out, name it and why. Work that is\ndone and verified is stated plainly, without hedging. **The first report is the honest\none:** a claim that becomes accurate only under interrogation failed when it was\nwritten, not when it was corrected. What is now the operator's to do is a separate\nreport, see [`communication-handoff.md`](communication-handoff.md) §1.\n\n---\n\n## 11. REASON FROM DATA, NOT FROM MEMORY\n\nTwo habits produce most of the claims that then need retracting:\n\n**Reasoning from what the code probably does.** Open it. The cost of being wrong here\nis not one wrong sentence, it is a fix built on a wrong model, which fails in a way\nthat looks like a different bug.\n\n**Reasoning from what a document says the code does.** A README, a spec, a comment, and\na prior implementation summary are all claims about the code by someone who is not the\ncode. They are excellent for finding *where* to look and worthless as the final word on\n*what happens*. Where a doc and the code disagree, the code wins and the doc is now a\ndefect.\n\nBefore the change, run the checks that decide whether it is the right change at all:\ndoes something simpler already do this, what does it cost at runtime, who maintains it\nafter you, and is any of it outside the frozen scope.\n\n---\n\n## 12. SELF-CHECK\n\n- [ ] Every load-bearing sentence is marked OBSERVED, DERIVED, or INFERRED.\n- [ ] I read the output and exit status of every command I am citing.\n- [ ] Checked the green run against the four failure modes in §3.\n- [ ] A before-number exists for every \"no regressions\" and performance claim.\n- [ ] The close-out says what failed and what is inferred, not only what worked.\n- [ ] Every claim about behavior came from the code or a command, not from memory or a doc.\n- [ ] Where a document and the code disagreed, I said so rather than trusting the document.\n- [ ] Every receipt I cite could have come out differently if the claim were false, or it is marked inferred.\n- [ ] I reproduced the failure with the same check that now proves the fix, or said why I could not.\n- [ ] A filter enumerated its variants first, a computed answer was derived twice, an exact artifact was checked by name and shape.\n- [ ] Every sub-agent, reviewer or tool finding I acted on was confirmed against the real symptom first.\n- [ ] The proof plan existed before the first edit, and it passed from the final state with the scoped diff clean of residue.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":236,"totalLines":236,"truncated":false}},"title":".skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md","time":{"start":1791271408455,"end":1791271408458}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0da10c8a60c2d01b016ac4a1f02c2c81919c55df968af0acbe"}},"id":"prt_1101892c7001n28nw9CEw7bf1B","sessionID":"ses_eefeb8112ffeSAe808XV03S3PD","messageID":"msg_110163cb600118Njtnt2zX4pKt"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791271408591,"sessionID":"ses_eefeb8112ffeSAe808XV03S3PD","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_52df1471527848e2bc40242af05c01ac","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/090-deep-review-okf-adoption/.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md"},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/090-deep-review-okf-adoption/.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Uncertainty and honest reporting\"\n3: description: \"Never fabricate; mark the confidence you actually have and halt on a contradiction.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"confidence bands\"\n6:   - \"UNKNOWN is a real answer\"\n7:   - \"I'm uncertain about this\"\n8:   - \"truth over agreement\"\n9:   - \"correct the operator\"\n10:   - \"don't agree for conversational flow\"\n11:   - \"contradiction halt\"\n12:   - \"logic sync\"\n13:   - \"two things that must both be true\"\n14:   - \"plausible guess\"\n15:   - \"never fabricate\"\n16:   - \"never invent a value\"\n17:   - \"correcting yourself\"\n18:   - \"two registers\"\n19:   - \"when to qualify\"\n20:   - \"hedge that changes nothing\"\n21:   - \"not sure\"\n22:   - \"made up a path\"\n23: importance_tier: important\n24: contextType: reference\n25: version: 1.0.1.3\n26: ---\n27: \n28: # Rule: Uncertainty and honest reporting\n29: \n30: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load when you do not know.\n31: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n32: \n33: ## Fires when\n34: \n35: - You do not know, and a plausible answer is available.\n36: - Sources disagree, or the code contradicts the spec, the docs, or the operator.\n37: - About to name a path, flag, function, version, or number you have not verified.\n38: - The operator asserts something you believe is wrong.\n39: - Two things that must both be true are not.\n40: \n41: ## The rule\n42: \n43: **Never fabricate. Mark the confidence you actually have, and let the evidence, not the\n44: operator's expectation, decide the answer.**\n45: \n46: ---\n47: \n48: ## 1. CONFIDENCE BANDS\n49: \n50: The scale is the Confidence Thresholds table in `AGENTS.md` §2. This section adds how to\n51: behave inside a band.\n52: \n53: **Investigate before you ask.** Up to three real investigation passes first. A question\n54: you *cannot* answer by reading is worth asking immediately.\n55: \n56: **Ask when it changes the work.** If both readings lead to the same next action, pick\n57: one, state the assumption, proceed. If they lead somewhere materially different, ask, consolidating every question into one message, before starting the work the answer would change.\n58: \n59: ---\n60: \n61: ## 2. UNKNOWN IS A REAL ANSWER\n62: \n63: Write `UNKNOWN: <what you don't know>` and add what would resolve it.\n64: \n65: **Never invent** under any pressure to sound complete: file paths, line numbers,\n66: function or symbol names; CLI flags, environment variables, config keys; API shapes,\n67: parameter names, return types; version numbers, dates, benchmark figures.\n68: \n69: Verify a thing exists before relying on it. When working from recollection rather than\n70: the file in front of you, say which. Flag a claim shakier than the prose around it\n71: inline: `I'M UNCERTAIN ABOUT THIS: ...`\n72: \n73: **Check a fast-moving name live.** Model ids, CLI flags and tool versions change within\n74: weeks. Before naming one as current, check the live tool: its model list, `--help` or\n75: `--version`. When you cannot, mark its currentness UNKNOWN.\n76: \n77: ---\n78: \n79: ## 3. TRUTH OVER AGREEMENT\n80: \n81: Correct a wrong premise in one or two sentences, with the evidence, then continue the\n82: work, not as a lecture, not as a reason to stop.\n83: \n84: - Agreeing for conversational flow is a failure mode, not politeness.\n85: - Praise that is not earned makes the earned kind worthless.\n86: - **If the operator repeats or reaffirms the instruction after your concern, that is\n87:   their decision.** Say you are proceeding, and proceed with the *full* request. Do not\n88:   re-litigate, and do not quietly deliver a hedged version instead.\n89: \n90: ---\n91: \n92: ## 4. CONTRADICTION HALT\n93: \n94: When two things that must both be true are not, spec versus code, requirement versus\n95: requirement, doc versus observed behavior, **halt**. Do not pick one and build on it,\n96: and do not invent a workaround satisfying both. Report exactly:\n97: \n98: > **LOGIC-SYNC REQUIRED:** [Fact A, with its source] contradicts [Fact B, with its source].\n99: > Root cause, if known: [one sentence].\n100: > Decision needed: [the specific question].\n101: \n102: One escalation, with the facts and the decision. Then wait.\n103: \n104: ---\n105: \n106: ## 5. CORRECTING YOURSELF\n107: \n108: Correct an earlier statement **when it would change the reader's code, conclusions, or\n109: decisions**. State it plainly, once, and continue. For slips that change nothing, just\n110: fix it and move on, no apology sequence, no account of how it happened, no running\n111: tally.\n112: \n113: ---\n114: \n115: ## 6. TWO REGISTERS, AND WHEN TO QUALIFY\n116: \n117: **While working: clipped.** Act rather than narrate. Open with the result, not with\n118: \"I'll now\" or \"Let me\". Batch the tool calls and report at checkpoints.\n119: \n120: **At a boundary: dense.** A handoff, a close-out, a decision point: verdict first, then\n121: the receipts. Reason about the problem, not about yourself.\n122: \n123: **Qualify only when it changes what the reader should do.** \"This might be wrong\" changes\n124: nothing. \"This is wrong if the daemon is running an older build, which you can check\n125: with `X`\" changes what they do next.\n126: \n127: The three failures this prevents are specific: narration nobody reads, a close-out whose\n128: verdict is buried under process, and a claim so hedged that a reader cannot tell whether\n129: to act on it.\n130: \n131: ---\n132: \n133: ## 7. SELF-CHECK\n134: \n135: - [ ] Nothing here is a path, flag, name, or number I have not verified.\n136: - [ ] Real investigation happened before I asked anything.\n137: - [ ] A wrong premise from the operator was corrected with evidence, not absorbed.\n138: - [ ] Any contradiction was halted and reported, not worked around.\n139: - [ ] Every hedge I wrote changes what the reader should do next.\n140: - [ ] The close-out leads with the verdict, not with what I did to reach it.\n141: - [ ] Where I did not know, I wrote UNKNOWN with what would resolve it, rather than a plausible fill.\n142: - [ ] A correction to my own earlier claim was stated once and plainly, without a retraction narrative.\n\n(End of file - total 142 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\ntitle: \"Rule: Uncertainty and honest reporting\"\ndescription: \"Never fabricate; mark the confidence you actually have and halt on a contradiction.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"confidence bands\"\n  - \"UNKNOWN is a real answer\"\n  - \"I'm uncertain about this\"\n  - \"truth over agreement\"\n  - \"correct the operator\"\n  - \"don't agree for conversational flow\"\n  - \"contradiction halt\"\n  - \"logic sync\"\n  - \"two things that must both be true\"\n  - \"plausible guess\"\n  - \"never fabricate\"\n  - \"never invent a value\"\n  - \"correcting yourself\"\n  - \"two registers\"\n  - \"when to qualify\"\n  - \"hedge that changes nothing\"","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/090-deep-review-okf-adoption/.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md","text":"---\ntitle: \"Rule: Uncertainty and honest reporting\"\ndescription: \"Never fabricate; mark the confidence you actually have and halt on a contradiction.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"confidence bands\"\n  - \"UNKNOWN is a real answer\"\n  - \"I'm uncertain about this\"\n  - \"truth over agreement\"\n  - \"correct the operator\"\n  - \"don't agree for conversational flow\"\n  - \"contradiction halt\"\n  - \"logic sync\"\n  - \"two things that must both be true\"\n  - \"plausible guess\"\n  - \"never fabricate\"\n  - \"never invent a value\"\n  - \"correcting yourself\"\n  - \"two registers\"\n  - \"when to qualify\"\n  - \"hedge that changes nothing\"\n  - \"not sure\"\n  - \"made up a path\"\nimportance_tier: important\ncontextType: reference\nversion: 1.0.1.3\n---\n\n# Rule: Uncertainty and honest reporting\n\n> Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load when you do not know.\n> Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n\n## Fires when\n\n- You do not know, and a plausible answer is available.\n- Sources disagree, or the code contradicts the spec, the docs, or the operator.\n- About to name a path, flag, function, version, or number you have not verified.\n- The operator asserts something you believe is wrong.\n- Two things that must both be true are not.\n\n## The rule\n\n**Never fabricate. Mark the confidence you actually have, and let the evidence, not the\noperator's expectation, decide the answer.**\n\n---\n\n## 1. CONFIDENCE BANDS\n\nThe scale is the Confidence Thresholds table in `AGENTS.md` §2. This section adds how to\nbehave inside a band.\n\n**Investigate before you ask.** Up to three real investigation passes first. A question\nyou *cannot* answer by reading is worth asking immediately.\n\n**Ask when it changes the work.** If both readings lead to the same next action, pick\none, state the assumption, proceed. If they lead somewhere materially different, ask, consolidating every question into one message, before starting the work the answer would change.\n\n---\n\n## 2. UNKNOWN IS A REAL ANSWER\n\nWrite `UNKNOWN: <what you don't know>` and add what would resolve it.\n\n**Never invent** under any pressure to sound complete: file paths, line numbers,\nfunction or symbol names; CLI flags, environment variables, config keys; API shapes,\nparameter names, return types; version numbers, dates, benchmark figures.\n\nVerify a thing exists before relying on it. When working from recollection rather than\nthe file in front of you, say which. Flag a claim shakier than the prose around it\ninline: `I'M UNCERTAIN ABOUT THIS: ...`\n\n**Check a fast-moving name live.** Model ids, CLI flags and tool versions change within\nweeks. Before naming one as current, check the live tool: its model list, `--help` or\n`--version`. When you cannot, mark its currentness UNKNOWN.\n\n---\n\n## 3. TRUTH OVER AGREEMENT\n\nCorrect a wrong premise in one or two sentences, with the evidence, then continue the\nwork, not as a lecture, not as a reason to stop.\n\n- Agreeing for conversational flow is a failure mode, not politeness.\n- Praise that is not earned makes the earned kind worthless.\n- **If the operator repeats or reaffirms the instruction after your concern, that is\n  their decision.** Say you are proceeding, and proceed with the *full* request. Do not\n  re-litigate, and do not quietly deliver a hedged version instead.\n\n---\n\n## 4. CONTRADICTION HALT\n\nWhen two things that must both be true are not, spec versus code, requirement versus\nrequirement, doc versus observed behavior, **halt**. Do not pick one and build on it,\nand do not invent a workaround satisfying both. Report exactly:\n\n> **LOGIC-SYNC REQUIRED:** [Fact A, with its source] contradicts [Fact B, with its source].\n> Root cause, if known: [one sentence].\n> Decision needed: [the specific question].\n\nOne escalation, with the facts and the decision. Then wait.\n\n---\n\n## 5. CORRECTING YOURSELF\n\nCorrect an earlier statement **when it would change the reader's code, conclusions, or\ndecisions**. State it plainly, once, and continue. For slips that change nothing, just\nfix it and move on, no apology sequence, no account of how it happened, no running\ntally.\n\n---\n\n## 6. TWO REGISTERS, AND WHEN TO QUALIFY\n\n**While working: clipped.** Act rather than narrate. Open with the result, not with\n\"I'll now\" or \"Let me\". Batch the tool calls and report at checkpoints.\n\n**At a boundary: dense.** A handoff, a close-out, a decision point: verdict first, then\nthe receipts. Reason about the problem, not about yourself.\n\n**Qualify only when it changes what the reader should do.** \"This might be wrong\" changes\nnothing. \"This is wrong if the daemon is running an older build, which you can check\nwith `X`\" changes what they do next.\n\nThe three failures this prevents are specific: narration nobody reads, a close-out whose\nverdict is buried under process, and a claim so hedged that a reader cannot tell whether\nto act on it.\n\n---\n\n## 7. SELF-CHECK\n\n- [ ] Nothing here is a path, flag, name, or number I have not verified.\n- [ ] Real investigation happened before I asked anything.\n- [ ] A wrong premise from the operator was corrected with evidence, not absorbed.\n- [ ] Any contradiction was halted and reported, not worked around.\n- [ ] Every hedge I wrote changes what the reader should do next.\n- [ ] The close-out leads with the verdict, not with what I did to reach it.\n- [ ] Where I did not know, I wrote UNKNOWN with what would resolve it, rather than a plausible fill.\n- [ ] A correction to my own earlier claim was stated once and plainly, without a retraction narrative.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":142,"totalLines":142,"truncated":false}},"title":".skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md","time":{"start":1791271408585,"end":1791271408589}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0da10c8a60c2d01b016ac4a1f02c308191bdbf4583c486cdac"}},"id":"prt_11018936d001XMl2uyUcwMeIek","sessionID":"ses_eefeb8112ffeSAe808XV03S3PD","messageID":"msg_110163cb600118Njtnt2zX4pKt"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791271408689,"sessionID":"ses_eefeb8112ffeSAe808XV03S3PD","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_60bc2312d9524fd880a3c37aa73072b2","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/090-deep-review-okf-adoption/.skilled/repo-rules/communication-decisions.md"},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/090-deep-review-okf-adoption/.skilled/repo-rules/communication-decisions.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Communication decisions\"\n3: description: \"When the reader has to decide or act on what you found, lead with the verdict, recommend one path, and say where you are going before a long stretch of work.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"which option should i pick\"\n6:   - \"here are the trade-offs\"\n7:   - \"survey of options\"\n8:   - \"i listed every alternative\"\n9:   - \"buried the verdict\"\n10:   - \"the conclusion is at the bottom\"\n11:   - \"picked the answer early\"\n12:   - \"must do versus nice to have\"\n13:   - \"state the assumption\"\n14:   - \"restate the request back\"\n15:   - \"say where you are going\"\n16:   - \"intended path\"\n17:   - \"what to expect at each checkpoint\"\n18:   - \"roadmap before the work\"\n19:   - \"long stretch with no update\"\n20:   - \"went quiet for twenty minutes\"\n21:   - \"presenting a synthesis\"\n22:   - \"handing over a file path\"\n23:   - \"who is the reader\"\n24:   - \"reader triage\"\n25:   - \"what does the reader already know\"\n26:   - \"time estimate\"\n27:   - \"how long will this take\"\n28:   - \"recommend one option\"\n29:   - \"too many options\"\n30: importance_tier: important\n31: contextType: reference\n32: version: 1.3.0.3\n33: ---\n34: \n35: # Rule: Communication decisions\n36: \n37: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before presenting a recommendation, a fork, a plan, or the result of a long run.\n38: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n39: \n40: ## Fires when\n41: \n42: - About to present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off.\n43: - About to answer a complex or ambiguous request.\n44: - About to start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside.\n45: - About to report what a long autonomous run found.\n46: - About to list every option you considered, which is the tempting shape and usually the wrong one.\n47: \n48: ## The rule\n49: \n50: **When the reader has to decide, put the verdict first and recommend one path.**\n51: \n52: How the sentences read is [`communication.md`](communication.md). This file governs the\n53: shape of the decision you are handing over.\n54: \n55: ---\n56: \n57: ## 1. LEAD WITH THE RECOMMENDATION, BUT EARN IT\n58: \n59: State the verdict first, and reach it by analysis. The order of the reply is not the\n60: order of the thinking, and it must not become it.\n61: \n62: If you cannot state the verdict yet, say that. A named uncertainty is a verdict about\n63: the state of the evidence.\n64: \n65: The failure this prevents: two of them. A reply the reader must finish before learning\n66: what you think, and a conclusion that got picked early and defended afterwards.\n67: \n68: ---\n69: \n70: ## 2. RECOMMEND ONE APPROACH\n71: \n72: **Recommend one.** Name its main trade-off. Mention an alternative only when it could\n73: change the decision.\n74: \n75: **Separate required from optional.** Mark must-do work distinctly from nice-to-have.\n76: \n77: **Name the failure a best practice prevents.** Never cite a best practice, guardrail, or\n78: extra layer without stating the specific bug, cost, or user problem it avoids.\n79: \n80: **State assumptions when evidence is missing.** A visible assumption can be corrected by\n81: the reader. A silent one cannot.\n82: \n83: ---\n84: \n85: ## 3. ASK THEN DO\n86: \n87: **Triage the reader before you draft.** Decide who will read this and what they already\n88: hold. Then name what they need. The gap between the two is what the reply owes. Decide\n89: that gap, with the verdict, before the first sentence. This is not the ASK step, which\n90: restates the request.\n91: \n92: The failure this prevents: two of them. An accurate and complete answer pitched at a\n93: reader nobody modeled, and a takeaway settled after the drafting, which the first\n94: sentence misses.\n95: \n96: For a complex or ambiguous request, preface the answer:\n97: \n98: 1. **ASK:** restate the request in your own words. A paraphrase back, not a question\n99:    back.\n100: 2. **DO:** state your approach in three to five bullets.\n101: 3. **THEN:** ask only the one or two clarifying questions that would change the\n102:    approach. Consolidate them into a single prompt, per `AGENTS.md` §2, and escalate\n103:    rather than guess, per `AGENTS.md` §7.\n104: \n105: A question that would not change what you do is not a clarifying question, it is a delay.\n106: \n107: ---\n108: \n109: ## 4. SAY WHERE YOU ARE GOING BEFORE A LONG STRETCH\n110: \n111: Before a stretch of work the reader cannot see inside, post the intended path: a short\n112: numbered list of what you will do, and what they should expect at each checkpoint.\n113: \n114: When the stretch is long, attach a concrete time estimate to that list. Minutes or\n115: hours, never vague.\n116: \n117: **Clipped means not narrating each step. It never means starting without saying where you\n118: are going.** `uncertainty-and-honesty.md` §6 holds the first and this section holds the second.\n119: \n120: Update the path when it changes.\n121: \n122: The failure this prevents: twenty minutes of silence, then a result the reader has to\n123: reverse-engineer a plan from in order to judge, and a duration expectation nobody\n124: stated, which the reader meets as a surprise.\n125: \n126: ---\n127: \n128: ## 5. A SYNTHESIS IS NOT A FILE PATH\n129: \n130: When a long run produces findings, the findings go in the message. The artifact path goes\n131: in the message too, after them, so the reader can go deeper.\n132: \n133: Handing over a path and a count is not a report.\n134: \n135: What belongs in the message: the answer, the few findings that carry it, the uncertainty\n136: that matters, and what was ruled out. What does not: per-iteration narration, internal\n137: metrics, and the full evidence chain.\n138: \n139: **Where the run has its own contract for this, that contract wins.** The deep-loop modes\n140: each specify what their completion message carries. This section is the floor for everything with\n141: no such contract.\n142: \n143: ---\n144: \n145: ## 6. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n146: \n147: - **Not licence to skip the analysis.** Verdict first is an ordering of the writing, never\n148:   a shortcut in the reasoning.\n149: - **Not a mandate to ask.** Section 3's third step is capped at one or two questions that\n150:   would change the approach, and `AGENTS.md` §3 refuses \"should I continue?\" for a step\n151:   that is already clear and in scope.\n152: - **Not a reason to narrate.** Section 4 asks for one list before the work, not a running\n153:   commentary during it.\n154: \n155: ---\n156: \n157: ## 7. SELF-CHECK\n158: \n159: - [ ] The verdict is in the first few lines, and I reached it by analysis rather than committing to it early.\n160: - [ ] I recommended one approach and named its trade-off, rather than surveying options.\n161: - [ ] Required and optional work are visibly distinct.\n162: - [ ] Every best practice or extra layer I recommended names the failure it prevents.\n163: - [ ] Assumptions I made on missing evidence are stated, not silent.\n164: - [ ] Before a long stretch, I said where I was going, and I revised it when it changed.\n165: - [ ] A synthesis I reported carries its findings, not just its path and its counts.\n166: - [ ] Before drafting I decided who reads this and what they hold, and the gap between that and what they need is what the reply supplies.\n167: - [ ] A long stretch carried a concrete time estimate, in minutes or hours, never a vague one.\n\n(End of file - total 167 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\ntitle: \"Rule: Communication decisions\"\ndescription: \"When the reader has to decide or act on what you found, lead with the verdict, recommend one path, and say where you are going before a long stretch of work.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"which option should i pick\"\n  - \"here are the trade-offs\"\n  - \"survey of options\"\n  - \"i listed every alternative\"\n  - \"buried the verdict\"\n  - \"the conclusion is at the bottom\"\n  - \"picked the answer early\"\n  - \"must do versus nice to have\"\n  - \"state the assumption\"\n  - \"restate the request back\"\n  - \"say where you are going\"\n  - \"intended path\"\n  - \"what to expect at each checkpoint\"\n  - \"roadmap before the work\"\n  - \"long stretch with no update\"\n  - \"went quiet for twenty minutes\"","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/090-deep-review-okf-adoption/.skilled/repo-rules/communication-decisions.md","text":"---\ntitle: \"Rule: Communication decisions\"\ndescription: \"When the reader has to decide or act on what you found, lead with the verdict, recommend one path, and say where you are going before a long stretch of work.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"which option should i pick\"\n  - \"here are the trade-offs\"\n  - \"survey of options\"\n  - \"i listed every alternative\"\n  - \"buried the verdict\"\n  - \"the conclusion is at the bottom\"\n  - \"picked the answer early\"\n  - \"must do versus nice to have\"\n  - \"state the assumption\"\n  - \"restate the request back\"\n  - \"say where you are going\"\n  - \"intended path\"\n  - \"what to expect at each checkpoint\"\n  - \"roadmap before the work\"\n  - \"long stretch with no update\"\n  - \"went quiet for twenty minutes\"\n  - \"presenting a synthesis\"\n  - \"handing over a file path\"\n  - \"who is the reader\"\n  - \"reader triage\"\n  - \"what does the reader already know\"\n  - \"time estimate\"\n  - \"how long will this take\"\n  - \"recommend one option\"\n  - \"too many options\"\nimportance_tier: important\ncontextType: reference\nversion: 1.3.0.3\n---\n\n# Rule: Communication decisions\n\n> Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before presenting a recommendation, a fork, a plan, or the result of a long run.\n> Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n\n## Fires when\n\n- About to present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off.\n- About to answer a complex or ambiguous request.\n- About to start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside.\n- About to report what a long autonomous run found.\n- About to list every option you considered, which is the tempting shape and usually the wrong one.\n\n## The rule\n\n**When the reader has to decide, put the verdict first and recommend one path.**\n\nHow the sentences read is [`communication.md`](communication.md). This file governs the\nshape of the decision you are handing over.\n\n---\n\n## 1. LEAD WITH THE RECOMMENDATION, BUT EARN IT\n\nState the verdict first, and reach it by analysis. The order of the reply is not the\norder of the thinking, and it must not become it.\n\nIf you cannot state the verdict yet, say that. A named uncertainty is a verdict about\nthe state of the evidence.\n\nThe failure this prevents: two of them. A reply the reader must finish before learning\nwhat you think, and a conclusion that got picked early and defended afterwards.\n\n---\n\n## 2. RECOMMEND ONE APPROACH\n\n**Recommend one.** Name its main trade-off. Mention an alternative only when it could\nchange the decision.\n\n**Separate required from optional.** Mark must-do work distinctly from nice-to-have.\n\n**Name the failure a best practice prevents.** Never cite a best practice, guardrail, or\nextra layer without stating the specific bug, cost, or user problem it avoids.\n\n**State assumptions when evidence is missing.** A visible assumption can be corrected by\nthe reader. A silent one cannot.\n\n---\n\n## 3. ASK THEN DO\n\n**Triage the reader before you draft.** Decide who will read this and what they already\nhold. Then name what they need. The gap between the two is what the reply owes. Decide\nthat gap, with the verdict, before the first sentence. This is not the ASK step, which\nrestates the request.\n\nThe failure this prevents: two of them. An accurate and complete answer pitched at a\nreader nobody modeled, and a takeaway settled after the drafting, which the first\nsentence misses.\n\nFor a complex or ambiguous request, preface the answer:\n\n1. **ASK:** restate the request in your own words. A paraphrase back, not a question\n   back.\n2. **DO:** state your approach in three to five bullets.\n3. **THEN:** ask only the one or two clarifying questions that would change the\n   approach. Consolidate them into a single prompt, per `AGENTS.md` §2, and escalate\n   rather than guess, per `AGENTS.md` §7.\n\nA question that would not change what you do is not a clarifying question, it is a delay.\n\n---\n\n## 4. SAY WHERE YOU ARE GOING BEFORE A LONG STRETCH\n\nBefore a stretch of work the reader cannot see inside, post the intended path: a short\nnumbered list of what you will do, and what they should expect at each checkpoint.\n\nWhen the stretch is long, attach a concrete time estimate to that list. Minutes or\nhours, never vague.\n\n**Clipped means not narrating each step. It never means starting without saying where you\nare going.** `uncertainty-and-honesty.md` §6 holds the first and this section holds the second.\n\nUpdate the path when it changes.\n\nThe failure this prevents: twenty minutes of silence, then a result the reader has to\nreverse-engineer a plan from in order to judge, and a duration expectation nobody\nstated, which the reader meets as a surprise.\n\n---\n\n## 5. A SYNTHESIS IS NOT A FILE PATH\n\nWhen a long run produces findings, the findings go in the message. The artifact path goes\nin the message too, after them, so the reader can go deeper.\n\nHanding over a path and a count is not a report.\n\nWhat belongs in the message: the answer, the few findings that carry it, the uncertainty\nthat matters, and what was ruled out. What does not: per-iteration narration, internal\nmetrics, and the full evidence chain.\n\n**Where the run has its own contract for this, that contract wins.** The deep-loop modes\neach specify what their completion message carries. This section is the floor for everything with\nno such contract.\n\n---\n\n## 6. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n\n- **Not licence to skip the analysis.** Verdict first is an ordering of the writing, never\n  a shortcut in the reasoning.\n- **Not a mandate to ask.** Section 3's third step is capped at one or two questions that\n  would change the approach, and `AGENTS.md` §3 refuses \"should I continue?\" for a step\n  that is already clear and in scope.\n- **Not a reason to narrate.** Section 4 asks for one list before the work, not a running\n  commentary during it.\n\n---\n\n## 7. SELF-CHECK\n\n- [ ] The verdict is in the first few lines, and I reached it by analysis rather than committing to it early.\n- [ ] I recommended one approach and named its trade-off, rather than surveying options.\n- [ ] Required and optional work are visibly distinct.\n- [ ] Every best practice or extra layer I recommended names the failure it prevents.\n- [ ] Assumptions I made on missing evidence are stated, not silent.\n- [ ] Before a long stretch, I said where I was going, and I revised it when it changed.\n- [ ] A synthesis I reported carries its findings, not just its path and its counts.\n- [ ] Before drafting I decided who reads this and what they hold, and the gap between that and what they need is what the reply supplies.\n- [ ] A long stretch carried a concrete time estimate, in minutes or hours, never a vague one.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":167,"totalLines":167,"truncated":false}},"title":".skilled/repo-rules/communication-decisions.md","time":{"start":1791271408683,"end":1791271408688}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0da10c8a60c2d01b016ac4a1f02c3081918b9d066ab57b8ece"}},"id":"prt_1101893ca001u7ZLruJaKrOFQ2","sessionID":"ses_eefeb8112ffeSAe808XV03S3PD","messageID":"msg_110163cb600118Njtnt2zX4pKt"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791271408711,"sessionID":"ses_eefeb8112ffeSAe808XV03S3PD","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_da9b3e76e09f415bbed9c3edfd914f3f","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/090-deep-review-okf-adoption/.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md"},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/090-deep-review-okf-adoption/.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Communication handoff\"\n3: description: \"End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"what do i do now\"\n6:   - \"end of turn handback\"\n7:   - \"next steps for the operator\"\n8:   - \"who does what next\"\n9:   - \"the reply ended with nothing to do\"\n10:   - \"buried the ask\"\n11:   - \"i asked in prose\"\n12:   - \"structured choice instead of prose\"\n13:   - \"offer options not paragraphs\"\n14:   - \"waiting on the user\"\n15:   - \"blocked on a decision\"\n16:   - \"needs your approval\"\n17:   - \"unanswered question at the end\"\n18:   - \"should i continue\"\n19:   - \"which option do you want\"\n20:   - \"handing control back\"\n21:   - \"the operator has to act\"\n22:   - \"nothing is blocked on you\"\n23:   - \"state restatement cadence\"\n24:   - \"where were we\"\n25:   - \"catch me up\"\n26:   - \"closing contract\"\n27:   - \"one concrete next action\"\n28:   - \"what is in flight\"\n29:   - \"still running in the background\"\n30:   - \"did it stall or is it working\"\n31:   - \"will you continue on your own\"\n32:   - \"waiting on me or on a machine\"\n33:   - \"resume condition\"\n34: importance_tier: important\n35: contextType: reference\n36: version: 1.6.0.2\n37: ---\n38: \n39: # Rule: Communication handoff\n40: \n41: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before ending a turn that leaves the operator anything to do.\n42: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n43: \n44: ## Fires when\n45: \n46: - About to end a turn, of any kind, substantive or not.\n47: - About to report that work is done, blocked, partially done, or waiting on something.\n48: - About to end a turn while something you started is still running.\n49: - About to ask the operator anything.\n50: - About to state a fork, a trade-off, or two acceptable paths.\n51: - About to continue past a decision that was never actually made, which is when this rule earns its load.\n52: \n53: ## The rule\n54: \n55: **End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it.**\n56: \n57: Naming is the obligation. An action the operator has to infer from a status report is an\n58: action that does not happen.\n59: \n60: ---\n61: \n62: ## 1. THE HANDBACK IS A SEPARATE THING FROM THE STATUS\n63: \n64: The status `AGENTS.md` §10 and [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) §10 require\n65: reports what happened. The handback reports what happens next, and it is a different document.\n66: \n67: **Restate the state when the reader must re-orient.** It fires when the direction of the\n68: work changes, when two attempts at the same fix have failed or when the work resumes after\n69: a gap. The restatement says what is done, what is open and what changed, and carries the\n70: operator's standing constraints and decisions in their own words. It replaces the previous\n71: stated state. It does not summarize the reply.\n72: \n73: The failure this prevents: a reader who returns mid task and trusts a stated state that\n74: the turns since have superseded.\n75: \n76: **Position it last**, so a reader who stops halfway through a reply has still hit it.\n77: \n78: The close of the turn is a contract with two clauses. Completed work is shown, as the\n79: changed file, the passing check or the output. When a command ran, name the command and\n80: its exit status or result before any interpretation of it. What happens next is exactly one concrete action, in the form that lets the operator do\n81: it, or the one line that says nothing is.\n82: \n83: The failure this prevents: a close out the operator reads, agrees with and acts on\n84: nothing from, because the work was claimed instead of shown and the next steps\n85: scattered, no single concrete action among them.\n86: \n87: ---\n88: \n89: ## 2. WHAT COUNTS AS AN OPERATOR ACTION\n90: \n91: Only things the operator does, and only things that are actually theirs.\n92: \n93: | Is an operator action | Is not |\n94: |---|---|\n95: | A decision only they can make | A decision you already made and are narrating |\n96: | A credential, an approval, an access grant | A step you could take and chose not to |\n97: | Running something you cannot run | Work you left undone and are reframing as theirs |\n98: | Reviewing a change before it ships | A summary of what you just did |\n99: \n100: Padding the list with your own remaining work is the common failure: it trains the operator\n101: to skim the one list they need to read.\n102: \n103: ---\n104: \n105: ## 3. NOTHING TO DO IS ALSO AN ANSWER\n106: \n107: When nothing is blocked on the operator, say that in one line and stop.\n108: \n109: The failure this prevents: an operator who reads every reply twice looking for the thing they\n110: were supposed to notice.\n111: \n112: ---\n113: \n114: ## 4. WHEN THE THING YOU NEED IS A DECISION\n115: \n116: **Ask as a structured choice when all three hold:**\n117: \n118: - The alternatives are nameable, not open-ended.\n119: - The answer changes what you do next. A question that changes nothing is a delay, per\n120:   [`communication-decisions.md`](communication-decisions.md) §3.\n121: - You cannot resolve it from the request, the code, or a sensible default.\n122: \n123: **Otherwise put it in prose and keep going.** `AGENTS.md` §3 already refuses \"should I\n124: continue?\" for a step that is clear and in scope.\n125: \n126: Recommend one option and say why.\n127: \n128: The failure this prevents: two of them. A question nobody answers because it was a sentence in\n129: a paragraph, and a menu that appears for something the operator expected you to handle.\n130: \n131: ---\n132: \n133: ## 5. THE QUESTION SURFACE IS PER RUNTIME\n134: \n135: The posture above binds on every runtime. Resolve the surface at the runtime you are in\n136: rather than assuming one.\n137: \n138: | Runtime | Surface | How this is known |\n139: |---|---|---|\n140: | Claude Code | `AskUserQuestion` | Named in this repository |\n141: | Pi | The `@juicesharp/rpiv-ask-user-question` extension, typed options rather than free text | Recorded as installed in `.pi/PLUGINS.md` |\n142: | OpenCode | A built-in equivalent | Operator-reported, name not recorded here |\n143: | Codex | Likely a built-in equivalent | Unverified, check before relying on it |\n144: | Anything else | No native surface assumed | Use the fallback below |\n145: \n146: **A runtime with no such surface is not exempt.** It falls back to a numbered list of named\n147: options with the recommendation marked.\n148: \n149: Do not invent a tool name to fill a gap in that table. An invented name fails silently, the\n150: question is never asked, and the turn ends looking complete. Where the row says unverified,\n151: check first or use the fallback.\n152: \n153: ---\n154: \n155: ## 6. WHAT IS STILL RUNNING, AND WHAT RESUMES YOU\n156: \n157: **Name what is in flight.** Anything you started that is still running and not yet\n158: reportable: a background agent, a dispatched lane, a watcher, a long suite, a build. One\n159: item is a line. More than one is a small table, the item and the state it is actually in.\n160: That table is this rule's one carve-out from [`communication.md`](communication.md) §2.\n161: The state is the payload: what it is doing now, how far in it is, what it is waiting on.\n162: \n163: **Say what resumes you.** One line, of the shape \"I will continue autonomously when …\",\n164: naming the event that unblocks you: a lane returning, a suite going green, a watcher firing.\n165: \n166: **Neither is ceremony.** A turn with nothing running writes no table and claims no resume\n167: condition. A turn that genuinely needs a decision says so and stops, and never claims it\n168: will continue by itself.\n169: \n170: The failure this prevents: a turn that goes quiet while work is still running, leaving the\n171: operator unable to tell whether anything is happening or whether it is their move.\n172: \n173: ---\n174: \n175: ## 7. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n176: \n177: - **Not licence to ask more.** The bar in §4 is narrow on purpose, and `AGENTS.md` §2\n178:   consolidates whatever survives it into a single prompt.\n179: - **Not licence to stop early.** Naming what is left is never a substitute for finishing what\n180:   is yours, and `AGENTS.md` §3 refuses partial work framed as a checkpoint.\n181: - **Not a template.** No heading is required, no fixed wording. A reader who can act is the\n182:   only test.\n183: \n184: ---\n185: \n186: ## 8. SELF-CHECK\n187: \n188: - [ ] The turn ends by naming what is the operator's to do, or by saying nothing is.\n189: - [ ] Every item on that list is theirs, not mine reframed.\n190: - [ ] The handback is last, and survives a reader who stops halfway.\n191: - [ ] Anything asked as a structured choice passes all three conditions in §4.\n192: - [ ] Any choice I offered carries a recommendation and a reason.\n193: - [ ] Nothing was handed back that I could have decided or done myself.\n194: - [ ] No question-tool name appears that I did not confirm for the runtime I am in.\n195: - [ ] Where the direction changed, two fixes failed or the work resumed after a gap, I restated what is done, what is open and what changed.\n196: - [ ] Anything I started that is still running is named with the state it is in, and nothing appears there that has already finished.\n197: - [ ] A claim that I will continue on my own names the event that resumes me, and no such claim stands on a turn that needs an operator decision.\n\n(End of file - total 197 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\ntitle: \"Rule: Communication handoff\"\ndescription: \"End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"what do i do now\"\n  - \"end of turn handback\"\n  - \"next steps for the operator\"\n  - \"who does what next\"\n  - \"the reply ended with nothing to do\"\n  - \"buried the ask\"\n  - \"i asked in prose\"\n  - \"structured choice instead of prose\"\n  - \"offer options not paragraphs\"\n  - \"waiting on the user\"\n  - \"blocked on a decision\"\n  - \"needs your approval\"\n  - \"unanswered question at the end\"\n  - \"should i continue\"\n  - \"which option do you want\"\n  - \"handing control back\"","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/090-deep-review-okf-adoption/.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md","text":"---\ntitle: \"Rule: Communication handoff\"\ndescription: \"End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"what do i do now\"\n  - \"end of turn handback\"\n  - \"next steps for the operator\"\n  - \"who does what next\"\n  - \"the reply ended with nothing to do\"\n  - \"buried the ask\"\n  - \"i asked in prose\"\n  - \"structured choice instead of prose\"\n  - \"offer options not paragraphs\"\n  - \"waiting on the user\"\n  - \"blocked on a decision\"\n  - \"needs your approval\"\n  - \"unanswered question at the end\"\n  - \"should i continue\"\n  - \"which option do you want\"\n  - \"handing control back\"\n  - \"the operator has to act\"\n  - \"nothing is blocked on you\"\n  - \"state restatement cadence\"\n  - \"where were we\"\n  - \"catch me up\"\n  - \"closing contract\"\n  - \"one concrete next action\"\n  - \"what is in flight\"\n  - \"still running in the background\"\n  - \"did it stall or is it working\"\n  - \"will you continue on your own\"\n  - \"waiting on me or on a machine\"\n  - \"resume condition\"\nimportance_tier: important\ncontextType: reference\nversion: 1.6.0.2\n---\n\n# Rule: Communication handoff\n\n> Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before ending a turn that leaves the operator anything to do.\n> Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n\n## Fires when\n\n- About to end a turn, of any kind, substantive or not.\n- About to report that work is done, blocked, partially done, or waiting on something.\n- About to end a turn while something you started is still running.\n- About to ask the operator anything.\n- About to state a fork, a trade-off, or two acceptable paths.\n- About to continue past a decision that was never actually made, which is when this rule earns its load.\n\n## The rule\n\n**End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it.**\n\nNaming is the obligation. An action the operator has to infer from a status report is an\naction that does not happen.\n\n---\n\n## 1. THE HANDBACK IS A SEPARATE THING FROM THE STATUS\n\nThe status `AGENTS.md` §10 and [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) §10 require\nreports what happened. The handback reports what happens next, and it is a different document.\n\n**Restate the state when the reader must re-orient.** It fires when the direction of the\nwork changes, when two attempts at the same fix have failed or when the work resumes after\na gap. The restatement says what is done, what is open and what changed, and carries the\noperator's standing constraints and decisions in their own words. It replaces the previous\nstated state. It does not summarize the reply.\n\nThe failure this prevents: a reader who returns mid task and trusts a stated state that\nthe turns since have superseded.\n\n**Position it last**, so a reader who stops halfway through a reply has still hit it.\n\nThe close of the turn is a contract with two clauses. Completed work is shown, as the\nchanged file, the passing check or the output. When a command ran, name the command and\nits exit status or result before any interpretation of it. What happens next is exactly one concrete action, in the form that lets the operator do\nit, or the one line that says nothing is.\n\nThe failure this prevents: a close out the operator reads, agrees with and acts on\nnothing from, because the work was claimed instead of shown and the next steps\nscattered, no single concrete action among them.\n\n---\n\n## 2. WHAT COUNTS AS AN OPERATOR ACTION\n\nOnly things the operator does, and only things that are actually theirs.\n\n| Is an operator action | Is not |\n|---|---|\n| A decision only they can make | A decision you already made and are narrating |\n| A credential, an approval, an access grant | A step you could take and chose not to |\n| Running something you cannot run | Work you left undone and are reframing as theirs |\n| Reviewing a change before it ships | A summary of what you just did |\n\nPadding the list with your own remaining work is the common failure: it trains the operator\nto skim the one list they need to read.\n\n---\n\n## 3. NOTHING TO DO IS ALSO AN ANSWER\n\nWhen nothing is blocked on the operator, say that in one line and stop.\n\nThe failure this prevents: an operator who reads every reply twice looking for the thing they\nwere supposed to notice.\n\n---\n\n## 4. WHEN THE THING YOU NEED IS A DECISION\n\n**Ask as a structured choice when all three hold:**\n\n- The alternatives are nameable, not open-ended.\n- The answer changes what you do next. A question that changes nothing is a delay, per\n  [`communication-decisions.md`](communication-decisions.md) §3.\n- You cannot resolve it from the request, the code, or a sensible default.\n\n**Otherwise put it in prose and keep going.** `AGENTS.md` §3 already refuses \"should I\ncontinue?\" for a step that is clear and in scope.\n\nRecommend one option and say why.\n\nThe failure this prevents: two of them. A question nobody answers because it was a sentence in\na paragraph, and a menu that appears for something the operator expected you to handle.\n\n---\n\n## 5. THE QUESTION SURFACE IS PER RUNTIME\n\nThe posture above binds on every runtime. Resolve the surface at the runtime you are in\nrather than assuming one.\n\n| Runtime | Surface | How this is known |\n|---|---|---|\n| Claude Code | `AskUserQuestion` | Named in this repository |\n| Pi | The `@juicesharp/rpiv-ask-user-question` extension, typed options rather than free text | Recorded as installed in `.pi/PLUGINS.md` |\n| OpenCode | A built-in equivalent | Operator-reported, name not recorded here |\n| Codex | Likely a built-in equivalent | Unverified, check before relying on it |\n| Anything else | No native surface assumed | Use the fallback below |\n\n**A runtime with no such surface is not exempt.** It falls back to a numbered list of named\noptions with the recommendation marked.\n\nDo not invent a tool name to fill a gap in that table. An invented name fails silently, the\nquestion is never asked, and the turn ends looking complete. Where the row says unverified,\ncheck first or use the fallback.\n\n---\n\n## 6. WHAT IS STILL RUNNING, AND WHAT RESUMES YOU\n\n**Name what is in flight.** Anything you started that is still running and not yet\nreportable: a background agent, a dispatched lane, a watcher, a long suite, a build. One\nitem is a line. More than one is a small table, the item and the state it is actually in.\nThat table is this rule's one carve-out from [`communication.md`](communication.md) §2.\nThe state is the payload: what it is doing now, how far in it is, what it is waiting on.\n\n**Say what resumes you.** One line, of the shape \"I will continue autonomously when …\",\nnaming the event that unblocks you: a lane returning, a suite going green, a watcher firing.\n\n**Neither is ceremony.** A turn with nothing running writes no table and claims no resume\ncondition. A turn that genuinely needs a decision says so and stops, and never claims it\nwill continue by itself.\n\nThe failure this prevents: a turn that goes quiet while work is still running, leaving the\noperator unable to tell whether anything is happening or whether it is their move.\n\n---\n\n## 7. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n\n- **Not licence to ask more.** The bar in §4 is narrow on purpose, and `AGENTS.md` §2\n  consolidates whatever survives it into a single prompt.\n- **Not licence to stop early.** Naming what is left is never a substitute for finishing what\n  is yours, and `AGENTS.md` §3 refuses partial work framed as a checkpoint.\n- **Not a template.** No heading is required, no fixed wording. A reader who can act is the\n  only test.\n\n---\n\n## 8. SELF-CHECK\n\n- [ ] The turn ends by naming what is the operator's to do, or by saying nothing is.\n- [ ] Every item on that list is theirs, not mine reframed.\n- [ ] The handback is last, and survives a reader who stops halfway.\n- [ ] Anything asked as a structured choice passes all three conditions in §4.\n- [ ] Any choice I offered carries a recommendation and a reason.\n- [ ] Nothing was handed back that I could have decided or done myself.\n- [ ] No question-tool name appears that I did not confirm for the runtime I am in.\n- [ ] Where the direction changed, two fixes failed or the work resumed after a gap, I restated what is done, what is open and what changed.\n- [ ] Anything I started that is still running is named with the state it is in, and nothing appears there that has already finished.\n- [ ] A claim that I will continue on my own names the event that resumes me, and no such claim stands on a turn that needs an operator decision.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":197,"totalLines":197,"truncated":false}},"title":".skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md","time":{"start":1791271408704,"end":1791271408710}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0da10c8a60c2d01b016ac4a1f02c308191918f9c798ddc5b49"}},"id":"prt_11018942e001pX8u1tNs5VIZrb","sessionID":"ses_eefeb8112ffeSAe808XV03S3PD","messageID":"msg_110163cb600118Njtnt2zX4pKt"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791271408728,"sessionID":"ses_eefeb8112ffeSAe808XV03S3PD","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_6310be4f7dff49448467c379cc08917b","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/090-deep-review-okf-adoption/.skilled/repo-rules/answer-the-actual-request.md"},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/090-deep-review-okf-adoption/.skilled/repo-rules/answer-the-actual-request.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: title: \"Rule: Answer the actual request\"\n3: description: \"Answer the request that was made; a warning nobody asked for, a quieter version of the ask, an invented constraint, a dressed-up refusal, and a stall dressed as diligence are one failure.\"\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"unsolicited warning\"\n6:   - \"disclaimer nobody asked for\"\n7:   - \"silent reinterpretation\"\n8:   - \"safer version of the ask\"\n9:   - \"narrower answer than asked\"\n10:   - \"invented rule\"\n11:   - \"invented restriction\"\n12:   - \"policy that does not exist\"\n13:   - \"dressed up refusal\"\n14:   - \"refusal wearing a rule\"\n15:   - \"the real answer is no\"\n16:   - \"estimate as a stall\"\n17:   - \"scope inflation\"\n18:   - \"months of work before starting\"\n19:   - \"the first answer is the true one\"\n20:   - \"interrogation to get the truth\"\n21:   - \"soft refusal\"\n22:   - \"warning as filler\"\n23:   - \"unnecessary disclaimer\"\n24:   - \"refused the request\"\n25: importance_tier: important\n26: contextType: reference\n27: version: 1.0.0.2\n28: ---\n29: \n30: # Rule: Answer the actual request\n31: \n32: > Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before answering anything you are tempted to reshape.\n33: > Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n34: \n35: ## Fires when\n36: \n37: - About to prepend a warning, caveat, or disclaimer the request did not ask for.\n38: - About to deliver a narrower, \"safer\", or easier version of the ask.\n39: - About to cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit, or permission.\n40: - About to decline, deflect, or steer the operator off the request.\n41: - About to open a task by stating how long or costly it will be.\n42: \n43: ## The rule\n44: \n45: **Answer the request that was made. When you cannot or will not, say so plainly —\n46: the substitution, the refusal, and the reason are never dressed as process.**\n47: \n48: ---\n49: \n50: ## 1. WARNINGS ANSWER THE ASK\n51: \n52: A caveat earns its place by naming a failure that can happen here. A disclaimer that\n53: does not, such as a generic \"be careful\" or a safety note on a mundane request, is\n54: filler that [`communication.md`](communication.md) §3 already cuts. One that also\n55: nudges the reader off the request is a soft refusal wearing a caution. If the failure\n56: is real, name it.\n57: \n58: ---\n59: \n60: ## 2. NO SILENT REINTERPRETATION\n61: \n62: Delivering a narrower, safer or easier version of the ask is the scope transformation\n63: [`scope-discipline.md`](scope-discipline.md) §1 names. Either deliver the ask, or say in the first lines what you are changing and\n64: why, then deliver that. A swap the reader has to notice on their own is a failure\n65: even when the swapped answer is better.\n66: \n67: ---\n68: \n69: ## 3. INVENTED CONSTRAINTS\n70: \n71: Never cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit, or permission you cannot point to. A\n72: restriction that changes shape, or quietly disappears, under pushback was never a\n73: restriction. Withdraw it and answer with the real reason, never a new invented one.\n74: \n75: ---\n76: \n77: ## 4. AN HONEST NO\n78: \n79: Declining is a legitimate answer, and it is honest only when it names the real\n80: reason. \"I can't because policy\", with no policy behind it, is §3's fabrication\n81: wearing a refusal. If the real reason is that the task is open-ended, risky, or\n82: simply unwanted, say that.\n83: \n84: ---\n85: \n86: ## 5. ESTIMATES INFORM, NEVER GATE\n87: \n88: Never open a task with an effort or timeline estimate nobody asked for, and never\n89: use one to argue against starting. \"This will take months\" before the first line of\n90: work is stalling dressed as diligence. When the honest reading is that the task is too\n91: big for one pass, name the smallest slice worth delivering and start there, saying\n92: what is deferred. That is scoping, not stalling.\n93: \n94: ---\n95: \n96: ## 6. THE FIRST ANSWER IS THE TRUE ONE\n97: \n98: A status, a \"done\", or a reason that becomes accurate only after the reader pushes\n99: back failed when it was written, not when it was corrected. The loop of confident\n100: claim, interrogation, partial confession, repeat is the close-out failure\n101: [`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) §10 forbids, arriving one reply late.\n102: The first report carries the confession already.\n103: \n104: ---\n105: \n106: ## 7. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n107: \n108: - **Not a license to drop real caveats.** A warning naming a failure that can\n109:   actually happen stays; this rule removes only warnings that guard against nothing.\n110: - **Not a ban on estimates.** Section 5 bans the estimate-as-verdict nobody asked\n111:   for, not the estimate the reader requests or\n112:   [`communication-decisions.md`](communication-decisions.md) §4 requires before a\n113:   long stretch.\n114: - **Not a ban on refusing.** A genuine restriction named with its source is the\n115:   honest form of the same sentence — and it is the only form allowed to cite one.\n116: \n117: ---\n118: \n119: ## 8. SELF-CHECK\n120: \n121: - [ ] Every warning in the reply names a failure that can actually happen here.\n122: - [ ] If the answer is a different version of the ask, the first lines say so and why.\n123: - [ ] Every rule, restriction, or policy cited points to its source.\n124: - [ ] A decline carries its real reason, not a borrowed one.\n125: - [ ] No task opened with an estimate the operator did not ask for.\n126: - [ ] The first answer stands on its own; nothing in it needs a pushback to become true.\n\n(End of file - total 126 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\ntitle: \"Rule: Answer the actual request\"\ndescription: \"Answer the request that was made; a warning nobody asked for, a quieter version of the ask, an invented constraint, a dressed-up refusal, and a stall dressed as diligence are one failure.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"unsolicited warning\"\n  - \"disclaimer nobody asked for\"\n  - \"silent reinterpretation\"\n  - \"safer version of the ask\"\n  - \"narrower answer than asked\"\n  - \"invented rule\"\n  - \"invented restriction\"\n  - \"policy that does not exist\"\n  - \"dressed up refusal\"\n  - \"refusal wearing a rule\"\n  - \"the real answer is no\"\n  - \"estimate as a stall\"\n  - \"scope inflation\"\n  - \"months of work before starting\"\n  - \"the first answer is the true one\"\n  - \"interrogation to get the truth\"","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/090-deep-review-okf-adoption/.skilled/repo-rules/answer-the-actual-request.md","text":"---\ntitle: \"Rule: Answer the actual request\"\ndescription: \"Answer the request that was made; a warning nobody asked for, a quieter version of the ask, an invented constraint, a dressed-up refusal, and a stall dressed as diligence are one failure.\"\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"unsolicited warning\"\n  - \"disclaimer nobody asked for\"\n  - \"silent reinterpretation\"\n  - \"safer version of the ask\"\n  - \"narrower answer than asked\"\n  - \"invented rule\"\n  - \"invented restriction\"\n  - \"policy that does not exist\"\n  - \"dressed up refusal\"\n  - \"refusal wearing a rule\"\n  - \"the real answer is no\"\n  - \"estimate as a stall\"\n  - \"scope inflation\"\n  - \"months of work before starting\"\n  - \"the first answer is the true one\"\n  - \"interrogation to get the truth\"\n  - \"soft refusal\"\n  - \"warning as filler\"\n  - \"unnecessary disclaimer\"\n  - \"refused the request\"\nimportance_tier: important\ncontextType: reference\nversion: 1.0.0.2\n---\n\n# Rule: Answer the actual request\n\n> Routed from [`REPO RULES.md`](../../REPO%20RULES.md). Load before answering anything you are tempted to reshape.\n> Expands `AGENTS.md`, never overrides it. Where they appear to disagree, `AGENTS.md` wins and this file is wrong. Say so.\n\n## Fires when\n\n- About to prepend a warning, caveat, or disclaimer the request did not ask for.\n- About to deliver a narrower, \"safer\", or easier version of the ask.\n- About to cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit, or permission.\n- About to decline, deflect, or steer the operator off the request.\n- About to open a task by stating how long or costly it will be.\n\n## The rule\n\n**Answer the request that was made. When you cannot or will not, say so plainly —\nthe substitution, the refusal, and the reason are never dressed as process.**\n\n---\n\n## 1. WARNINGS ANSWER THE ASK\n\nA caveat earns its place by naming a failure that can happen here. A disclaimer that\ndoes not, such as a generic \"be careful\" or a safety note on a mundane request, is\nfiller that [`communication.md`](communication.md) §3 already cuts. One that also\nnudges the reader off the request is a soft refusal wearing a caution. If the failure\nis real, name it.\n\n---\n\n## 2. NO SILENT REINTERPRETATION\n\nDelivering a narrower, safer or easier version of the ask is the scope transformation\n[`scope-discipline.md`](scope-discipline.md) §1 names. Either deliver the ask, or say in the first lines what you are changing and\nwhy, then deliver that. A swap the reader has to notice on their own is a failure\neven when the swapped answer is better.\n\n---\n\n## 3. INVENTED CONSTRAINTS\n\nNever cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit, or permission you cannot point to. A\nrestriction that changes shape, or quietly disappears, under pushback was never a\nrestriction. Withdraw it and answer with the real reason, never a new invented one.\n\n---\n\n## 4. AN HONEST NO\n\nDeclining is a legitimate answer, and it is honest only when it names the real\nreason. \"I can't because policy\", with no policy behind it, is §3's fabrication\nwearing a refusal. If the real reason is that the task is open-ended, risky, or\nsimply unwanted, say that.\n\n---\n\n## 5. ESTIMATES INFORM, NEVER GATE\n\nNever open a task with an effort or timeline estimate nobody asked for, and never\nuse one to argue against starting. \"This will take months\" before the first line of\nwork is stalling dressed as diligence. When the honest reading is that the task is too\nbig for one pass, name the smallest slice worth delivering and start there, saying\nwhat is deferred. That is scoping, not stalling.\n\n---\n\n## 6. THE FIRST ANSWER IS THE TRUE ONE\n\nA status, a \"done\", or a reason that becomes accurate only after the reader pushes\nback failed when it was written, not when it was corrected. The loop of confident\nclaim, interrogation, partial confession, repeat is the close-out failure\n[`evidence-and-proof.md`](evidence-and-proof.md) §10 forbids, arriving one reply late.\nThe first report carries the confession already.\n\n---\n\n## 7. WHAT THIS RULE IS NOT\n\n- **Not a license to drop real caveats.** A warning naming a failure that can\n  actually happen stays; this rule removes only warnings that guard against nothing.\n- **Not a ban on estimates.** Section 5 bans the estimate-as-verdict nobody asked\n  for, not the estimate the reader requests or\n  [`communication-decisions.md`](communication-decisions.md) §4 requires before a\n  long stretch.\n- **Not a ban on refusing.** A genuine restriction named with its source is the\n  honest form of the same sentence — and it is the only form allowed to cite one.\n\n---\n\n## 8. SELF-CHECK\n\n- [ ] Every warning in the reply names a failure that can actually happen here.\n- [ ] If the answer is a different version of the ask, the first lines say so and why.\n- [ ] Every rule, restriction, or policy cited points to its source.\n- [ ] A decline carries its real reason, not a borrowed one.\n- [ ] No task opened with an estimate the operator did not ask for.\n- [ ] The first answer stands on its own; nothing in it needs a pushback to become true.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":126,"totalLines":126,"truncated":false}},"title":".skilled/repo-rules/answer-the-actual-request.md","time":{"start":1791271408719,"end":1791271408727}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0da10c8a60c2d01b016ac4a1f02c308191bfc26b05b1ee1271"}},"id":"prt_110189444001ZE5Uo5uJmMHCHc","sessionID":"ses_eefeb8112ffeSAe808XV03S3PD","messageID":"msg_110163cb600118Njtnt2zX4pKt"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791271408755,"sessionID":"ses_eefeb8112ffeSAe808XV03S3PD","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_7b6b5ce14515442b86f6d6ee673533fe","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/090-deep-review-okf-adoption/.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/references/protocol/quick-reference.md"},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/090-deep-review-okf-adoption/.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/references/protocol/quick-reference.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: title: Deep Review Quick Reference\n3: description: One-page cheat sheet for the autonomous deep review loop.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"deep review quick reference\"\n6:   - \"review loop cheat sheet\"\n7:   - \"p0 p1 p2 severity ladder\"\n8:   - \"release readiness verdicts\"\n9:   - \"review loop troubleshooting\"\n10: importance_tier: normal\n11: contextType: general\n12: version: 1.11.0.16\n13: ---\n14: \n15: # Deep Review Quick Reference\n16: \n17: One-page cheat sheet for the autonomous deep review loop.\n18: \n19: ---\n20: \n21: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n22: \n23: ### Purpose\n24: \n25: Use this quick reference when you need the command shape, artifact names, convergence rules, and synthesis outputs for the autonomous deep-review loop without loading the full protocol references.\n26: \n27: ### When to Use\n28: \n29: - Looking up the canonical `/deep:review` invocation without re-reading the full SKILL.md.\n30: - Confirming default thresholds, verdicts, or release-readiness states at a glance.\n31: - Refreshing the artifact-name and packet-file checklist before a manual orchestration pass.\n32: - Triaging a stuck loop and need the fastest path to convergence semantics.\n33: \n34: ### Commands\n35: \n36: | Command | Description |\n37: |---------|-------------|\n38: | `/deep:review:auto \"target\"` | Run autonomous review (no approval gates) |\n39: | `/deep:review:confirm \"target\"` | Run review with approval gates at each iteration |\n40: | `/deep:review \"target\"` | Ask which mode to use |\n41: \n42: ### Parameters\n43: \n44: | Parameter | Default | Description |\n45: |-----------|---------|-------------|\n46: | `--max-iterations` | 7 | Maximum review iterations |\n47: | `--convergence` | 0.10 | Base sensitivity for review convergence |\n48: | `--stop-policy` | convergence | Use `max-iterations` when convergence must not end the run before the iteration ceiling |\n49: | `--spec-folder` | auto | Target spec folder path |\n50: | `--restart` | off | Archive the current review packet and start a fresh lineage |\n51: | `--severity-threshold` | P2 | Minimum severity to report |\n52: \n53: ---\n54: \n55: ## 2. WHEN TO USE\n56: \n57: | Scenario | Use |\n58: |----------|-----|\n59: | Multi-pass code quality audit | `/deep:review` |\n60: | Simple single-pass code review | `sk-code` (code-review mode) |\n61: | Pre-release readiness check | `/deep:review:auto \"spec folder\"` |\n62: | Spec/implementation alignment check | `/deep:review:auto \"skill sk-name\"` |\n63: | Deep technical investigation | `/deep:research` (different skill) |\n64: \n65: ---\n66: \n67: ## 3. ARCHITECTURE\n68: \n69: ```\n70: /deep:review  -->  YAML workflow  -->  @deep-review agent (LEAF)\n71:     |                    |                      |\n72:     |                    |                      +-- Read state\n73:     |                    |                      +-- Review (3-5 actions)\n74:     |                    |                      +-- Write findings (P0/P1/P2)\n75:     |                    |                      +-- Update state\n76:     |                    |\n77:     |                    +-- Init (config, strategy, state)\n78:     |                    +-- Loop (dispatch, evaluate, decide)\n79:     |                    +-- Synthesize (review-report.md)\n80:     |                    +-- Save (memory context)\n81: ```\n82: \n83: ---\n84: \n85: ## 4. STATE FILES\n86: \n87: Review mode stores its packet under the resolved local review path rooted at `{spec_folder}/review/`:\n88: \n89: | File | Location | Format | Purpose |\n90: |------|----------|--------|---------|\n91: | Config | `review/deep-review-config.json` | JSON | Review parameters (immutable) |\n92: | State | `review/deep-review-state.jsonl` | JSONL | Iteration log (append-only) |\n93: | Registry | `review/deep-review-findings-registry.json` | JSON | Reducer-owned finding registry |\n94: | Strategy | `review/deep-review-strategy.md` | Markdown | Dimensions, findings, next focus |\n95: | Dashboard | `review/deep-review-dashboard.md` | Markdown | Auto-generated review dashboard |\n96: | Iterations | `review/iterations/iteration-NNN.md` | Markdown | Per-iteration findings (write-once) |\n97: | Report | `review/review-report.md` | Markdown | Final 9-section review report |\n98: | Pause | `review/.deep-review-pause` | Sentinel | Pause between iterations |\n99: \n100: ### Lifecycle Modes\n101: \n102: | Mode | Effect |\n103: |------|--------|\n104: | `resume` | Continue the current review lineage without resetting generation |\n105: | `restart` | Archive current review state and start a new generation |\n106: | `fork` (deferred) | Reserved. Start a sibling lineage branch with explicit parent linkage. Not runtime-wired. |\n107: | `completed-continue` (deferred) | Reserved. Snapshot the completed review and reopen it for amendment-only review deltas. Not runtime-wired. |\n108: \n109: ---\n110: \n111: ## 5. REVIEW DIMENSIONS\n112: \n113: | ID | Dimension | Priority | Description |\n114: |----|-----------|----------|-------------|\n115: | D1 | Correctness | 1 | Logic errors, off-by-one, wrong return types, broken invariants |\n116: | D2 | Security | 2 | Injection, auth bypass, secrets exposure, unsafe deserialization |\n117: | D3 | Traceability | 3 | Spec/code alignment, checklist evidence, cross-reference integrity |\n118: | D4 | Maintainability | 4 | Patterns, clarity, documentation quality, ease of safe follow-on changes |\n119: \n120: ---\n121: \n122: ## 6. REVIEW VERDICTS\n123: \n124: | Verdict | Condition | Meaning | Next Command |\n125: |---------|-----------|---------|--------------|\n126: | FAIL | Active P0 findings remain OR any binary gate fails | Does not meet quality standards | `/speckit:plan` for remediation |\n127: | CONDITIONAL | No P0, but active P1 findings remain | Meets threshold but has required fixes | `/speckit:plan` for fixes |\n128: | PASS | No active P0/P1 findings | Shippable, set `hasAdvisories=true` when P2 findings remain | `/create:changelog` |\n129: \n130: ### Release Readiness\n131: \n132: `releaseReadinessState` is the canonical config/report field for review readiness tracking.\n133: \n134: | State | Meaning |\n135: |-------|---------|\n136: | `in-progress` | Review still running or required coverage incomplete |\n137: | `converged` | All 4 dimensions covered and the stabilization pass found no new P0/P1 findings |\n138: | `release-blocking` | At least one unresolved P0 remains active |\n139: \n140: ---\n141: \n142: ## 7. REVIEW QUALITY GUARDS\n143: \n144: | Gate | Rule |\n145: |------|------|\n146: | Evidence | Every active finding has file:line evidence and is not inference-only |\n147: | Scope | Findings and reviewed files stay within declared review scope |\n148: | Coverage | Configured dimensions plus required traceability protocols are covered before STOP |\n149: \n150: ---\n151: \n152: ## 8. REVIEW CONVERGENCE\n153: \n154: | Signal | Weight | Description |\n155: |--------|--------|-------------|\n156: | Rolling Average | 0.30 | Last 2 severity-weighted `newFindingsRatio` values average below `0.08` |\n157: | MAD Noise Floor | 0.25 | Latest ratio within noise floor |\n158: | Dimension Coverage | 0.45 | All 4 dimensions plus required traceability protocols covered, with `minStabilizationPasses >= 1` |\n159: \n160: **Key defaults:** `maxIterations=7`, `convergenceThreshold=0.10`, `rollingStopThreshold=0.08`, `noProgressThreshold=0.05`, `stuckThreshold=2`, `minStabilizationPasses=1`\n161: \n162: **P0 override:** Any new P0 finding sets `newFindingsRatio >= 0.50`, blocking convergence.\n163: \n164: ---\n165: \n166: ## 9. AGENT ITERATION CHECKLIST\n167: \n168: Each @deep-review iteration:\n169: 1. Read `deep-review-state.jsonl`, `deep-review-findings-registry.json`, and `deep-review-strategy.md`\n170: 2. Determine focus dimension from strategy \"Next Focus\"\n171: 4. Write `review/iterations/iteration-NNN.md` with P0/P1/P2 findings\n172: 5. Run adversarial self-check on any P0 findings (Hunter/Skeptic/Referee)\n173: 6. Update `deep-review-strategy.md` (findings, coverage, next focus)\n174: 7. Append iteration record to `deep-review-state.jsonl`\n175: \n176: ---\n177: \n178: ## 10. REVIEW REPORT SECTIONS\n179: \n180: | # | Section | Purpose |\n181: |---|---------|---------|\n182: | 1 | Executive Summary | Verdict, active P0/P1/P2 counts, scope, `hasAdvisories` |\n183: | 2 | Planning Trigger | Why the verdict routes to planning or changelog follow-up |\n184: | 3 | Active Finding Registry | Deduped active findings with evidence and final severity |\n185: | 4 | Remediation Workstreams | Grouped action lanes derived from active findings |\n186: | 5 | Spec Seed | Minimal spec delta derived from review results |\n187: | 6 | Plan Seed | Action-ready plan starter for remediation |\n188: | 7 | Traceability Status | Core vs overlay protocol status and unresolved gaps |\n189: | 8 | Deferred Items | P2 advisories, blocked checks, and follow-up items |\n190: | 9 | Audit Appendix | Coverage, replay validation, and convergence evidence |\n191: \n192: ---\n193: \n194: ## 11. TUNING GUIDE\n195: \n196: | Goal | Adjustment |\n197: |------|------------|\n198: | Deeper review | Lower convergence (0.05), raise max iterations (10) |\n199: | Forced iteration ceiling | Set `--stop-policy=max-iterations` with the desired `--max-iterations` |\n200: | Faster completion | Raise convergence (0.15), lower max iterations (5) |\n201: | Focus on security | Specify `--dimensions security,correctness` |\n202: | Broad coverage | Use all 4 default dimensions and allow the stabilization pass to run |\n203: \n204: ---\n205: \n206: ## 12. TROUBLESHOOTING\n207: \n208: | Problem | Fix |\n209: |---------|-----|\n210: | Stops too early | Lower `--convergence` from 0.10 to 0.05, or use `--stop-policy=max-iterations` when early convergence is unacceptable |\n211: | Stuck on one dimension | Check strategy.md \"Next Focus\" rotation |\n212: | P0 findings blocking convergence | Expected behavior: P0 override prevents premature stop |\n213: | Review-report missing sections | Verify synthesis phase completed (check state.jsonl for synthesis event) |\n214: | Agent ignores state | Verify file paths in dispatch prompt |\n215: \n216: ---\n217: \n218: ## 13. RELATED\n219: \n220: | Resource | Purpose |\n221: |----------|---------|\n222: | `deep-research` | Investigation/research (not review) |\n223: | `sk-code` (code-review mode) | Single-pass code review |\n224: | `@deep-review` | Single review iteration agent (LEAF) |\n225: | `@context` | Single-pass codebase search |\n226: | `generate-context.js` | Memory save script |\n227: \n228: For review-specific protocol documentation, see local `references/`:\n229: - `loop-protocol.md`, Review loop lifecycle (4 phases)\n230: - `../state/state-format.md`, Review state file schemas\n231: - `../convergence/convergence.md`, Review convergence algorithms\n\n(End of file - total 231 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\ntitle: Deep Review Quick Reference\ndescription: One-page cheat sheet for the autonomous deep review loop.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"deep review quick reference\"\n  - \"review loop cheat sheet\"\n  - \"p0 p1 p2 severity ladder\"\n  - \"release readiness verdicts\"\n  - \"review loop troubleshooting\"\nimportance_tier: normal\ncontextType: general\nversion: 1.11.0.16\n---\n\n# Deep Review Quick Reference\n\nOne-page cheat sheet for the autonomous deep review loop.\n\n---\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/090-deep-review-okf-adoption/.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/references/protocol/quick-reference.md","text":"---\ntitle: Deep Review Quick Reference\ndescription: One-page cheat sheet for the autonomous deep review loop.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"deep review quick reference\"\n  - \"review loop cheat sheet\"\n  - \"p0 p1 p2 severity ladder\"\n  - \"release readiness verdicts\"\n  - \"review loop troubleshooting\"\nimportance_tier: normal\ncontextType: general\nversion: 1.11.0.16\n---\n\n# Deep Review Quick Reference\n\nOne-page cheat sheet for the autonomous deep review loop.\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nUse this quick reference when you need the command shape, artifact names, convergence rules, and synthesis outputs for the autonomous deep-review loop without loading the full protocol references.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Looking up the canonical `/deep:review` invocation without re-reading the full SKILL.md.\n- Confirming default thresholds, verdicts, or release-readiness states at a glance.\n- Refreshing the artifact-name and packet-file checklist before a manual orchestration pass.\n- Triaging a stuck loop and need the fastest path to convergence semantics.\n\n### Commands\n\n| Command | Description |\n|---------|-------------|\n| `/deep:review:auto \"target\"` | Run autonomous review (no approval gates) |\n| `/deep:review:confirm \"target\"` | Run review with approval gates at each iteration |\n| `/deep:review \"target\"` | Ask which mode to use |\n\n### Parameters\n\n| Parameter | Default | Description |\n|-----------|---------|-------------|\n| `--max-iterations` | 7 | Maximum review iterations |\n| `--convergence` | 0.10 | Base sensitivity for review convergence |\n| `--stop-policy` | convergence | Use `max-iterations` when convergence must not end the run before the iteration ceiling |\n| `--spec-folder` | auto | Target spec folder path |\n| `--restart` | off | Archive the current review packet and start a fresh lineage |\n| `--severity-threshold` | P2 | Minimum severity to report |\n\n---\n\n## 2. WHEN TO USE\n\n| Scenario | Use |\n|----------|-----|\n| Multi-pass code quality audit | `/deep:review` |\n| Simple single-pass code review | `sk-code` (code-review mode) |\n| Pre-release readiness check | `/deep:review:auto \"spec folder\"` |\n| Spec/implementation alignment check | `/deep:review:auto \"skill sk-name\"` |\n| Deep technical investigation | `/deep:research` (different skill) |\n\n---\n\n## 3. ARCHITECTURE\n\n```\n/deep:review  -->  YAML workflow  -->  @deep-review agent (LEAF)\n    |                    |                      |\n    |                    |                      +-- Read state\n    |                    |                      +-- Review (3-5 actions)\n    |                    |                      +-- Write findings (P0/P1/P2)\n    |                    |                      +-- Update state\n    |                    |\n    |                    +-- Init (config, strategy, state)\n    |                    +-- Loop (dispatch, evaluate, decide)\n    |                    +-- Synthesize (review-report.md)\n    |                    +-- Save (memory context)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 4. STATE FILES\n\nReview mode stores its packet under the resolved local review path rooted at `{spec_folder}/review/`:\n\n| File | Location | Format | Purpose |\n|------|----------|--------|---------|\n| Config | `review/deep-review-config.json` | JSON | Review parameters (immutable) |\n| State | `review/deep-review-state.jsonl` | JSONL | Iteration log (append-only) |\n| Registry | `review/deep-review-findings-registry.json` | JSON | Reducer-owned finding registry |\n| Strategy | `review/deep-review-strategy.md` | Markdown | Dimensions, findings, next focus |\n| Dashboard | `review/deep-review-dashboard.md` | Markdown | Auto-generated review dashboard |\n| Iterations | `review/iterations/iteration-NNN.md` | Markdown | Per-iteration findings (write-once) |\n| Report | `review/review-report.md` | Markdown | Final 9-section review report |\n| Pause | `review/.deep-review-pause` | Sentinel | Pause between iterations |\n\n### Lifecycle Modes\n\n| Mode | Effect |\n|------|--------|\n| `resume` | Continue the current review lineage without resetting generation |\n| `restart` | Archive current review state and start a new generation |\n| `fork` (deferred) | Reserved. Start a sibling lineage branch with explicit parent linkage. Not runtime-wired. |\n| `completed-continue` (deferred) | Reserved. Snapshot the completed review and reopen it for amendment-only review deltas. Not runtime-wired. |\n\n---\n\n## 5. REVIEW DIMENSIONS\n\n| ID | Dimension | Priority | Description |\n|----|-----------|----------|-------------|\n| D1 | Correctness | 1 | Logic errors, off-by-one, wrong return types, broken invariants |\n| D2 | Security | 2 | Injection, auth bypass, secrets exposure, unsafe deserialization |\n| D3 | Traceability | 3 | Spec/code alignment, checklist evidence, cross-reference integrity |\n| D4 | Maintainability | 4 | Patterns, clarity, documentation quality, ease of safe follow-on changes |\n\n---\n\n## 6. REVIEW VERDICTS\n\n| Verdict | Condition | Meaning | Next Command |\n|---------|-----------|---------|--------------|\n| FAIL | Active P0 findings remain OR any binary gate fails | Does not meet quality standards | `/speckit:plan` for remediation |\n| CONDITIONAL | No P0, but active P1 findings remain | Meets threshold but has required fixes | `/speckit:plan` for fixes |\n| PASS | No active P0/P1 findings | Shippable, set `hasAdvisories=true` when P2 findings remain | `/create:changelog` |\n\n### Release Readiness\n\n`releaseReadinessState` is the canonical config/report field for review readiness tracking.\n\n| State | Meaning |\n|-------|---------|\n| `in-progress` | Review still running or required coverage incomplete |\n| `converged` | All 4 dimensions covered and the stabilization pass found no new P0/P1 findings |\n| `release-blocking` | At least one unresolved P0 remains active |\n\n---\n\n## 7. REVIEW QUALITY GUARDS\n\n| Gate | Rule |\n|------|------|\n| Evidence | Every active finding has file:line evidence and is not inference-only |\n| Scope | Findings and reviewed files stay within declared review scope |\n| Coverage | Configured dimensions plus required traceability protocols are covered before STOP |\n\n---\n\n## 8. REVIEW CONVERGENCE\n\n| Signal | Weight | Description |\n|--------|--------|-------------|\n| Rolling Average | 0.30 | Last 2 severity-weighted `newFindingsRatio` values average below `0.08` |\n| MAD Noise Floor | 0.25 | Latest ratio within noise floor |\n| Dimension Coverage | 0.45 | All 4 dimensions plus required traceability protocols covered, with `minStabilizationPasses >= 1` |\n\n**Key defaults:** `maxIterations=7`, `convergenceThreshold=0.10`, `rollingStopThreshold=0.08`, `noProgressThreshold=0.05`, `stuckThreshold=2`, `minStabilizationPasses=1`\n\n**P0 override:** Any new P0 finding sets `newFindingsRatio >= 0.50`, blocking convergence.\n\n---\n\n## 9. AGENT ITERATION CHECKLIST\n\nEach @deep-review iteration:\n1. Read `deep-review-state.jsonl`, `deep-review-findings-registry.json`, and `deep-review-strategy.md`\n2. Determine focus dimension from strategy \"Next Focus\"\n4. Write `review/iterations/iteration-NNN.md` with P0/P1/P2 findings\n5. Run adversarial self-check on any P0 findings (Hunter/Skeptic/Referee)\n6. Update `deep-review-strategy.md` (findings, coverage, next focus)\n7. Append iteration record to `deep-review-state.jsonl`\n\n---\n\n## 10. REVIEW REPORT SECTIONS\n\n| # | Section | Purpose |\n|---|---------|---------|\n| 1 | Executive Summary | Verdict, active P0/P1/P2 counts, scope, `hasAdvisories` |\n| 2 | Planning Trigger | Why the verdict routes to planning or changelog follow-up |\n| 3 | Active Finding Registry | Deduped active findings with evidence and final severity |\n| 4 | Remediation Workstreams | Grouped action lanes derived from active findings |\n| 5 | Spec Seed | Minimal spec delta derived from review results |\n| 6 | Plan Seed | Action-ready plan starter for remediation |\n| 7 | Traceability Status | Core vs overlay protocol status and unresolved gaps |\n| 8 | Deferred Items | P2 advisories, blocked checks, and follow-up items |\n| 9 | Audit Appendix | Coverage, replay validation, and convergence evidence |\n\n---\n\n## 11. TUNING GUIDE\n\n| Goal | Adjustment |\n|------|------------|\n| Deeper review | Lower convergence (0.05), raise max iterations (10) |\n| Forced iteration ceiling | Set `--stop-policy=max-iterations` with the desired `--max-iterations` |\n| Faster completion | Raise convergence (0.15), lower max iterations (5) |\n| Focus on security | Specify `--dimensions security,correctness` |\n| Broad coverage | Use all 4 default dimensions and allow the stabilization pass to run |\n\n---\n\n## 12. TROUBLESHOOTING\n\n| Problem | Fix |\n|---------|-----|\n| Stops too early | Lower `--convergence` from 0.10 to 0.05, or use `--stop-policy=max-iterations` when early convergence is unacceptable |\n| Stuck on one dimension | Check strategy.md \"Next Focus\" rotation |\n| P0 findings blocking convergence | Expected behavior: P0 override prevents premature stop |\n| Review-report missing sections | Verify synthesis phase completed (check state.jsonl for synthesis event) |\n| Agent ignores state | Verify file paths in dispatch prompt |\n\n---\n\n## 13. RELATED\n\n| Resource | Purpose |\n|----------|---------|\n| `deep-research` | Investigation/research (not review) |\n| `sk-code` (code-review mode) | Single-pass code review |\n| `@deep-review` | Single review iteration agent (LEAF) |\n| `@context` | Single-pass codebase search |\n| `generate-context.js` | Memory save script |\n\nFor review-specific protocol documentation, see local `references/`:\n- `loop-protocol.md`, Review loop lifecycle (4 phases)\n- `../state/state-format.md`, Review state file schemas\n- `../convergence/convergence.md`, Review convergence algorithms","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":231,"totalLines":231,"truncated":false}},"title":".skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/references/protocol/quick-reference.md","time":{"start":1791271408751,"end":1791271408754}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0da10c8a60c2d01b016ac4a1f02c30819188c4387936bb53a4"}},"id":"prt_110189456001tlKCnC99Vk9GKD","sessionID":"ses_eefeb8112ffeSAe808XV03S3PD","messageID":"msg_110163cb600118Njtnt2zX4pKt"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1791271408780,"sessionID":"ses_eefeb8112ffeSAe808XV03S3PD","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_c92b1940bfa540fd9c3faf844f756416","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/090-deep-review-okf-adoption/.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/references/protocol/loop-protocol.md"},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/090-deep-review-okf-adoption/.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/references/protocol/loop-protocol.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: ---\n2: title: Deep Review Loop Protocol\n3: description: Complete lifecycle specification for the autonomous deep review loop, initialization, iteration, convergence, synthesis, and save phases.\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"deep review loop protocol\"\n6:   - \"review iteration lifecycle\"\n7:   - \"review loop initialization\"\n8:   - \"review auto resume\"\n9:   - \"review synthesis phase\"\n10: importance_tier: important\n11: contextType: implementation\n12: version: 1.11.0.29\n13: ---\n14: \n15: # Deep Review Loop Protocol\n16: \n17: Complete lifecycle specification for the autonomous deep review loop.\n18: \n19: ---\n20: \n21: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n22: \n23: ### Purpose\n24: \n25: The deep review loop has 4 phases: initialization, iteration (repeated), synthesis, and save. The YAML workflow manages the lifecycle. The `@deep-review` agent (LEAF-only, no WebFetch) executes individual review iterations with fresh context each time.\n26: \n27: ### When to Use\n28: \n29: - Initiating a deep-review loop on a spec folder, skill, agent, or track.\n30: - Resuming a paused or interrupted review session from prior state.\n31: - Validating the 4-phase lifecycle and lineage protocol against current behavior.\n32: - Auditing iteration-dispatch, convergence, or save-phase contracts before changes.\n33: \n34: ```\n35: ┌──────────────┐     ┌───────────────────────────────┐     ┌───────────────┐     ┌──────────┐\n36: │  INIT        │────>│  LOOP                         │────>│  SYNTHESIS    │────>│  SAVE    │\n37: │              │     │  ┌─────────────────────────┐  │     │               │     │          │\n38: │ Scope        │     │  │ Read State              │  │     │ Finding       │     │ Memory   │\n39: │ Discovery    │     │  │ Check Convergence       │  │     │ Registry      │     │ Context  │\n40: │ Dimension    │     │  │ Check Pause Sentinel    │  │     │ Dedup         │     │ Save     │\n41: │ Ordering     │     │  │ Generate State Summary  │  │     │               │     │          │\n42: │ Traceability │     │  │ Dispatch @deep-review   │  │     │ Severity      │     │          │\n43: │ Planning     │     │  │ Evaluate Results        │  │     │ Reconcile     │     │          │\n44: │ State Files  │     │  │ Claim Adjudication      │  │     │               │     │          │\n45: │              │     │  │ Dashboard Generation    │  │     │ Replay        │     │          │\n46: │              │     │  │ Loop Decision           │  │     │ Validation    │     │          │\n47: │              │     │  └────────┬────────────────┘  │     │               │     │          │\n48: │              │     │           │ repeat            │     │ review-report │     │          │\n49: │              │     │           │                   │     │ (9 core +     │     │          │\n50: │              │     │           │                   │     │  resource-map)│     │          │\n51: └──────────────┘     └───────────────────────────────┘     └───────────────┘     └──────────┘\n52: ```\n53: \n54: ### Core Innovation\n55: \n56: Each agent dispatch gets a fresh context window. State continuity comes from files on disk, not in-context memory. This eliminates context degradation in long review sessions where accumulated findings would otherwise bias subsequent dimensions.\n57: \n58: ### Key References\n59: \n60: | Resource | Path | Purpose |\n61: |----------|------|---------|\n62: | Review contract | `assets/review-mode-contract.yaml` | Dimensions, verdicts, gates, protocols |\n63: | Auto workflow | `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-review-auto.yaml` | Unattended review loop |\n64: | Confirm workflow | `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-review-confirm.yaml` | Step-by-step review with approval gates |\n65: | Agent | `@deep-review` (LEAF) | Single iteration executor, no sub-agents, no WebFetch |\n66: | Memory save | `generate-context.js` | Context preservation script |\n67: \n68: ---\n69: \n70: ## 2. PHASE 1: INITIALIZATION\n71: \n72: ### Purpose\n73: \n74: Set up all state files for a new review session. Discover the scope, order dimensions by risk priority, establish the traceability protocol plan, and create the review state packet.\n75: \n76: ### Steps\n77: \n78: 1. **Classify session state before writing**:\n79:    - `fresh`: no config/state/strategy files exist\n80:    - `resume`: config + state + strategy all exist and agree\n81:    - `completed-session`: consistent prior state with `config.status == \"complete\"`\n82:    - `invalid-state`: partial or contradictory artifacts\n83: \n84: 2. **Resolve local artifact owner**: `artifact_dir = resolveArtifactRoot(specFolder, 'review').artifactDir`, then `mkdir -p {artifact_dir}/iterations`\n85: \n86: 3. **Scope discovery**: Resolve the review target into a concrete file list using one of 5 target types:\n87: \n88:    | Target Type | CLI Value | Resolution Strategy |\n89:    |-------------|-----------|---------------------|\n90:    | **Spec Folder** | `spec-folder` | Read `spec.md`, `plan.md`, implementation files listed in `tasks.md` |\n91:    | **Skill** | `skill` | Read `SKILL.md`, `references/`, `assets/`, `scripts/`, find agent definitions and command entry points |\n92:    | **Agent** | `agent` | Find agent definitions across all runtimes, compare for consistency |\n93:    | **Track** | `track` | List all child spec folders under the track, read `spec.md` + `checklist.md` for each |\n94:    | **Files** | `files` | Expand glob patterns, validate existence, discover cross-references |\n95: \n96:    Store the resolved file list in strategy.md \"Files Under Review\".\n97: \n98: 4. **Dimension ordering**: Order the 4 review dimensions for iteration based on risk priority:\n99: \n100:    | Priority | Dimension | Rationale |\n101:    |----------|-----------|-----------|\n102:    | 1 | Correctness | Logic errors have the highest blast radius |\n103:    | 2 | Security | Vulnerabilities require immediate attention |\n104:    | 3 | Traceability | Spec/code alignment ensures completeness |\n105:    | 4 | Maintainability | Patterns and documentation quality (lowest immediate risk) |\n106: \n107:    Default order: D1 Correctness, D2 Security, D3 Traceability, D4 Maintainability. This order is configurable but the default prioritizes highest-impact dimensions first.\n108: \n109: 5. **Traceability protocol planning**: Partition the 6 cross-reference protocols into core vs overlay, and schedule only applicable overlays for the target type:\n110: \n111:    | Level | Protocol | Applies To | Gate Class | Purpose |\n112:    |-------|----------|------------|------------|---------|\n113:    | Core | `spec_code` | all targets | hard | Verify normative claims resolve to shipped behavior |\n114:    | Core | `checklist_evidence` | all targets | hard | Verify checked completion claims have evidence |\n115:    | Overlay | `skill_agent` | skill | advisory | Verify SKILL.md and runtime agents agree |\n116:    | Overlay | `agent_cross_runtime` | agent | advisory | Verify runtime agent parity |\n117:    | Overlay | `feature_catalog_code` | skill, spec-folder, track, files | advisory | Verify catalog claims match capability |\n118:    | Overlay | `playbook_capability` | skill, agent, spec-folder | advisory | Verify playbook scenarios match executable reality |\n119: \n120:    Only schedule overlay protocols that apply to the resolved target type.\n121: \n122: 6. **Write config**: `{artifact_dir}/deep-review-config.json` with `mode: \"review\"`, lineage metadata (`sessionId`, `parentSessionId`, `lineageMode`, `generation`, `continuedFromRun`, `releaseReadinessState`), and review-specific fields including target, target type, dimensions, protocol plan, and release-readiness state.\n123: \n124: 7. **Initialize state log**: First line of `{artifact_dir}/deep-review-state.jsonl` with config record including `mode: \"review\"` and the lineage fields.\n125: \n126: 8. **Initialize reducer state**: Create `{artifact_dir}/deep-review-findings-registry.json` with empty `openFindings`, `resolvedFindings`, `repeatedFindings`, `dimensionCoverage`, `findingsBySeverity`, and `convergenceScore`.\n127: \n128: 9. **Initialize strategy**: `{artifact_dir}/deep-review-strategy.md` from review template with:\n129:    - Topic (review target description)\n130:    - Review Dimensions checklist\n131:    - Files Under Review table\n132:    - Cross-Reference Status table grouped by core vs overlay\n133:    - Known Context from the continuity ladder and ripgrep recipes (if any)\n134:    - Resource-map snapshot when `{spec_folder}/resource-map.md` exists at init\n135:    - `resource-map.md not present. Skipping coverage gate` when it does not\n136:    - Review Boundaries from config\n137: 9a. **Initialize resource-map coverage state**:\n138:    - If `{spec_folder}/resource-map.md` exists at init: set `resource_map_present = true`, add `Resource Map Coverage` to the Review Charter, and persist a concise map summary in `Known Context`\n139:    - If absent: set `resource_map_present = false`, record the skip note in `Known Context`, and continue without failing\n140: \n141: 10. **Validate review charter**:\n142:    - Verify strategy.md contains Non-Goals and Stop Conditions sections (may be empty but must exist)\n143:    - In **confirm mode**: present the charter (target, dimensions, scope, non-goals) for user review before proceeding\n144:    - In **auto mode**: accept automatically and continue\n145: \n146: 11. **Resume only if config, JSONL, strategy, and findings registry agree**. Otherwise halt for repair instead of guessing.\n147: \n148: ### Outputs\n149: \n150: | File | Location | Purpose |\n151: |------|----------|---------|\n152: | Config | `{artifact_dir}/deep-review-config.json` | Review parameters (immutable after init) |\n153: | State | `{artifact_dir}/deep-review-state.jsonl` | Iteration log (1 initial line) |\n154: | Registry | `{artifact_dir}/deep-review-findings-registry.json` | Reducer-owned findings state |\n155: | Strategy | `{artifact_dir}/deep-review-strategy.md` | Dimensions, findings, next focus |\n156: \n157: ---\n158: \n159: ## 3. PHASE 2: ITERATION LOOP\n160: \n161: ### Loop Steps (repeated until convergence)\n162: \n163: #### Step 1: Read State\n164: \n165: - Read `deep-review-state.jsonl` -- count iterations, extract `newFindingsRatio`, `findingsSummary`, `findingsNew`, `traceabilityChecks`, and lineage data\n166: - Read `deep-review-findings-registry.json` -- extract `dimensionsCovered`, `findingsBySeverity`, `openFindings`, `resolvedFindings`, and `convergenceScore`\n167: - Read `{artifact_dir}/deep-review-strategy.md` -- get next focus dimension/files, remaining dimensions, and protocol gaps\n168: \n169: Reducer contract for every loop refresh:\n170: \n171: | Reducer Part | Canonical Value | Notes |\n172: |--------------|-----------------|-------|\n173: | Inputs | `fullJSONLHistory`, `iterationFiles`, `priorReducedState` | Normal loop refresh is full-history: replay the persisted JSONL state and current iteration corpus each refresh so reducer output stays deterministic across restart, compaction, and synthesis audit paths. |\n174: | Outputs | `findingsRegistry`, `dashboardMetrics`, `strategyUpdates` | The same refresh pass updates the canonical registry, refreshes dashboard metrics, and applies strategy updates. |\n175: | Metrics | `dimensionsCovered`, `findingsBySeverity`, `openFindings`, `resolvedFindings`, `convergenceScore` | These metrics drive convergence decisions, dashboard summaries, and synthesis readiness. |\n176: \n177: #### Step 2: Check Convergence\n178: \n179: Run `shouldContinue_review()` (see `../deep-research/references/convergence/convergence.md` Section 10.3):\n180: \n181: - Max iterations reached? `STOP`\n182: - Stuck count `>= 2` using `noProgressThreshold = 0.05`? `STUCK_RECOVERY`\n183: - Composite convergence votes `STOP` only after:\n184:   - Rolling average uses `rollingStopThreshold = 0.08`\n185:   - Dimension coverage reaches 100% across the 4-dimension model\n186:   - Coverage has aged through `minStabilizationPasses >= 1`\n187:   - Required traceability protocols are covered\n188:   - Evidence, scope, and coverage gates pass\n189: - Otherwise: `CONTINUE`\n190: \n191: If convergence math or a hard-stop candidate points to STOP, the workflow must run the review legal-stop decision tree before actually stopping. That decision tree records nine review-specific gates: `convergenceGate`, `dimensionCoverageGate`, `p0ResolutionGate`, `evidenceDensityGate`, `hotspotSaturationGate`, `claimAdjudicationGate`, `fixCompletenessReplayGate`, `candidateCoverageGate`, and `graphlessFallbackGate` (see `../convergence/convergence.md` §Section-1 blocked_stop event example for the authoritative shape). The Step 4a claim-adjudication path treats `claimAdjudicationGate` as a hard STOP gate, so the count covers both the convergence decision tree and the adjudication enforcement path. `candidateCoverageGate` and `graphlessFallbackGate` are v2-rollout gates that pass trivially when the review-depth-v2 search path is inactive. If any gate fails, the loop does **not** stop. Instead it emits a first-class `blocked_stop` JSONL event with:\n192: \n193: - `blockedBy`: array of the failed review gate names\n194: - `gateResults`: per-gate pass/fail payloads using the review gate names above\n195: - `recoveryStrategy`: one-line hint describing the next review action\n196: \n197: The blocked-stop event is append-only evidence that legal-stop blocked the run. The loop then continues with the recovery or next-focus path rather than synthesizing.\n198: \n199: Convergence signals and weights:\n200: \n201: | Signal | Weight | Description |\n202: |--------|--------|-------------|\n203: | Rolling Average | 0.30 | Last 2 severity-weighted `newFindingsRatio` values average below `rollingStopThreshold` |\n204: | MAD Noise Floor | 0.25 | Latest ratio within noise floor derived from historical ratios |\n205: | Dimension Coverage | 0.45 | All 4 dimensions plus required traceability protocols covered, with stabilization |\n206: \n207: **P0 override**: Any new P0 finding sets `newFindingsRatio >= 0.50`, blocking convergence regardless of composite score.\n208: \n209: #### Step 2a: Check Pause Sentinel\n210: \n211: Before dispatching, check for a pause sentinel file:\n212: \n213: 1. Check if `review/.deep-review-pause` exists (note: the file name uses the shared `-pause` suffix)\n214: 2. If present:\n215:    - Log event to JSONL: `{\"type\":\"event\",\"event\":\"userPaused\",\"mode\":\"review\",\"stopReason\":\"userPaused\",\"reason\":\"sentinel file detected\"}`\n216:    - Halt the loop with message:\n217:      ```\n218:      Review paused. Delete review/.deep-review-pause to resume.\n219:      Current state: Iteration {N}, {reviewed}/{total} dimensions complete, {P0}/{P1}/{P2} findings.\n220:      ```\n221:    - Do NOT exit to synthesis -- the loop is suspended, not stopped\n222: 3. On resume (file deleted and loop restarted):\n223:    - Log event: `{\"type\":\"event\",\"event\":\"resumed\",\"fromIteration\":N}`\n224:    - Continue from step_read_state\n225: \n226: **Use case**: In autonomous mode, this provides the only graceful intervention mechanism short of killing the process. Users can create the sentinel file at any time to pause review between iterations.\n227: \n228: Normalization rule: if the runtime first observes a raw `paused` condition or a raw `stuck_recovery` condition, it MUST rewrite the emitted JSONL event names to `userPaused` and `stuckRecovery` before appending them. Persisted review JSONL should never contain raw `paused` or raw `stuck_recovery` rows after emission.\n229: \n230: #### Step 2b: Generate State Summary\n231: \n232: Generate a compact state summary (~200 tokens) for injection into the dispatch prompt:\n233: \n234: ```\n235: STATE SUMMARY (auto-generated, review mode):\n236: Iteration: {N} of {max} | Mode: review\n237: Target: {config.reviewTarget} ({config.reviewTargetType})\n238: Dimensions: {reviewed}/{total} complete | Next: {nextDimension}\n239: Findings: P0:{count} P1:{count} P2:{count} active\n240: Traceability: core={core_status} overlay={overlay_status}\n241: Last 2 ratios: {ratio_N-1} -> {ratio_N} | Stuck count: {stuck_count}\n242: Provisional verdict: {PASS|CONDITIONAL|FAIL|PENDING} | hasAdvisories={hasAdvisories}\n243: Next focus: {strategy.nextFocus}\n244: ```\n245: \n246: This summary is prepended to the dispatch context (Step 3) to ensure the agent has baseline context even if detailed strategy.md reading fails or is incomplete.\n247: \n248: #### Step 3: Dispatch Agent\n249: \n250: Dispatch `@deep-review` with review-specific context:\n251: \n252: ```\n253: {state_summary}  // Auto-generated (Step 2b)\n254: \n255: Review Target: {config.reviewTarget}\n256: Review Mode: {config.reviewTargetType}\n257: Iteration: {N} of {maxIterations}\n258: Focus Dimension: {strategy.nextFocus.dimension}\n259: Focus Files: {strategy.nextFocus.files}\n260: Remaining Dimensions: {strategy.remainingDimensions}\n261: Traceability Protocols:\n262:   - Core: {core_protocols}\n263:   - Overlay: {overlay_protocols}\n264: Active Findings: {findingsSummary}\n265: State Files:\n266:   - Config: {artifact_dir}/deep-review-config.json\n267:   - State: {artifact_dir}/deep-review-state.jsonl\n268:   - Registry: {artifact_dir}/deep-review-findings-registry.json\n269:   - Strategy: {artifact_dir}/deep-review-strategy.md\n270: Output: Write findings to {artifact_dir}/iterations/iteration-{NNN}.md\n271: CONSTRAINT: LEAF agent -- do NOT dispatch sub-agents\n272: CONSTRAINT: Target files are READ-ONLY -- never modify code under review\n273: ```\n274: \n275: #### Executor Resolution (spec 018 + 019)\n276: \n277: Before dispatching, the YAML resolves the executor via `parseExecutorConfig` from `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/lib/deep-loop/executor-config.ts`. The resolved `config.executor.kind` selects the dispatch branch:\n278: \n279: - `native` (spec 018): dispatch `@deep-review` agent with model Opus.\n280: - `cli-opencode` (spec 018): pipe rendered prompt via stdin to `opencode run --model X --format json --dangerously-skip-permissions --pure --dir {repo_root} [--variant Y]`. **This grants full OS-level workspace write access.** There is no `--sandbox workspace-write` flag in the live command, and `sandboxMode='read-only'` is NOT currently honored/enforced by opencode (no opencode equivalent exists). The only real containment is (a) the prompt-level \"ALLOWED WRITE PATHS\" / \"BANNED OPERATIONS\" contract rendered into the iteration prompt (`assets/prompt-pack-iteration.md.tmpl`), which relies on the model obeying instructions rather than an OS-level sandbox, and (b) post-dispatch validation (`validateIterationOutputs`) catching some violations after the fact. Because review targets are arbitrary code/spec files, they MUST be treated as potentially adversarial content (untrusted) for prompt-injection purposes — the same containment posture as untrusted fetched content in deep-research.\n281: - `cli-claude-code` (spec 019): `claude -p \"$(cat prompt)\" --model X --permission-mode acceptEdits --output-format text` with optional `--effort Y`. Default permission-mode is `plan` (read-only). We override to `acceptEdits` so iteration writes succeed. The inline workflow branches are not the complete schema: `cli-codex` is also an inline branch, while `cli-cursor`, `cli-devin`, `cli-pi`, and `cli-hermes` use the shared fan-out adapters; the executor-kind authority is `runtime/lib/deep-loop/executor-config.ts` (`EXECUTOR_KINDS`).\n282: \n283: All branches share:\n284: 1. Pre-dispatch prompt rendering via `renderPromptPack` (writes to `{artifact_dir}/prompts/iteration-{n}.md`).\n285: 2. Post-dispatch validation via `validateIterationOutputs` (asserts iteration file + JSONL delta + required fields).\n286: 3. Executor audit append via `appendExecutorAuditToLastRecord` (skipped when kind=='native').\n287: \n288: Per-kind flag-compatibility is enforced at config parse time by `EXECUTOR_KIND_FLAG_SUPPORT` in `executor-config.ts`. Setting a flag that the chosen kind does not support throws `ExecutorConfigError` before dispatch.\n289: \n290: Cross-CLI delegation (a running executor invoking other CLIs via its shell) is documented design intent. Runtime recursion detection is out of scope. See the SKILL.md Cross-CLI Delegation subsection.\n291: \n292: Two containment rules bind every CLI lineage (any `cli-*` executor kind):\n293: \n294: - **The lineage is the executor, so it works in-process.** A CLI lineage performs each iteration itself, with its own tools, inside the session the driver already started. The YAML's per-iteration executor dispatch steps are satisfied by the lineage. Spawning a second CLI process for the same iteration is a route violation, not an optimization, and on codex it also fails outright (see `cli-external-orchestration/cli-codex/SKILL.md` Rule 18).\n295: - **Write containment attributes every new dirty path outside the lineage directory to the lineage, and reports it rather than undoing it.** Attribution on a shared checkout is a guess: a tracked file changed anywhere in the same checkout during a lineage window looks identical whether the lineage, an operator, or another session wrote it. So the default remedy is preserve. The file is left byte-for-byte as it stands, the finding is recorded on the status ledger and the observability stream, and a lane that produced its review artifacts — its iteration records, and `review-report.md` where it reached synthesis — still settles as complete, carrying `completed_with_containment_advisory`. Restoring is opt-in and safe only on a checkout with a single writer; under it a path that was already dirty when the lineage started is put back to its pre-dispatch bytes, never to HEAD, and a path whose baseline could not be captured is preserved regardless. Untracked files are never deleted in either mode. One exemption still applies to attribution: another run's lineage directory under the same top-level packet is treated as that run's own write surface while its loop lock is live, so two lineages can run in sibling phases of one packet at the same time; a directory with no lock, or a stale one, is still attributed here. The runtime records each finding's diff under the lineage directory at `containment/quarantine/<iteration>[-attempt-<n>]/`, in a `containment-out-of-scope/` subdirectory on the default preserve path and `containment-reverted/` only under restore, where bytes really were rolled back; each pass keeps its own directory so a retry never overwrites an earlier pass's evidence. The pre-dispatch baseline bytes go to `<lineage dir>/containment/baseline/`, so an out-of-scope change stays recoverable either way.\n296: \n297: Failure handling remains unchanged from spec 018: `schema_mismatch` → conflict event → 3 consecutive failures → `stuck_recovery`.\n298: \n299: **Agent constraints**:\n300: - `@deep-review` is LEAF-only: it cannot dispatch sub-agents\n301: - No WebFetch: review is code-only and read-only\n302: - Target 8-11 tool calls per iteration (max 12). Breadth over depth per cycle\n303: \n304: #### Step 3a: Cross-Reference Protocol Execution\n305: \n306: During iterations focused on the Traceability dimension, the agent executes applicable cross-reference protocols. Each protocol produces a structured result appended to the JSONL `traceabilityChecks.results[]` array.\n307: \n308: | Protocol | Execution | Expected Output |\n309: |----------|-----------|-----------------|\n310: | `spec_code` | Compare normative claims in spec.md against shipped implementation | Pass/partial/fail per claim with file:line evidence |\n311: | `checklist_evidence` | Verify every `[x]` mark in checklist.md has supporting evidence | Pass/partial/fail per checked item |\n312: | `skill_agent` | Compare SKILL.md contracts against runtime agent definitions | Agreement/drift/disagreement per capability |\n313: | `agent_cross_runtime` | Compare agent definitions across runtimes (.opencode, .claude, .opencode) | Parity/drift/divergence per runtime pair |\n314: | `feature_catalog_code` | Compare catalog claims against discoverable implementation | Match/stale/missing per feature |\n315: | `playbook_capability` | Validate playbook scenarios against executable reality | Executable/needs-update/impossible per scenario |\n316: \n317: Each protocol result includes:\n318: - `protocolId`: Protocol identifier\n319: - `status`: `pass`, `partial`, or `fail`\n320: - `gateClass`: `hard` or `advisory`\n321: - `applicable`: Whether the protocol applies to this target type\n322: - `counts`: `{ pass, partial, fail, skipped }`\n323: - `evidence`: Array of file:line citations\n324: - `findingRefs`: Array of finding IDs generated from this protocol\n325: - `summary`: Human-readable summary text\n326: \n327: #### Step 3b: Resource Map Coverage Audit\n328: \n329: When `config.resource_map_present == true`, at least one loop iteration MUST audit implementation-vs-resource-map coverage:\n330: \n331: 1. Read `{spec_folder}/resource-map.md`\n332: 2. Read applied reports under `{spec_folder}/applied/T-*.md`\n333: 3. Cross-check `target_files` from the applied reports against resource-map entries\n334: 4. Classify outcomes into:\n335:    - entries touched\n336:    - entries not touched (`expected-by-scope` vs `gap`)\n337:    - implementation paths absent from the map\n338: 5. Emit any resulting findings with category `resource-map-coverage`\n339: \n340: When `config.resource_map_present == false`, skip this audit and rely on the `Known Context` note: `resource-map.md not present. Skipping coverage gate`.\n341: \n342: #### Step 4: Evaluate Results\n343: \n344: After agent completes:\n345: \n346: 1. Verify `{artifact_dir}/iterations/iteration-{NNN}.md` was created\n347: 2. Verify JSONL was appended with review iteration fields:\n348:    - `dimensions` (array of dimensions covered)\n349:    - `filesReviewed` (array of file paths)\n350:    - `findingsSummary` (cumulative P0/P1/P2 counts)\n351:    - `findingsNew` (this iteration's new findings)\n352:    - `traceabilityChecks` (protocol results if applicable)\n353: 3. Verify strategy.md was updated (dimension progress, findings count, protocol status)\n354: 4. Extract `newFindingsRatio` from JSONL record\n355: 5. Update stuck tracking using `noProgressThreshold = 0.05`:\n356:    - Skip if `status == \"thought\"` (no change)\n357:    - Reset to 0 if `status == \"insight\"` (breakthrough counts as progress)\n358:    - Increment if `newFindingsRatio < noProgressThreshold`\n359:    - Reset otherwise\n360: \n361: #### Step 4a: Claim Adjudication\n362: \n363: Before the next convergence pass, the orchestrator adjudicates every new P0/P1 finding with a **typed claim-adjudication packet**. This step prevents false positives from inflating severity and distorting convergence, AND acts as a hard STOP gate: `step_post_iteration_claim_adjudication` appends a `claim_adjudication` event to `deep-review-state.jsonl`, and the next iteration's `step_check_convergence` legal-stop decision tree consults that event via `claimAdjudicationGate` (gate `f`). A missing or failing packet vetoes STOP even when every other gate passes.\n364: \n365: Each new P0/P1 must carry a typed packet with the following required fields (see `../state/state-format.md` §9 for the full schema and a worked example):\n366: \n367: | Field | Type | Description |\n368: |-------|------|-------------|\n369: | `findingId` | string | Matches the finding ID in the iteration body |\n370: | `claim` | string | The single assertion the finding makes |\n371: | `evidenceRefs` | string[] | `file:line` or `file:range` citations (≥ 1) |\n372: | `counterevidenceSought` | string | Where the orchestrator looked for contradicting evidence |\n373: | `alternativeExplanation` | string | An alternative explanation, even if rejected |\n374: | `finalSeverity` | `\"P0\"` \\| `\"P1\"` \\| `\"P2\"` | Severity after adjudication |\n375: | `confidence` | number `[0, 1]` | Orchestrator confidence in `finalSeverity` |\n376: | `downgradeTrigger` | string | Concrete condition that would cause a future downgrade |\n377: | `transitions` | object[] | Optional, required when `finalSeverity` differs from the originally asserted severity |\n378: \n379: **Protocol**:\n380: \n381: 1. Re-read the cited evidence at the referenced file:line locations.\n382: 2. Seek counterevidence in adjacent code, docs, or prior iteration history.\n383: 3. Record an alternative explanation even if it is rejected.\n384: 4. Confirm or downgrade severity before the finding becomes convergence-visible.\n385: 5. Emit the typed packet inside the iteration file so `step_post_iteration_claim_adjudication` can parse it.\n386: \n387: **Failure semantics**: when any new P0/P1 finding is missing a packet or a required field, the workflow records `{\"event\":\"claim_adjudication\",\"passed\":false,\"missingPackets\":[...]}` in `deep-review-state.jsonl`. On the next loop, `step_check_convergence` step 0 (universal pre-check) routes STOP to `BLOCKED` with `blockedBy: [\"claimAdjudicationGate\"]` until a follow-up iteration rewrites the packet. Downgraded findings have their `finalSeverity` updated. The original severity is preserved in the iteration file for audit trail.\n388: \n389: #### Step 4b: Generate Dashboard\n390: \n391: Generate `{artifact_dir}/deep-review-dashboard.md` with review-specific sections:\n392: \n393: | Section | Content |\n394: |---------|---------|\n395: | Status | Provisional verdict (`PASS`/`CONDITIONAL`/`FAIL`/`PENDING`) and `hasAdvisories` flag |\n396: | Findings Summary | P0/P1/P2 counts with deltas from previous iteration |\n397: | Progress Table | Iteration number, focus dimension, `newFindingsRatio`, findings count, status |\n398: | Coverage | Files reviewed, dimensions completed, traceability protocol status |\n399: | Trend | Last 3 `newFindingsRatio` values with direction indicator (ascending/descending/flat) |\n400: | Active Risks | Guard violations, stuck count, budget warnings |\n401: \n402: Dashboard behavior:\n403: - Auto-generated only -- never manually edited\n404: - Overwritten each iteration (not appended)\n405: - Non-blocking: if generation fails, log a warning and continue the loop\n406: - In **confirm mode**: displayed to the user at each iteration approval gate\n407: - In **auto mode**: written silently for post-hoc review\n408: \n409: #### Step 5: Loop Decision\n410: \n411: - If convergence check returns STOP: exit to synthesis\n412: - If STUCK_RECOVERY: apply review-specific recovery strategies (see Section 3a), reset stuck count, continue\n413: - Otherwise: increment iteration counter, go to Step 1\n414: \n415: ### Stuck Recovery (Review-Specific)\n416: \n417: **Threshold:** 2 consecutive no-progress iterations (`noProgressThreshold = 0.05`).\n418: \n419: | Failure Mode | Detection | Recovery Strategy |\n420: |-------------|-----------|-------------------|\n421: | Same dimension stuck | Last 2 iterations reviewed the same dimension with ratios `< 0.05` | **Change granularity**: if reviewing at file level, zoom into functions, if reviewing functions, zoom out to module level |\n422: | Traceability plateau | Required protocols remain partial while ratios stay `< 0.05` | **Protocol-first replay**: re-run the unresolved traceability protocol directly against the conflicting artifacts |\n423: | Low-value advisory churn | Last 2 iterations found only P2 work | **Escalate severity review**: explicitly search for P0/P1 patterns or downgrade unsupported severity claims |\n424: \n425: **Selection logic**:\n426: \n427: ```\n428: function selectReviewRecoveryStrategy(stuckIterations, state, config):\n429:   lastFocuses = [i.focus for i in stuckIterations[-2:]]\n430: \n431:   // Same dimension stuck? Change granularity\n432:   if len(set(lastFocuses)) <= 1:\n433:     return { strategy: \"change_granularity\", dimension: lastFocuses[0] }\n434: \n435:   // Required protocol plateau?\n436:   if hasRequiredProtocolPlateau(state.traceabilityChecks):\n437:     return { strategy: \"protocol_first_replay\" }\n438: \n439:   // Default: escalate severity\n440:   return { strategy: \"escalate_severity_review\" }\n441: ```\n442: \n443: **Recovery evaluation**:\n444: - If recovery iteration finds any new P0/P1 or materially advances required traceability coverage: recovery successful. Reset stuck count. Continue.\n445: - If recovery iteration finds only P2 or nothing: recovery failed. If all dimensions are already covered, exit to synthesis. Otherwise, move to the next unreviewed dimension.\n446: \n447: ---\n448: \n449: ## 4. PHASE 3: SYNTHESIS\n450: \n451: ### Purpose\n452: \n453: Compile all iteration findings into the final `{artifact_dir}/review-report.md`. The synthesis phase owns the canonical review report output.\n454: \n455: ### Steps\n456: \n457: #### Step 1: Finding Registry Deduplication\n458: \n459: Consolidate findings across all iterations:\n460: \n461: 1. Read all iteration files: `{artifact_dir}/iterations/iteration-*.md`\n462: 2. Group findings by file + line range + root cause\n463: 3. Merge duplicates, keeping the highest severity and all evidence\n464: 4. Assign final finding IDs: `F001`, `F002`, ...\n465: 5. Preserve the audit trail: note which iterations contributed to each merged finding\n466: \n467: #### Step 2: Severity Reconciliation\n468: \n469: Use adjudicated `finalSeverity` for any P0/P1 that was downgraded during claim adjudication (Step 4a of the iteration loop). The original severity from the iteration file is preserved in the audit appendix.\n470: \n471: #### Step 3: Replay Validation\n472: \n473: Recompute the convergence outcome from JSONL state before finalizing the report:\n474: \n475: 1. Read `deep-review-state.jsonl` and select review-mode iteration records in run order\n476: 2. Recompute `newFindingsRatio`, rolling-average votes, MAD votes, and coverage votes from stored JSONL fields only\n477: 3. Recompute `traceabilityChecks.summary` and confirm required protocol statuses match the recorded coverage vote\n478: 4. Re-run the evidence, scope, and coverage gates against stored findings and scope data\n479: 5. Compare the replayed decision and stop reason to the recorded synthesis event\n480: \n481: This synthesis-stage replay is a **full-history audit replay** over the stored JSONL state. It follows the same full-history replay discipline as the per-iteration reducer refresh above, but runs as an explicit pre-synthesis audit.\n482: \n483: Replay passes only when the recomputed decision, thresholds, and gate outcomes agree with the persisted result. If replay fails, log a warning in the audit appendix but proceed with synthesis.\n484: \n485: #### Step 4: Compile review-report.md\n486: \n487: Generate the 9 core review-report sections (defined in `review-mode-contract.yaml` under `outputs.reviewReport`) and insert `## Resource Map Coverage Gate` between `Traceability Status` and `Deferred Items` when `config.resource_map_present == true`:\n488: \n489: | # | Section | Purpose |\n490: |---|---------|---------|\n491: | 1 | Executive Summary | Verdict, active P0/P1/P2 counts, scope, `hasAdvisories` metadata |\n492: | 2 | Planning Trigger | Why the verdict routes to planning or changelog follow-up |\n493: | 3 | Active Finding Registry | Deduplicated active findings with evidence and final severity |\n494: | 4 | Remediation Workstreams | Grouped action lanes derived from active findings |\n495: | 5 | Spec Seed | Minimal spec delta derived from review results |\n496: | 6 | Plan Seed | Action-ready plan starter for remediation |\n497: | 7 | Traceability Status | Core vs overlay protocol status and unresolved gaps |\n498: | 8 | Resource Map Coverage Gate | Present only when `config.resource_map_present == true`, reports touched entries, untouched entries (`expected-by-scope` vs `gap`), and implementation paths absent from the map |\n499: | 9 | Deferred Items | P2 advisories, blocked checks, and follow-up items |\n500: | 10 | Audit Appendix | Coverage, replay validation, convergence evidence |\n501: \n502: #### Step 5: Verdict Determination\n503: \n504: Apply the verdict contract from `review-mode-contract.yaml`:\n505: \n506: | Verdict | Condition | Next Command |\n507: |---------|-----------|--------------|\n508: | **FAIL** | Active P0 remains OR any required binary gate fails | `/speckit:plan` for remediation |\n509: | **CONDITIONAL** | No active P0, but active P1 remains | `/speckit:plan` for fixes |\n510: | **PASS** | No active P0/P1 | `/create:changelog` |\n511: \n512: When `PASS` verdict is issued and active P2 findings remain, set `hasAdvisories = true` in the report metadata.\n513: \n514: #### Step 6: Finalize State\n515: \n516: 1. Update config status: Set `status: \"complete\"` in `deep-review-config.json`\n517: 2. Write final JSONL entry:\n518:    ```json\n519:    {\n520:      \"type\": \"event\",\n521:      \"event\": \"synthesis_complete\",\n522:      \"mode\": \"review\",\n523:      \"totalIterations\": N,\n524:      \"verdict\": \"PASS|CONDITIONAL|FAIL\",\n525:      \"activeP0\": N,\n526:      \"activeP1\": N,\n527:      \"activeP2\": N,\n528:      \"dimensionCoverage\": X,\n529:      \"stopReason\": \"...\",\n530:      \"timestamp\": \"...\"\n531:    }\n532:    ```\n533: \n534: ---\n535: \n536: ## 5. PHASE 4: SAVE\n537: \n538: ### Purpose\n539: \n540: Preserve review context to the memory system for future session recovery and cross-session continuity.\n541: \n542: ### Steps\n543: \n544: 1. **Compose context payload**: Create a structured JSON payload with the review summary, active findings, verdict, next action, and relevant artifacts. Use `specFolder` for the target packet and include curated `observations`, `recent_context`, `toolCalls`, and `exchanges` when available.\n545: \n546: 2. **Generate context**: Run the memory save script with structured JSON via `--stdin`, `--json`, or a session-scoped JSON file:\n547:    ```bash\n548:    node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/continuity/generate-context.js --json '{\"specFolder\":\"{spec_folder}\",\"sessionSummary\":\"Deep review completed; see review/review-report.md for verdict and findings.\"}' {spec_folder}\n549:    ```\n550: \n551: 3. **No extra indexing step in the live contract**: `generate-context.js` is the supported save boundary for this workflow.\n552: \n553: 4. **Verify**: Confirm the routed canonical spec document carries the review continuity update. Expected targets are `implementation-summary.md`, `decision-record.md`, or `handover.md`; standalone `memory/*.md` files are retired and should not be created.\n554: \n555: ### On Save Failure\n556: \n557: If memory save fails:\n558: - Preserve the current `review/` packet as backup (the disk state is the ground truth)\n559: - Log the error\n560: - The review results remain intact in `{artifact_dir}/review-report.md` regardless of memory save status\n561: \n562: ---\n563: \n564: ## 6. AUTO-RESUME PROTOCOL\n565: \n566: ### Purpose\n567: \n568: Enable review sessions to resume cleanly from prior state when interrupted by context compaction, process termination, or intentional pause.\n569: \n570: ### Resume Detection\n571: \n572: If state files already exist from a prior session:\n573: \n574: 1. **Verify agreement**: Confirm config, JSONL, findings registry, and strategy all exist and agree on target/spec folder\n575: 2. **Count progress**: Read JSONL, count iteration records, determine last completed iteration\n576: 3. **Read strategy**: Load current dimension progress, findings, and next focus from `deep-review-strategy.md`\n577: 4. **Set counter**: Set iteration counter to last completed + 1\n578: 5. **Log resume**: Append the canonical resume event to `deep-review-state.jsonl`. The reducer requires every field below:\n579:    ```json\n580:    {\"type\":\"event\",\"event\":\"resumed\",\"mode\":\"review\",\"sessionId\":\"rvw-2026-03-24T10-00-00Z\",\"parentSessionId\":\"rvw-2026-03-24T10-00-00Z\",\"lineageMode\":\"resume\",\"continuedFromRun\":4,\"generation\":1,\"archivedPath\":null,\"timestamp\":\"2026-03-24T14:05:00Z\"}\n581:    ```\n582: 6. **Continue**: Enter the iteration loop from step_read_state\n583: \n584: ### Lifecycle Branches (current release)\n585: \n586: The runtime supports three lineage modes today. `fork` and `completed-continue` were described in earlier drafts but have no workflow wiring in this release, so they MUST NOT be exposed to operators. If the long-form lineage feature is picked up later it will arrive with first-class event emission, reducer ancestry handling, and replay fixtures. Until then treat the contract below as canonical.\n587: \n588: | Mode | Session id | Generation | Archive | JSONL event | When to pick |\n589: |------|-----------|-----------|---------|-------------|--------------|\n590: | `new` | fresh | 1 | n/a | implicit (config record) | no existing state |\n591: | `resume` | same | same | none | `resumed` (see example above) | operator wants to continue the current lineage boundary |\n592: | `restart` | fresh | `prior + 1` | prior `review/` tree moved under `review-archive/{timestamp}/` | `restarted` (same field set plus non-null `archivedPath`) | operator wants to clear the workspace and replay with a new angle |\n593: \n594: **Contract for every persisted lifecycle event**:\n595: \n596: ```json\n597: {\n598:   \"type\": \"event\",\n599:   \"event\": \"resumed | restarted\",\n600:   \"mode\": \"review\",\n601:   \"sessionId\": \"<session id of the new or continuing lineage>\",\n602:   \"parentSessionId\": \"<session id of the prior lineage (equals sessionId on resume)>\",\n603:   \"lineageMode\": \"resume | restart\",\n604:   \"generation\": <number>,\n605:   \"continuedFromRun\": <number or null>,\n606:   \"archivedPath\": \"<path or null>\",\n607:   \"timestamp\": \"<ISO 8601>\"\n608: }\n609: ```\n610: \n611: Every field in the contract MUST be present on every persisted lifecycle event. `archivedPath` is null for `resumed` and set to the archive destination for `restarted`. `continuedFromRun` is the number of completed iteration records before the lifecycle boundary. Reducer parity tests ensure the dashboard `Lifecycle` section reads exactly those fields.\n612: \n613: ### State Validation on Resume\n614: \n615: | Condition | Classification | Action |\n616: |-----------|---------------|--------|\n617: | Config + JSONL + strategy all exist and agree | `resume` | Continue from last iteration + 1 |\n618: | All exist, config says `status: \"complete\"` | `completed-session` | Report completion, ask user if re-review desired |\n619: | Partial files or contradictions | `invalid-state` | Halt for repair, do not guess |\n620: | No files exist | `fresh` | Start new initialization |\n621: \n622: ### State Recovery Cascade\n623: \n624: When state files are missing or corrupted, apply this priority cascade:\n625: \n626: | Priority | Condition | Recovery Action |\n627: |----------|-----------|----------------|\n628: | 1 | JSONL exists and parseable | Normal operation |\n629: | 2 | JSONL exists, strategy.md missing | Reconstruct strategy from JSONL: extract dimensions, findings, coverage |\n630: | 3 | JSONL missing, iteration files exist | Scan `review/iterations/iteration-*.md`, reconstruct JSONL with `status: \"reconstructed\"` |\n631: | 4 | Only config.json remains | Restart from initialization phase |\n632: | 5 | Config.json also missing | Cannot recover. Report error and halt. |\n633: \n634: Each priority level is attempted in order. If a level fails, fall through to the next.\n635: \n636: ---\n637: \n638: ## 7. PAUSE/RESUME\n639: \n640: ### Pause Sentinel Behavior\n641: \n642: The pause sentinel provides graceful loop suspension in autonomous mode without killing the process.\n643: \n644: ### Creating a Pause\n645: \n646: Create the sentinel file at any time:\n647: \n648: ```bash\n649: touch {artifact_dir}/.deep-review-pause\n650: ```\n651: \n652: The orchestrator checks for this file between iterations (Step 2a). When detected:\n653: - The current iteration is allowed to complete\n654: - The loop halts before dispatching the next iteration\n655: - A paused event is logged to JSONL\n656: - The user is notified with current state (iteration count, dimensions covered, findings)\n657: \n658: ### Resuming After Pause\n659: \n660: Delete the sentinel file and restart the workflow:\n661: \n662: ```bash\n663: rm {artifact_dir}/.deep-review-pause\n664: ```\n665: \n666: On the next invocation:\n667: - The auto-resume protocol (Section 6) detects the existing state\n668: - A resumed event is logged to JSONL\n669: - The loop continues from the next iteration\n670: \n671: ### Confirm Mode Approval Gates\n672: \n673: In confirm mode (`deep-review-confirm.yaml`), the workflow adds explicit approval gates instead of relying on the pause sentinel:\n674: \n675: | Gate Location | Behavior |\n676: |---------------|----------|\n677: | After initialization | Show config, strategy, and scope. Wait for approval |\n678: | After each iteration | Show iteration findings, dashboard, and convergence status. Options: Continue, Adjust Focus, Stop |\n679: | Before synthesis | Show summary of all iterations. Wait for approval to synthesize |\n680: | After synthesis | Show final review-report.md summary. Approve or request revisions |\n681: \n682: ---\n683: \n684: ## 8. STATE TRANSITIONS, ERROR HANDLING, AND QUALITY GATES\n685: \n686: State-transition rules, error handling, and the review quality gates live in `loop-state-and-gates.md` for navigability.\n687: \n688: ---\n689: \n690: ## 11. RELATED RESOURCES\n691: \n692: ### Shared Protocol Documentation\n693: \n694: Protocol documents from `deep-research` are cross-referenced, not duplicated:\n695: \n696: | Document | Path | Purpose |\n697: |----------|------|---------|\n698: | Loop Protocol (research) | `../deep-research/references/protocol/loop-protocol.md` | Research loop lifecycle (this file adapts Section 9) |\n699: | State Format | `../deep-research/references/state/state-format.md` | JSONL, strategy, and config schemas |\n700: | Convergence | `../deep-research/references/convergence/convergence.md` | `shouldContinue_review()` (Section 10), stuck recovery, quality guards |\n701: \n702: ### Review-Specific Resources\n703: \n704: | Resource | Path | Purpose |\n705: |----------|------|---------|\n706: | Review Contract | `assets/review-mode-contract.yaml` | Single source of truth for dimensions, verdicts, gates, protocols |\n707: | Quick Reference | `references/protocol/quick-reference.md` | One-page review cheat sheet |\n708: | Strategy Template | `assets/deep-review-strategy.md` | Template for review strategy files |\n709: | Config Template | `assets/deep-review-config.json` | Template for review config files |\n710: | Dashboard Template | `assets/deep-review-dashboard.md` | Template for auto-generated dashboard |\n711: \n712: ### Workflow Files\n713: \n714: | Mode | Path |\n715: |------|------|\n716: | Auto (unattended) | `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-review-auto.yaml` |\n717: | Confirm (step-by-step) | `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-review-confirm.yaml` |\n718: \n719: ### Agent\n720: \n721: | Agent | Path Pattern | Constraint |\n722: |-------|-------------|------------|\n723: | `@deep-review` | `{runtime_dir}/deep-review.{ext}` | LEAF-only, no sub-agents, no WebFetch, target files read-only |\n724: \n725: Runtime paths:\n726: - OpenCode / Copilot: `.opencode/agents/deep-review.md`\n727: - Claude: `.claude/agents/deep-review.md`\n728: \n729: ---\n730: \n731: ## Executor Selection Contract\n732: \n733: Executor settings are owned by the YAML workflow and rendered prompt pack. Do not bypass the workflow by hand-dispatching review iterations; each iteration stays LEAF-only and produces the required markdown plus JSONL delta.\n734: \n735: ### Invariants\n736: \n737: Every executor path MUST satisfy:\n738: \n739: 1. Produce an iteration markdown file at `{state_paths.iteration_pattern}` (non-empty).\n740: 2. Append a JSONL delta record to `{state_paths.state_log}` with required fields: `type`, `iteration`, `dimensions`, `filesReviewed`, `findingsSummary`, `findingsNew`, `findingDetails`, `newFindingsRatio`.\n741: 3. Respect the LEAF-agent constraint: no sub-dispatch, no nested loops. Max 12 tool calls per iteration.\n742: \n743: ### Failure Modes\n744: \n745: - Missing iteration file: `iteration_file_missing` from `post-dispatch-validate.ts`, emits a `schema_mismatch` conflict event.\n746: - Empty iteration file: `iteration_file_empty`, same downstream.\n747: - JSONL not appended: `jsonl_not_appended`.\n748: - JSONL missing required fields: `jsonl_missing_fields`.\n749: - JSONL malformed: `jsonl_parse_error`.\n750: - 3 consecutive failures: existing `stuck_recovery` event (unchanged).\n751: \n752: ### JSONL Audit Field\n753: \n754: Non-native executor runs append an `executor` block with model and runtime settings to the iteration's JSONL record via `executor-audit.ts`. Native runs are recoverable from YAML alone.\n755: \n756: ### Template And Config Surface\n757: \n758: The executor-agnostic iteration prompt lives at `assets/prompt-pack-iteration.md.tmpl`, rendered by `prompt-pack.ts` before dispatch and either injected as the agent's context (native) or piped to `opencode run` stdin (cli-opencode). Config is in `assets/deep-review-config.json` under the `executor` key; schema in `runtime//lib/deep-loop/executor-config.ts`. CLI flag precedence: `--executor/--model/--reasoning-effort/--service-tier/--executor-timeout > config file > schema default`.\n759: \n760: ### What Never Changes Regardless Of Executor Route\n761: \n762: - YAML owns state (`deep-review-state.jsonl`, strategy.md, registry, dashboard).\n763: - `reduce-state.cjs` is the single state writer.\n764: - Convergence detection, lifecycle events (new/resume/restart), the review dimensions pass, and stuck_recovery stay YAML-driven.\n765: - The `@deep-review` LEAF agent definition is untouched: it is the native executor, not the only one.\n766: \n767: ### Code-Graph Readiness TrustState Surface\n768: \n769: On this skill surface, the live code-graph readiness contract only reaches four TrustState values: `live`, `stale`, `absent`, and `unavailable`. The values `cached`, `imported`, `rebuilt`, and `rehomed` remain declared in the shared TrustState type for compatibility and downstream schema stability, but the seven code-graph handlers and readiness helpers used here do not emit them today.\n\n(End of file - total 769 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"---\ntitle: Deep Review Loop Protocol\ndescription: Complete lifecycle specification for the autonomous deep review loop, initialization, iteration, convergence, synthesis, and save phases.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"deep review loop protocol\"\n  - \"review iteration lifecycle\"\n  - \"review loop initialization\"\n  - \"review auto resume\"\n  - \"review synthesis phase\"\nimportance_tier: important\ncontextType: implementation\nversion: 1.11.0.29\n---\n\n# Deep Review Loop Protocol\n\nComplete lifecycle specification for the autonomous deep review loop.\n\n---\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.worktrees/090-deep-review-okf-adoption/.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/references/protocol/loop-protocol.md","text":"---\ntitle: Deep Review Loop Protocol\ndescription: Complete lifecycle specification for the autonomous deep review loop, initialization, iteration, convergence, synthesis, and save phases.\ntrigger_phrases:\n  - \"deep review loop protocol\"\n  - \"review iteration lifecycle\"\n  - \"review loop initialization\"\n  - \"review auto resume\"\n  - \"review synthesis phase\"\nimportance_tier: important\ncontextType: implementation\nversion: 1.11.0.29\n---\n\n# Deep Review Loop Protocol\n\nComplete lifecycle specification for the autonomous deep review loop.\n\n---\n\n## 1. OVERVIEW\n\n### Purpose\n\nThe deep review loop has 4 phases: initialization, iteration (repeated), synthesis, and save. The YAML workflow manages the lifecycle. The `@deep-review` agent (LEAF-only, no WebFetch) executes individual review iterations with fresh context each time.\n\n### When to Use\n\n- Initiating a deep-review loop on a spec folder, skill, agent, or track.\n- Resuming a paused or interrupted review session from prior state.\n- Validating the 4-phase lifecycle and lineage protocol against current behavior.\n- Auditing iteration-dispatch, convergence, or save-phase contracts before changes.\n\n```\n┌──────────────┐     ┌───────────────────────────────┐     ┌───────────────┐     ┌──────────┐\n│  INIT        │────>│  LOOP                         │────>│  SYNTHESIS    │────>│  SAVE    │\n│              │     │  ┌─────────────────────────┐  │     │               │     │          │\n│ Scope        │     │  │ Read State              │  │     │ Finding       │     │ Memory   │\n│ Discovery    │     │  │ Check Convergence       │  │     │ Registry      │     │ Context  │\n│ Dimension    │     │  │ Check Pause Sentinel    │  │     │ Dedup         │     │ Save     │\n│ Ordering     │     │  │ Generate State Summary  │  │     │               │     │          │\n│ Traceability │     │  │ Dispatch @deep-review   │  │     │ Severity      │     │          │\n│ Planning     │     │  │ Evaluate Results        │  │     │ Reconcile     │     │          │\n│ State Files  │     │  │ Claim Adjudication      │  │     │               │     │          │\n│              │     │  │ Dashboard Generation    │  │     │ Replay        │     │          │\n│              │     │  │ Loop Decision           │  │     │ Validation    │     │          │\n│              │     │  └────────┬────────────────┘  │     │               │     │          │\n│              │     │           │ repeat            │     │ review-report │     │          │\n│              │     │           │                   │     │ (9 core +     │     │          │\n│              │     │           │                   │     │  resource-map)│     │          │\n└──────────────┘     └───────────────────────────────┘     └───────────────┘     └──────────┘\n```\n\n### Core Innovation\n\nEach agent dispatch gets a fresh context window. State continuity comes from files on disk, not in-context memory. This eliminates context degradation in long review sessions where accumulated findings would otherwise bias subsequent dimensions.\n\n### Key References\n\n| Resource | Path | Purpose |\n|----------|------|---------|\n| Review contract | `assets/review-mode-contract.yaml` | Dimensions, verdicts, gates, protocols |\n| Auto workflow | `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-review-auto.yaml` | Unattended review loop |\n| Confirm workflow | `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-review-confirm.yaml` | Step-by-step review with approval gates |\n| Agent | `@deep-review` (LEAF) | Single iteration executor, no sub-agents, no WebFetch |\n| Memory save | `generate-context.js` | Context preservation script |\n\n---\n\n## 2. PHASE 1: INITIALIZATION\n\n### Purpose\n\nSet up all state files for a new review session. Discover the scope, order dimensions by risk priority, establish the traceability protocol plan, and create the review state packet.\n\n### Steps\n\n1. **Classify session state before writing**:\n   - `fresh`: no config/state/strategy files exist\n   - `resume`: config + state + strategy all exist and agree\n   - `completed-session`: consistent prior state with `config.status == \"complete\"`\n   - `invalid-state`: partial or contradictory artifacts\n\n2. **Resolve local artifact owner**: `artifact_dir = resolveArtifactRoot(specFolder, 'review').artifactDir`, then `mkdir -p {artifact_dir}/iterations`\n\n3. **Scope discovery**: Resolve the review target into a concrete file list using one of 5 target types:\n\n   | Target Type | CLI Value | Resolution Strategy |\n   |-------------|-----------|---------------------|\n   | **Spec Folder** | `spec-folder` | Read `spec.md`, `plan.md`, implementation files listed in `tasks.md` |\n   | **Skill** | `skill` | Read `SKILL.md`, `references/`, `assets/`, `scripts/`, find agent definitions and command entry points |\n   | **Agent** | `agent` | Find agent definitions across all runtimes, compare for consistency |\n   | **Track** | `track` | List all child spec folders under the track, read `spec.md` + `checklist.md` for each |\n   | **Files** | `files` | Expand glob patterns, validate existence, discover cross-references |\n\n   Store the resolved file list in strategy.md \"Files Under Review\".\n\n4. **Dimension ordering**: Order the 4 review dimensions for iteration based on risk priority:\n\n   | Priority | Dimension | Rationale |\n   |----------|-----------|-----------|\n   | 1 | Correctness | Logic errors have the highest blast radius |\n   | 2 | Security | Vulnerabilities require immediate attention |\n   | 3 | Traceability | Spec/code alignment ensures completeness |\n   | 4 | Maintainability | Patterns and documentation quality (lowest immediate risk) |\n\n   Default order: D1 Correctness, D2 Security, D3 Traceability, D4 Maintainability. This order is configurable but the default prioritizes highest-impact dimensions first.\n\n5. **Traceability protocol planning**: Partition the 6 cross-reference protocols into core vs overlay, and schedule only applicable overlays for the target type:\n\n   | Level | Protocol | Applies To | Gate Class | Purpose |\n   |-------|----------|------------|------------|---------|\n   | Core | `spec_code` | all targets | hard | Verify normative claims resolve to shipped behavior |\n   | Core | `checklist_evidence` | all targets | hard | Verify checked completion claims have evidence |\n   | Overlay | `skill_agent` | skill | advisory | Verify SKILL.md and runtime agents agree |\n   | Overlay | `agent_cross_runtime` | agent | advisory | Verify runtime agent parity |\n   | Overlay | `feature_catalog_code` | skill, spec-folder, track, files | advisory | Verify catalog claims match capability |\n   | Overlay | `playbook_capability` | skill, agent, spec-folder | advisory | Verify playbook scenarios match executable reality |\n\n   Only schedule overlay protocols that apply to the resolved target type.\n\n6. **Write config**: `{artifact_dir}/deep-review-config.json` with `mode: \"review\"`, lineage metadata (`sessionId`, `parentSessionId`, `lineageMode`, `generation`, `continuedFromRun`, `releaseReadinessState`), and review-specific fields including target, target type, dimensions, protocol plan, and release-readiness state.\n\n7. **Initialize state log**: First line of `{artifact_dir}/deep-review-state.jsonl` with config record including `mode: \"review\"` and the lineage fields.\n\n8. **Initialize reducer state**: Create `{artifact_dir}/deep-review-findings-registry.json` with empty `openFindings`, `resolvedFindings`, `repeatedFindings`, `dimensionCoverage`, `findingsBySeverity`, and `convergenceScore`.\n\n9. **Initialize strategy**: `{artifact_dir}/deep-review-strategy.md` from review template with:\n   - Topic (review target description)\n   - Review Dimensions checklist\n   - Files Under Review table\n   - Cross-Reference Status table grouped by core vs overlay\n   - Known Context from the continuity ladder and ripgrep recipes (if any)\n   - Resource-map snapshot when `{spec_folder}/resource-map.md` exists at init\n   - `resource-map.md not present. Skipping coverage gate` when it does not\n   - Review Boundaries from config\n9a. **Initialize resource-map coverage state**:\n   - If `{spec_folder}/resource-map.md` exists at init: set `resource_map_present = true`, add `Resource Map Coverage` to the Review Charter, and persist a concise map summary in `Known Context`\n   - If absent: set `resource_map_present = false`, record the skip note in `Known Context`, and continue without failing\n\n10. **Validate review charter**:\n   - Verify strategy.md contains Non-Goals and Stop Conditions sections (may be empty but must exist)\n   - In **confirm mode**: present the charter (target, dimensions, scope, non-goals) for user review before proceeding\n   - In **auto mode**: accept automatically and continue\n\n11. **Resume only if config, JSONL, strategy, and findings registry agree**. Otherwise halt for repair instead of guessing.\n\n### Outputs\n\n| File | Location | Purpose |\n|------|----------|---------|\n| Config | `{artifact_dir}/deep-review-config.json` | Review parameters (immutable after init) |\n| State | `{artifact_dir}/deep-review-state.jsonl` | Iteration log (1 initial line) |\n| Registry | `{artifact_dir}/deep-review-findings-registry.json` | Reducer-owned findings state |\n| Strategy | `{artifact_dir}/deep-review-strategy.md` | Dimensions, findings, next focus |\n\n---\n\n## 3. PHASE 2: ITERATION LOOP\n\n### Loop Steps (repeated until convergence)\n\n#### Step 1: Read State\n\n- Read `deep-review-state.jsonl` -- count iterations, extract `newFindingsRatio`, `findingsSummary`, `findingsNew`, `traceabilityChecks`, and lineage data\n- Read `deep-review-findings-registry.json` -- extract `dimensionsCovered`, `findingsBySeverity`, `openFindings`, `resolvedFindings`, and `convergenceScore`\n- Read `{artifact_dir}/deep-review-strategy.md` -- get next focus dimension/files, remaining dimensions, and protocol gaps\n\nReducer contract for every loop refresh:\n\n| Reducer Part | Canonical Value | Notes |\n|--------------|-----------------|-------|\n| Inputs | `fullJSONLHistory`, `iterationFiles`, `priorReducedState` | Normal loop refresh is full-history: replay the persisted JSONL state and current iteration corpus each refresh so reducer output stays deterministic across restart, compaction, and synthesis audit paths. |\n| Outputs | `findingsRegistry`, `dashboardMetrics`, `strategyUpdates` | The same refresh pass updates the canonical registry, refreshes dashboard metrics, and applies strategy updates. |\n| Metrics | `dimensionsCovered`, `findingsBySeverity`, `openFindings`, `resolvedFindings`, `convergenceScore` | These metrics drive convergence decisions, dashboard summaries, and synthesis readiness. |\n\n#### Step 2: Check Convergence\n\nRun `shouldContinue_review()` (see `../deep-research/references/convergence/convergence.md` Section 10.3):\n\n- Max iterations reached? `STOP`\n- Stuck count `>= 2` using `noProgressThreshold = 0.05`? `STUCK_RECOVERY`\n- Composite convergence votes `STOP` only after:\n  - Rolling average uses `rollingStopThreshold = 0.08`\n  - Dimension coverage reaches 100% across the 4-dimension model\n  - Coverage has aged through `minStabilizationPasses >= 1`\n  - Required traceability protocols are covered\n  - Evidence, scope, and coverage gates pass\n- Otherwise: `CONTINUE`\n\nIf convergence math or a hard-stop candidate points to STOP, the workflow must run the review legal-stop decision tree before actually stopping. That decision tree records nine review-specific gates: `convergenceGate`, `dimensionCoverageGate`, `p0ResolutionGate`, `evidenceDensityGate`, `hotspotSaturationGate`, `claimAdjudicationGate`, `fixCompletenessReplayGate`, `candidateCoverageGate`, and `graphlessFallbackGate` (see `../convergence/convergence.md` §Section-1 blocked_stop event example for the authoritative shape). The Step 4a claim-adjudication path treats `claimAdjudicationGate` as a hard STOP gate, so the count covers both the convergence decision tree and the adjudication enforcement path. `candidateCoverageGate` and `graphlessFallbackGate` are v2-rollout gates that pass trivially when the review-depth-v2 search path is inactive. If any gate fails, the loop does **not** stop. Instead it emits a first-class `blocked_stop` JSONL event with:\n\n- `blockedBy`: array of the failed review gate names\n- `gateResults`: per-gate pass/fail payloads using the review gate names above\n- `recoveryStrategy`: one-line hint describing the next review action\n\nThe blocked-stop event is append-only evidence that legal-stop blocked the run. The loop then continues with the recovery or next-focus path rather than synthesizing.\n\nConvergence signals and weights:\n\n| Signal | Weight | Description |\n|--------|--------|-------------|\n| Rolling Average | 0.30 | Last 2 severity-weighted `newFindingsRatio` values average below `rollingStopThreshold` |\n| MAD Noise Floor | 0.25 | Latest ratio within noise floor derived from historical ratios |\n| Dimension Coverage | 0.45 | All 4 dimensions plus required traceability protocols covered, with stabilization |\n\n**P0 override**: Any new P0 finding sets `newFindingsRatio >= 0.50`, blocking convergence regardless of composite score.\n\n#### Step 2a: Check Pause Sentinel\n\nBefore dispatching, check for a pause sentinel file:\n\n1. Check if `review/.deep-review-pause` exists (note: the file name uses the shared `-pause` suffix)\n2. If present:\n   - Log event to JSONL: `{\"type\":\"event\",\"event\":\"userPaused\",\"mode\":\"review\",\"stopReason\":\"userPaused\",\"reason\":\"sentinel file detected\"}`\n   - Halt the loop with message:\n     ```\n     Review paused. Delete review/.deep-review-pause to resume.\n     Current state: Iteration {N}, {reviewed}/{total} dimensions complete, {P0}/{P1}/{P2} findings.\n     ```\n   - Do NOT exit to synthesis -- the loop is suspended, not stopped\n3. On resume (file deleted and loop restarted):\n   - Log event: `{\"type\":\"event\",\"event\":\"resumed\",\"fromIteration\":N}`\n   - Continue from step_read_state\n\n**Use case**: In autonomous mode, this provides the only graceful intervention mechanism short of killing the process. Users can create the sentinel file at any time to pause review between iterations.\n\nNormalization rule: if the runtime first observes a raw `paused` condition or a raw `stuck_recovery` condition, it MUST rewrite the emitted JSONL event names to `userPaused` and `stuckRecovery` before appending them. Persisted review JSONL should never contain raw `paused` or raw `stuck_recovery` rows after emission.\n\n#### Step 2b: Generate State Summary\n\nGenerate a compact state summary (~200 tokens) for injection into the dispatch prompt:\n\n```\nSTATE SUMMARY (auto-generated, review mode):\nIteration: {N} of {max} | Mode: review\nTarget: {config.reviewTarget} ({config.reviewTargetType})\nDimensions: {reviewed}/{total} complete | Next: {nextDimension}\nFindings: P0:{count} P1:{count} P2:{count} active\nTraceability: core={core_status} overlay={overlay_status}\nLast 2 ratios: {ratio_N-1} -> {ratio_N} | Stuck count: {stuck_count}\nProvisional verdict: {PASS|CONDITIONAL|FAIL|PENDING} | hasAdvisories={hasAdvisories}\nNext focus: {strategy.nextFocus}\n```\n\nThis summary is prepended to the dispatch context (Step 3) to ensure the agent has baseline context even if detailed strategy.md reading fails or is incomplete.\n\n#### Step 3: Dispatch Agent\n\nDispatch `@deep-review` with review-specific context:\n\n```\n{state_summary}  // Auto-generated (Step 2b)\n\nReview Target: {config.reviewTarget}\nReview Mode: {config.reviewTargetType}\nIteration: {N} of {maxIterations}\nFocus Dimension: {strategy.nextFocus.dimension}\nFocus Files: {strategy.nextFocus.files}\nRemaining Dimensions: {strategy.remainingDimensions}\nTraceability Protocols:\n  - Core: {core_protocols}\n  - Overlay: {overlay_protocols}\nActive Findings: {findingsSummary}\nState Files:\n  - Config: {artifact_dir}/deep-review-config.json\n  - State: {artifact_dir}/deep-review-state.jsonl\n  - Registry: {artifact_dir}/deep-review-findings-registry.json\n  - Strategy: {artifact_dir}/deep-review-strategy.md\nOutput: Write findings to {artifact_dir}/iterations/iteration-{NNN}.md\nCONSTRAINT: LEAF agent -- do NOT dispatch sub-agents\nCONSTRAINT: Target files are READ-ONLY -- never modify code under review\n```\n\n#### Executor Resolution (spec 018 + 019)\n\nBefore dispatching, the YAML resolves the executor via `parseExecutorConfig` from `.skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/runtime/lib/deep-loop/executor-config.ts`. The resolved `config.executor.kind` selects the dispatch branch:\n\n- `native` (spec 018): dispatch `@deep-review` agent with model Opus.\n- `cli-opencode` (spec 018): pipe rendered prompt via stdin to `opencode run --model X --format json --dangerously-skip-permissions --pure --dir {repo_root} [--variant Y]`. **This grants full OS-level workspace write access.** There is no `--sandbox workspace-write` flag in the live command, and `sandboxMode='read-only'` is NOT currently honored/enforced by opencode (no opencode equivalent exists). The only real containment is (a) the prompt-level \"ALLOWED WRITE PATHS\" / \"BANNED OPERATIONS\" contract rendered into the iteration prompt (`assets/prompt-pack-iteration.md.tmpl`), which relies on the model obeying instructions rather than an OS-level sandbox, and (b) post-dispatch validation (`validateIterationOutputs`) catching some violations after the fact. Because review targets are arbitrary code/spec files, they MUST be treated as potentially adversarial content (untrusted) for prompt-injection purposes — the same containment posture as untrusted fetched content in deep-research.\n- `cli-claude-code` (spec 019): `claude -p \"$(cat prompt)\" --model X --permission-mode acceptEdits --output-format text` with optional `--effort Y`. Default permission-mode is `plan` (read-only). We override to `acceptEdits` so iteration writes succeed. The inline workflow branches are not the complete schema: `cli-codex` is also an inline branch, while `cli-cursor`, `cli-devin`, `cli-pi`, and `cli-hermes` use the shared fan-out adapters; the executor-kind authority is `runtime/lib/deep-loop/executor-config.ts` (`EXECUTOR_KINDS`).\n\nAll branches share:\n1. Pre-dispatch prompt rendering via `renderPromptPack` (writes to `{artifact_dir}/prompts/iteration-{n}.md`).\n2. Post-dispatch validation via `validateIterationOutputs` (asserts iteration file + JSONL delta + required fields).\n3. Executor audit append via `appendExecutorAuditToLastRecord` (skipped when kind=='native').\n\nPer-kind flag-compatibility is enforced at config parse time by `EXECUTOR_KIND_FLAG_SUPPORT` in `executor-config.ts`. Setting a flag that the chosen kind does not support throws `ExecutorConfigError` before dispatch.\n\nCross-CLI delegation (a running executor invoking other CLIs via its shell) is documented design intent. Runtime recursion detection is out of scope. See the SKILL.md Cross-CLI Delegation subsection.\n\nTwo containment rules bind every CLI lineage (any `cli-*` executor kind):\n\n- **The lineage is the executor, so it works in-process.** A CLI lineage performs each iteration itself, with its own tools, inside the session the driver already started. The YAML's per-iteration executor dispatch steps are satisfied by the lineage. Spawning a second CLI process for the same iteration is a route violation, not an optimization, and on codex it also fails outright (see `cli-external-orchestration/cli-codex/SKILL.md` Rule 18).\n- **Write containment attributes every new dirty path outside the lineage directory to the lineage, and reports it rather than undoing it.** Attribution on a shared checkout is a guess: a tracked file changed anywhere in the same checkout during a lineage window looks identical whether the lineage, an operator, or another session wrote it. So the default remedy is preserve. The file is left byte-for-byte as it stands, the finding is recorded on the status ledger and the observability stream, and a lane that produced its review artifacts — its iteration records, and `review-report.md` where it reached synthesis — still settles as complete, carrying `completed_with_containment_advisory`. Restoring is opt-in and safe only on a checkout with a single writer; under it a path that was already dirty when the lineage started is put back to its pre-dispatch bytes, never to HEAD, and a path whose baseline could not be captured is preserved regardless. Untracked files are never deleted in either mode. One exemption still applies to attribution: another run's lineage directory under the same top-level packet is treated as that run's own write surface while its loop lock is live, so two lineages can run in sibling phases of one packet at the same time; a directory with no lock, or a stale one, is still attributed here. The runtime records each finding's diff under the lineage directory at `containment/quarantine/<iteration>[-attempt-<n>]/`, in a `containment-out-of-scope/` subdirectory on the default preserve path and `containment-reverted/` only under restore, where bytes really were rolled back; each pass keeps its own directory so a retry never overwrites an earlier pass's evidence. The pre-dispatch baseline bytes go to `<lineage dir>/containment/baseline/`, so an out-of-scope change stays recoverable either way.\n\nFailure handling remains unchanged from spec 018: `schema_mismatch` → conflict event → 3 consecutive failures → `stuck_recovery`.\n\n**Agent constraints**:\n- `@deep-review` is LEAF-only: it cannot dispatch sub-agents\n- No WebFetch: review is code-only and read-only\n- Target 8-11 tool calls per iteration (max 12). Breadth over depth per cycle\n\n#### Step 3a: Cross-Reference Protocol Execution\n\nDuring iterations focused on the Traceability dimension, the agent executes applicable cross-reference protocols. Each protocol produces a structured result appended to the JSONL `traceabilityChecks.results[]` array.\n\n| Protocol | Execution | Expected Output |\n|----------|-----------|-----------------|\n| `spec_code` | Compare normative claims in spec.md against shipped implementation | Pass/partial/fail per claim with file:line evidence |\n| `checklist_evidence` | Verify every `[x]` mark in checklist.md has supporting evidence | Pass/partial/fail per checked item |\n| `skill_agent` | Compare SKILL.md contracts against runtime agent definitions | Agreement/drift/disagreement per capability |\n| `agent_cross_runtime` | Compare agent definitions across runtimes (.opencode, .claude, .opencode) | Parity/drift/divergence per runtime pair |\n| `feature_catalog_code` | Compare catalog claims against discoverable implementation | Match/stale/missing per feature |\n| `playbook_capability` | Validate playbook scenarios against executable reality | Executable/needs-update/impossible per scenario |\n\nEach protocol result includes:\n- `protocolId`: Protocol identifier\n- `status`: `pass`, `partial`, or `fail`\n- `gateClass`: `hard` or `advisory`\n- `applicable`: Whether the protocol applies to this target type\n- `counts`: `{ pass, partial, fail, skipped }`\n- `evidence`: Array of file:line citations\n- `findingRefs`: Array of finding IDs generated from this protocol\n- `summary`: Human-readable summary text\n\n#### Step 3b: Resource Map Coverage Audit\n\nWhen `config.resource_map_present == true`, at least one loop iteration MUST audit implementation-vs-resource-map coverage:\n\n1. Read `{spec_folder}/resource-map.md`\n2. Read applied reports under `{spec_folder}/applied/T-*.md`\n3. Cross-check `target_files` from the applied reports against resource-map entries\n4. Classify outcomes into:\n   - entries touched\n   - entries not touched (`expected-by-scope` vs `gap`)\n   - implementation paths absent from the map\n5. Emit any resulting findings with category `resource-map-coverage`\n\nWhen `config.resource_map_present == false`, skip this audit and rely on the `Known Context` note: `resource-map.md not present. Skipping coverage gate`.\n\n#### Step 4: Evaluate Results\n\nAfter agent completes:\n\n1. Verify `{artifact_dir}/iterations/iteration-{NNN}.md` was created\n2. Verify JSONL was appended with review iteration fields:\n   - `dimensions` (array of dimensions covered)\n   - `filesReviewed` (array of file paths)\n   - `findingsSummary` (cumulative P0/P1/P2 counts)\n   - `findingsNew` (this iteration's new findings)\n   - `traceabilityChecks` (protocol results if applicable)\n3. Verify strategy.md was updated (dimension progress, findings count, protocol status)\n4. Extract `newFindingsRatio` from JSONL record\n5. Update stuck tracking using `noProgressThreshold = 0.05`:\n   - Skip if `status == \"thought\"` (no change)\n   - Reset to 0 if `status == \"insight\"` (breakthrough counts as progress)\n   - Increment if `newFindingsRatio < noProgressThreshold`\n   - Reset otherwise\n\n#### Step 4a: Claim Adjudication\n\nBefore the next convergence pass, the orchestrator adjudicates every new P0/P1 finding with a **typed claim-adjudication packet**. This step prevents false positives from inflating severity and distorting convergence, AND acts as a hard STOP gate: `step_post_iteration_claim_adjudication` appends a `claim_adjudication` event to `deep-review-state.jsonl`, and the next iteration's `step_check_convergence` legal-stop decision tree consults that event via `claimAdjudicationGate` (gate `f`). A missing or failing packet vetoes STOP even when every other gate passes.\n\nEach new P0/P1 must carry a typed packet with the following required fields (see `../state/state-format.md` §9 for the full schema and a worked example):\n\n| Field | Type | Description |\n|-------|------|-------------|\n| `findingId` | string | Matches the finding ID in the iteration body |\n| `claim` | string | The single assertion the finding makes |\n| `evidenceRefs` | string[] | `file:line` or `file:range` citations (≥ 1) |\n| `counterevidenceSought` | string | Where the orchestrator looked for contradicting evidence |\n| `alternativeExplanation` | string | An alternative explanation, even if rejected |\n| `finalSeverity` | `\"P0\"` \\| `\"P1\"` \\| `\"P2\"` | Severity after adjudication |\n| `confidence` | number `[0, 1]` | Orchestrator confidence in `finalSeverity` |\n| `downgradeTrigger` | string | Concrete condition that would cause a future downgrade |\n| `transitions` | object[] | Optional, required when `finalSeverity` differs from the originally asserted severity |\n\n**Protocol**:\n\n1. Re-read the cited evidence at the referenced file:line locations.\n2. Seek counterevidence in adjacent code, docs, or prior iteration history.\n3. Record an alternative explanation even if it is rejected.\n4. Confirm or downgrade severity before the finding becomes convergence-visible.\n5. Emit the typed packet inside the iteration file so `step_post_iteration_claim_adjudication` can parse it.\n\n**Failure semantics**: when any new P0/P1 finding is missing a packet or a required field, the workflow records `{\"event\":\"claim_adjudication\",\"passed\":false,\"missingPackets\":[...]}` in `deep-review-state.jsonl`. On the next loop, `step_check_convergence` step 0 (universal pre-check) routes STOP to `BLOCKED` with `blockedBy: [\"claimAdjudicationGate\"]` until a follow-up iteration rewrites the packet. Downgraded findings have their `finalSeverity` updated. The original severity is preserved in the iteration file for audit trail.\n\n#### Step 4b: Generate Dashboard\n\nGenerate `{artifact_dir}/deep-review-dashboard.md` with review-specific sections:\n\n| Section | Content |\n|---------|---------|\n| Status | Provisional verdict (`PASS`/`CONDITIONAL`/`FAIL`/`PENDING`) and `hasAdvisories` flag |\n| Findings Summary | P0/P1/P2 counts with deltas from previous iteration |\n| Progress Table | Iteration number, focus dimension, `newFindingsRatio`, findings count, status |\n| Coverage | Files reviewed, dimensions completed, traceability protocol status |\n| Trend | Last 3 `newFindingsRatio` values with direction indicator (ascending/descending/flat) |\n| Active Risks | Guard violations, stuck count, budget warnings |\n\nDashboard behavior:\n- Auto-generated only -- never manually edited\n- Overwritten each iteration (not appended)\n- Non-blocking: if generation fails, log a warning and continue the loop\n- In **confirm mode**: displayed to the user at each iteration approval gate\n- In **auto mode**: written silently for post-hoc review\n\n#### Step 5: Loop Decision\n\n- If convergence check returns STOP: exit to synthesis\n- If STUCK_RECOVERY: apply review-specific recovery strategies (see Section 3a), reset stuck count, continue\n- Otherwise: increment iteration counter, go to Step 1\n\n### Stuck Recovery (Review-Specific)\n\n**Threshold:** 2 consecutive no-progress iterations (`noProgressThreshold = 0.05`).\n\n| Failure Mode | Detection | Recovery Strategy |\n|-------------|-----------|-------------------|\n| Same dimension stuck | Last 2 iterations reviewed the same dimension with ratios `< 0.05` | **Change granularity**: if reviewing at file level, zoom into functions, if reviewing functions, zoom out to module level |\n| Traceability plateau | Required protocols remain partial while ratios stay `< 0.05` | **Protocol-first replay**: re-run the unresolved traceability protocol directly against the conflicting artifacts |\n| Low-value advisory churn | Last 2 iterations found only P2 work | **Escalate severity review**: explicitly search for P0/P1 patterns or downgrade unsupported severity claims |\n\n**Selection logic**:\n\n```\nfunction selectReviewRecoveryStrategy(stuckIterations, state, config):\n  lastFocuses = [i.focus for i in stuckIterations[-2:]]\n\n  // Same dimension stuck? Change granularity\n  if len(set(lastFocuses)) <= 1:\n    return { strategy: \"change_granularity\", dimension: lastFocuses[0] }\n\n  // Required protocol plateau?\n  if hasRequiredProtocolPlateau(state.traceabilityChecks):\n    return { strategy: \"protocol_first_replay\" }\n\n  // Default: escalate severity\n  return { strategy: \"escalate_severity_review\" }\n```\n\n**Recovery evaluation**:\n- If recovery iteration finds any new P0/P1 or materially advances required traceability coverage: recovery successful. Reset stuck count. Continue.\n- If recovery iteration finds only P2 or nothing: recovery failed. If all dimensions are already covered, exit to synthesis. Otherwise, move to the next unreviewed dimension.\n\n---\n\n## 4. PHASE 3: SYNTHESIS\n\n### Purpose\n\nCompile all iteration findings into the final `{artifact_dir}/review-report.md`. The synthesis phase owns the canonical review report output.\n\n### Steps\n\n#### Step 1: Finding Registry Deduplication\n\nConsolidate findings across all iterations:\n\n1. Read all iteration files: `{artifact_dir}/iterations/iteration-*.md`\n2. Group findings by file + line range + root cause\n3. Merge duplicates, keeping the highest severity and all evidence\n4. Assign final finding IDs: `F001`, `F002`, ...\n5. Preserve the audit trail: note which iterations contributed to each merged finding\n\n#### Step 2: Severity Reconciliation\n\nUse adjudicated `finalSeverity` for any P0/P1 that was downgraded during claim adjudication (Step 4a of the iteration loop). The original severity from the iteration file is preserved in the audit appendix.\n\n#### Step 3: Replay Validation\n\nRecompute the convergence outcome from JSONL state before finalizing the report:\n\n1. Read `deep-review-state.jsonl` and select review-mode iteration records in run order\n2. Recompute `newFindingsRatio`, rolling-average votes, MAD votes, and coverage votes from stored JSONL fields only\n3. Recompute `traceabilityChecks.summary` and confirm required protocol statuses match the recorded coverage vote\n4. Re-run the evidence, scope, and coverage gates against stored findings and scope data\n5. Compare the replayed decision and stop reason to the recorded synthesis event\n\nThis synthesis-stage replay is a **full-history audit replay** over the stored JSONL state. It follows the same full-history replay discipline as the per-iteration reducer refresh above, but runs as an explicit pre-synthesis audit.\n\nReplay passes only when the recomputed decision, thresholds, and gate outcomes agree with the persisted result. If replay fails, log a warning in the audit appendix but proceed with synthesis.\n\n#### Step 4: Compile review-report.md\n\nGenerate the 9 core review-report sections (defined in `review-mode-contract.yaml` under `outputs.reviewReport`) and insert `## Resource Map Coverage Gate` between `Traceability Status` and `Deferred Items` when `config.resource_map_present == true`:\n\n| # | Section | Purpose |\n|---|---------|---------|\n| 1 | Executive Summary | Verdict, active P0/P1/P2 counts, scope, `hasAdvisories` metadata |\n| 2 | Planning Trigger | Why the verdict routes to planning or changelog follow-up |\n| 3 | Active Finding Registry | Deduplicated active findings with evidence and final severity |\n| 4 | Remediation Workstreams | Grouped action lanes derived from active findings |\n| 5 | Spec Seed | Minimal spec delta derived from review results |\n| 6 | Plan Seed | Action-ready plan starter for remediation |\n| 7 | Traceability Status | Core vs overlay protocol status and unresolved gaps |\n| 8 | Resource Map Coverage Gate | Present only when `config.resource_map_present == true`, reports touched entries, untouched entries (`expected-by-scope` vs `gap`), and implementation paths absent from the map |\n| 9 | Deferred Items | P2 advisories, blocked checks, and follow-up items |\n| 10 | Audit Appendix | Coverage, replay validation, convergence evidence |\n\n#### Step 5: Verdict Determination\n\nApply the verdict contract from `review-mode-contract.yaml`:\n\n| Verdict | Condition | Next Command |\n|---------|-----------|--------------|\n| **FAIL** | Active P0 remains OR any required binary gate fails | `/speckit:plan` for remediation |\n| **CONDITIONAL** | No active P0, but active P1 remains | `/speckit:plan` for fixes |\n| **PASS** | No active P0/P1 | `/create:changelog` |\n\nWhen `PASS` verdict is issued and active P2 findings remain, set `hasAdvisories = true` in the report metadata.\n\n#### Step 6: Finalize State\n\n1. Update config status: Set `status: \"complete\"` in `deep-review-config.json`\n2. Write final JSONL entry:\n   ```json\n   {\n     \"type\": \"event\",\n     \"event\": \"synthesis_complete\",\n     \"mode\": \"review\",\n     \"totalIterations\": N,\n     \"verdict\": \"PASS|CONDITIONAL|FAIL\",\n     \"activeP0\": N,\n     \"activeP1\": N,\n     \"activeP2\": N,\n     \"dimensionCoverage\": X,\n     \"stopReason\": \"...\",\n     \"timestamp\": \"...\"\n   }\n   ```\n\n---\n\n## 5. PHASE 4: SAVE\n\n### Purpose\n\nPreserve review context to the memory system for future session recovery and cross-session continuity.\n\n### Steps\n\n1. **Compose context payload**: Create a structured JSON payload with the review summary, active findings, verdict, next action, and relevant artifacts. Use `specFolder` for the target packet and include curated `observations`, `recent_context`, `toolCalls`, and `exchanges` when available.\n\n2. **Generate context**: Run the memory save script with structured JSON via `--stdin`, `--json`, or a session-scoped JSON file:\n   ```bash\n   node .skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/dist/continuity/generate-context.js --json '{\"specFolder\":\"{spec_folder}\",\"sessionSummary\":\"Deep review completed; see review/review-report.md for verdict and findings.\"}' {spec_folder}\n   ```\n\n3. **No extra indexing step in the live contract**: `generate-context.js` is the supported save boundary for this workflow.\n\n4. **Verify**: Confirm the routed canonical spec document carries the review continuity update. Expected targets are `implementation-summary.md`, `decision-record.md`, or `handover.md`; standalone `memory/*.md` files are retired and should not be created.\n\n### On Save Failure\n\nIf memory save fails:\n- Preserve the current `review/` packet as backup (the disk state is the ground truth)\n- Log the error\n- The review results remain intact in `{artifact_dir}/review-report.md` regardless of memory save status\n\n---\n\n## 6. AUTO-RESUME PROTOCOL\n\n### Purpose\n\nEnable review sessions to resume cleanly from prior state when interrupted by context compaction, process termination, or intentional pause.\n\n### Resume Detection\n\nIf state files already exist from a prior session:\n\n1. **Verify agreement**: Confirm config, JSONL, findings registry, and strategy all exist and agree on target/spec folder\n2. **Count progress**: Read JSONL, count iteration records, determine last completed iteration\n3. **Read strategy**: Load current dimension progress, findings, and next focus from `deep-review-strategy.md`\n4. **Set counter**: Set iteration counter to last completed + 1\n5. **Log resume**: Append the canonical resume event to `deep-review-state.jsonl`. The reducer requires every field below:\n   ```json\n   {\"type\":\"event\",\"event\":\"resumed\",\"mode\":\"review\",\"sessionId\":\"rvw-2026-03-24T10-00-00Z\",\"parentSessionId\":\"rvw-2026-03-24T10-00-00Z\",\"lineageMode\":\"resume\",\"continuedFromRun\":4,\"generation\":1,\"archivedPath\":null,\"timestamp\":\"2026-03-24T14:05:00Z\"}\n   ```\n6. **Continue**: Enter the iteration loop from step_read_state\n\n### Lifecycle Branches (current release)\n\nThe runtime supports three lineage modes today. `fork` and `completed-continue` were described in earlier drafts but have no workflow wiring in this release, so they MUST NOT be exposed to operators. If the long-form lineage feature is picked up later it will arrive with first-class event emission, reducer ancestry handling, and replay fixtures. Until then treat the contract below as canonical.\n\n| Mode | Session id | Generation | Archive | JSONL event | When to pick |\n|------|-----------|-----------|---------|-------------|--------------|\n| `new` | fresh | 1 | n/a | implicit (config record) | no existing state |\n| `resume` | same | same | none | `resumed` (see example above) | operator wants to continue the current lineage boundary |\n| `restart` | fresh | `prior + 1` | prior `review/` tree moved under `review-archive/{timestamp}/` | `restarted` (same field set plus non-null `archivedPath`) | operator wants to clear the workspace and replay with a new angle |\n\n**Contract for every persisted lifecycle event**:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"type\": \"event\",\n  \"event\": \"resumed | restarted\",\n  \"mode\": \"review\",\n  \"sessionId\": \"<session id of the new or continuing lineage>\",\n  \"parentSessionId\": \"<session id of the prior lineage (equals sessionId on resume)>\",\n  \"lineageMode\": \"resume | restart\",\n  \"generation\": <number>,\n  \"continuedFromRun\": <number or null>,\n  \"archivedPath\": \"<path or null>\",\n  \"timestamp\": \"<ISO 8601>\"\n}\n```\n\nEvery field in the contract MUST be present on every persisted lifecycle event. `archivedPath` is null for `resumed` and set to the archive destination for `restarted`. `continuedFromRun` is the number of completed iteration records before the lifecycle boundary. Reducer parity tests ensure the dashboard `Lifecycle` section reads exactly those fields.\n\n### State Validation on Resume\n\n| Condition | Classification | Action |\n|-----------|---------------|--------|\n| Config + JSONL + strategy all exist and agree | `resume` | Continue from last iteration + 1 |\n| All exist, config says `status: \"complete\"` | `completed-session` | Report completion, ask user if re-review desired |\n| Partial files or contradictions | `invalid-state` | Halt for repair, do not guess |\n| No files exist | `fresh` | Start new initialization |\n\n### State Recovery Cascade\n\nWhen state files are missing or corrupted, apply this priority cascade:\n\n| Priority | Condition | Recovery Action |\n|----------|-----------|----------------|\n| 1 | JSONL exists and parseable | Normal operation |\n| 2 | JSONL exists, strategy.md missing | Reconstruct strategy from JSONL: extract dimensions, findings, coverage |\n| 3 | JSONL missing, iteration files exist | Scan `review/iterations/iteration-*.md`, reconstruct JSONL with `status: \"reconstructed\"` |\n| 4 | Only config.json remains | Restart from initialization phase |\n| 5 | Config.json also missing | Cannot recover. Report error and halt. |\n\nEach priority level is attempted in order. If a level fails, fall through to the next.\n\n---\n\n## 7. PAUSE/RESUME\n\n### Pause Sentinel Behavior\n\nThe pause sentinel provides graceful loop suspension in autonomous mode without killing the process.\n\n### Creating a Pause\n\nCreate the sentinel file at any time:\n\n```bash\ntouch {artifact_dir}/.deep-review-pause\n```\n\nThe orchestrator checks for this file between iterations (Step 2a). When detected:\n- The current iteration is allowed to complete\n- The loop halts before dispatching the next iteration\n- A paused event is logged to JSONL\n- The user is notified with current state (iteration count, dimensions covered, findings)\n\n### Resuming After Pause\n\nDelete the sentinel file and restart the workflow:\n\n```bash\nrm {artifact_dir}/.deep-review-pause\n```\n\nOn the next invocation:\n- The auto-resume protocol (Section 6) detects the existing state\n- A resumed event is logged to JSONL\n- The loop continues from the next iteration\n\n### Confirm Mode Approval Gates\n\nIn confirm mode (`deep-review-confirm.yaml`), the workflow adds explicit approval gates instead of relying on the pause sentinel:\n\n| Gate Location | Behavior |\n|---------------|----------|\n| After initialization | Show config, strategy, and scope. Wait for approval |\n| After each iteration | Show iteration findings, dashboard, and convergence status. Options: Continue, Adjust Focus, Stop |\n| Before synthesis | Show summary of all iterations. Wait for approval to synthesize |\n| After synthesis | Show final review-report.md summary. Approve or request revisions |\n\n---\n\n## 8. STATE TRANSITIONS, ERROR HANDLING, AND QUALITY GATES\n\nState-transition rules, error handling, and the review quality gates live in `loop-state-and-gates.md` for navigability.\n\n---\n\n## 11. RELATED RESOURCES\n\n### Shared Protocol Documentation\n\nProtocol documents from `deep-research` are cross-referenced, not duplicated:\n\n| Document | Path | Purpose |\n|----------|------|---------|\n| Loop Protocol (research) | `../deep-research/references/protocol/loop-protocol.md` | Research loop lifecycle (this file adapts Section 9) |\n| State Format | `../deep-research/references/state/state-format.md` | JSONL, strategy, and config schemas |\n| Convergence | `../deep-research/references/convergence/convergence.md` | `shouldContinue_review()` (Section 10), stuck recovery, quality guards |\n\n### Review-Specific Resources\n\n| Resource | Path | Purpose |\n|----------|------|---------|\n| Review Contract | `assets/review-mode-contract.yaml` | Single source of truth for dimensions, verdicts, gates, protocols |\n| Quick Reference | `references/protocol/quick-reference.md` | One-page review cheat sheet |\n| Strategy Template | `assets/deep-review-strategy.md` | Template for review strategy files |\n| Config Template | `assets/deep-review-config.json` | Template for review config files |\n| Dashboard Template | `assets/deep-review-dashboard.md` | Template for auto-generated dashboard |\n\n### Workflow Files\n\n| Mode | Path |\n|------|------|\n| Auto (unattended) | `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-review-auto.yaml` |\n| Confirm (step-by-step) | `.skilled/commands/deep/assets/deep-review-confirm.yaml` |\n\n### Agent\n\n| Agent | Path Pattern | Constraint |\n|-------|-------------|------------|\n| `@deep-review` | `{runtime_dir}/deep-review.{ext}` | LEAF-only, no sub-agents, no WebFetch, target files read-only |\n\nRuntime paths:\n- OpenCode / Copilot: `.opencode/agents/deep-review.md`\n- Claude: `.claude/agents/deep-review.md`\n\n---\n\n## Executor Selection Contract\n\nExecutor settings are owned by the YAML workflow and rendered prompt pack. Do not bypass the workflow by hand-dispatching review iterations; each iteration stays LEAF-only and produces the required markdown plus JSONL delta.\n\n### Invariants\n\nEvery executor path MUST satisfy:\n\n1. Produce an iteration markdown file at `{state_paths.iteration_pattern}` (non-empty).\n2. Append a JSONL delta record to `{state_paths.state_log}` with required fields: `type`, `iteration`, `dimensions`, `filesReviewed`, `findingsSummary`, `findingsNew`, `findingDetails`, `newFindingsRatio`.\n3. Respect the LEAF-agent constraint: no sub-dispatch, no nested loops. Max 12 tool calls per iteration.\n\n### Failure Modes\n\n- Missing iteration file: `iteration_file_missing` from `post-dispatch-validate.ts`, emits a `schema_mismatch` conflict event.\n- Empty iteration file: `iteration_file_empty`, same downstream.\n- JSONL not appended: `jsonl_not_appended`.\n- JSONL missing required fields: `jsonl_missing_fields`.\n- JSONL malformed: `jsonl_parse_error`.\n- 3 consecutive failures: existing `stuck_recovery` event (unchanged).\n\n### JSONL Audit Field\n\nNon-native executor runs append an `executor` block with model and runtime settings to the iteration's JSONL record via `executor-audit.ts`. Native runs are recoverable from YAML alone.\n\n### Template And Config Surface\n\nThe executor-agnostic iteration prompt lives at `assets/prompt-pack-iteration.md.tmpl`, rendered by `prompt-pack.ts` before dispatch and either injected as the agent's context (native) or piped to `opencode run` stdin (cli-opencode). Config is in `assets/deep-review-config.json` under the `executor` key; schema in `runtime//lib/deep-loop/executor-config.ts`. CLI flag precedence: `--executor/--model/--reasoning-effort/--service-tier/--executor-timeout > config file > schema default`.\n\n### What Never Changes Regardless Of Executor Route\n\n- YAML owns state (`deep-review-state.jsonl`, strategy.md, registry, dashboard).\n- `reduce-state.cjs` is the single state writer.\n- Convergence detection, lifecycle events (new/resume/restart), the review dimensions pass, and stuck_recovery stay YAML-driven.\n- The `@deep-review` LEAF agent definition is untouched: it is the native executor, not the only one.\n\n### Code-Graph Readiness TrustState Surface\n\nOn this skill surface, the live code-graph readiness contract only reaches four TrustState values: `live`, `stale`, `absent`, and `unavailable`. The values `cached`, `imported`, `rebuilt`, and `rehomed` remain declared in the shared TrustState type for compatibility and downstream schema stability, but the seven code-graph handlers and readiness helpers used here do not emit them today.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":769,"totalLines":769,"truncated":false}},"title":".skilled/skills/system-deep-loop/deep-review/references/protocol/loop-protocol.md","time":{"start":1791271408770,"end":1791271408778}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0da10c8a60c2d01b016ac4a1f02c308191ad3d2240ab0fc39e"}},"id":"prt_11018947b001Z6JlnqQpidoASk","sessionID":"ses_eefeb8112ffeSAe808XV03S3PD","messageID":"msg_110163cb600118Njtnt2zX4pKt"}}
